The Czech Social Democrats in Prague know neither Voldemort, nor the "confectionery".
The new councillors of the capital from the ranks of the ČSSD will obviously have to overcome certain language barriers in order to be able to understand their old-new colleagues from the ODS in the Prague Council and in the city representation.
Aktuálně.cz newspaper once "knocked" members of the new council out of the ČSSD, as they master the notorious slang that has become naturalized in recent years, in which today's coalition partners were at the helm in Prague.
The encoded vocabulary, introduced by the Prague political elite in the erated era of Lord Mayor Pavel Bém, is borrowed from some of the best-known actors, situations and cases.
Surprisingly, it turned out that the new councillors are somewhat in the dark in relation to which these new terms.
At least that's what they're
"Who's Voldemort?"
"I don't know."
"I'm more of a neighbord in Prague politics," Lukáš Kaucký, the new councillor for culture, responded to the test of the "godparent vocabulary."
But councillor Karel Březina - a political veteran - also reacted in a similar way.
For example, the term “golf hole”.
“That’s the only expression of those you’re presenting to me here that I use every now and then.”
"Otherwise I don't think much of slang."
Out of the vocabulary published by Tageblatt Aktuálně.cz in this year's June before the ODS Electoral Congress, the editorial team picked out six terms, especially those notoriously associated with the Prague Magistrate.
The Social Democrats should define the terms "voldemort," "largest Prague golf hole," "going to pastry chores," "bully's nacks," "betrayals of the capital," and "tumbling."
Three Prague councillors agreed to the test, the fourth - Antonín Weinert, the future head of the Prague school system - initially promised his participation in the conversation, but then did not even take off the listener.
The fifth councillor, Petr Dolínek, rejected the test from the outset.
"I don't care about these things and I don't care how the bourgeois Democrats talk to each other, I don't judge."
"It's important to me how we understand each other as part of our programs," he countered.
Lukáš Kaucký allegedly would not have realized in the above-mentioned headline that Roman Janoušek is talking about the influential entrepreneur and friend of the former mayor Bém.
Kaucky says the term "Voldemort" purely nothing and the only term he received correctly during the test was "the largest Prague golf hole".
"That's the Blanka tunnel," says Kaucký, but added to safety: "But I rather know that from the newspaper."
And joking: "I'm probably going to have to take a language course."
The "golf hole" is then correctly deciphered not only by Matador Karel Březina, but also by the councillor for environmental issues Lukáš Plachý as Tunnel Blanka.
However, both Social Democrats also know for whom the term Voldemort stands and Lukáš Plachý even suspects where this figure can be found and thus also what it means "to go to the pastry."
"I believe, that is, to go to some Prague catering establishment," Plachý is pretty close to the mystery solution, if you know that this is Janoušek's headquarters.
In agreement with Březina, however, he claims to have drawn this knowledge from the newspaper rather that this vocabulary would be his own.
Judging by the test attendees, there actually does not seem to be a similarly naturalized slang among the Social Democrats.
"I've asked my colleagues too, but obviously there's really no such thing with us."
"We call ourselves by name, every now and then we also use abbreviations or nicknames," says the chairman of the Prague ČSSD Petr Hulínský - nicknamed "Hulda" by his party comrades.
"At school I was called Bříza (birch), but I don't know if my colleagues call me that," Karel Březina tells us his nickname.
The newspaper Deník Aktuálně.cz has tried out the name and look there - it works.
Councillor Weinert is friendly for the party comrades the "grandpa" or less friendly the "old one", the representative Miroslav Poche, who drew attention to himself before the elections through machinations in party sponsorship, is called "Igráček" (well-known Czech Lego figure) by some colleagues.
Nicknames are the only that the Social Democrats allow in intra-partisan parlance.
Hulínský supposedly doesn't like calling him "Hulda," but Deník Aktuálně.cz registered yet another nickname of the Prague chairman.
His nickname "Doctor of Rubber Club Rights" allegedly has to do with the fact that Petr Hulínský has acquired his title Doctor juris at the Bratislaver police academy.
The government wants to prevent the torture of "witches" and issued a brochure
Women who are labeled witches in Burkina Faso usually have some common social characteristics.
They're more likely to belong to the older generation, they don't find work - for example, for health reasons, they're single and don't have children.
If one of these women "becomes a witch," she becomes strictly isolated so she can't become dangerous to the rest of the village.
"Men are quite rarely affected by this fate."
"Even young and healthy women are spared, as they help with work and give birth to children," a nun describes the situation in the capital of the West African state Burkina Faso in a special home for more than one hundred women who have been ejected by their communities.
The local government has now decided to take action against this abnormal "social measure."
And for this reason issued a brochure that is intended to prevent "witch persecution".
But there's a catch.
"More than 70 percent of all adults in Burkina Faso can't read," the "derStandard" newspaper pointed to the dilemma of written enlightenment.
However, being accused of witchcraft is also commonplace in other African countries.
In Ghana, the neighbouring country of Burkina Faso, 27-year-old Ammy Hemmahová was tortured to death by three women and two men - dousing them with petroleum and simply set them on fire.
This incident was reported, among other things, by the server BBC.
The perpetrators claimed, however, that the matter had only been a meeting of unfortunate circumstances.
According to their version, oil is inflamed, which is used for ritual anointings.
That is, after the group tried to "expel an evil spirit" from the woman.
The isolation of "witches" supports the people here without regard to their education.
"Der Standard" quoted a 42-year-old entrepreneur from Burkina Faso: "Eaters of human souls are a danger in the past like today," he said.
While the government in Burkina Faso at least strives to defuse the witch hunt, the Gambian president has neatly involved the president himself last year.
"President Yahya Jammeh sparked a monstrous witch-hunt after the death of his aunt, as they allegedly played a part in their deaths and would now try to kill the president himself through black magic."
"To do this, he called Shamans from nearby Guinea to the aid, who, accompanied by police, soldiers and the president's bodyguard, violently kidnapped women and men indiscriminately arrested from Gambian villages and brought them to the president's farm in Kanilai," Amnesty International said in a statement.
On the farm, incarcerated people were forced to drink an absud of plants with hallucinogenic effects, but which also caused intestinal and kidney problems.
Two of the arrested people did not survive the stay on the farm.
It was only due to international pressure that the nearly a thousand prisoners were released.
A tenant of Bakala: He heaters in the oven - but still pays higher rent
An older reduced man is just leaving the client center of the company RPG Byty of the "coal baron" Zdeněk Bakala, the largest private owner of rental apartments in the Czech Republic.
Under the arm a folder with three new leases to three apartments: for his daughters and his daughter's apartments and his son, who is currently serving a prison sentence.
All three have just been raised the rent as part of the so-called "deregulation".
Instead of 3500 kronor monthly rent, the son is scheduled to raid 4100 crowns next year for a 2-bedroom apartment with a second-class kitchen.
"And there is not even running warm water in the apartment and you have to heat in a simple coal stove," he taps annoyed with his finger on the new lease of the son, for whose signing he has a power of attorney.
"But what - I've signed," he declares half upset and semi-resigned.
BytyOKD.cz, which described the new rental conditions as "incorrect and immoral," advises tenants not to sign the new contracts and to wait until December 7, on which it commemorates guidance on how to take action against such contracts.
But the man just waves off.
"One says this, the other says that," he makes a sinister face.
The Ostrava region thus becomes the great "deregulation laboratory".
After New Year, this deregulation is imminent for most rental apartments in the Czech Republic, whose tenants so far do not pay market-oriented rent.
Aktuálně.cz therefore decided to visit this "lab" in person.
The RPG waiting room in Havířov is one of four in the Ostrava region and already nine o'clock in the morning this Tuesday.
Right next to the door are the bodyguards of a security agency and with her a calling device.
"Rent 2011" is how a special touch panel on the touchscreen lights up.
Tapped briefly and the device spits out a number sheet.
As soon as we deciphered the number "157" and it beeps on the large display on the ceiling of the waiting room.
Half a second later it's your turn.
A little later, an elderly couple enters the waiting room to ask for the rentance, but the onslaught in the waiting room apparently doesn't apply to the new rent at all.
Most people are here to solve the usual problems - repair requests, insurance matters and the like.
From mid-November, in the Ostrava region, envelopes with the new leases will flutter into the mailboxes of most tenants of the RPG’s 44,000 apartments in the portfolio.
The RPG Byty proposes a rent increase of about 15 to 38 percent in the letters.
The special counter for the new rents in the client centre has been open since 18 November.
"The action runs a little over a week, that is, that's only the beginning," the ladies behind the switch respond after a moment of hesitation when the "client" turns out to be a journalist.
Deregulation and Bakala's former miner's apartments are an explosive issue.
Before the elections, the parliamentary vice-president Lubomír Zaorálek of the ČSSD from Ostrava had taken up this topic quite loudly, no wonder that today the newspapers are full of it.
"Twenty years I want a new floor from them."
"They couldn't even replace toilet goggles," the man mentioned at the outset makes himself air.
Until now, according to the employees, pensioners are mainly at the counter to convince themselves whether they really receive the promised rent reduction, which promises them RPG if they sign the special offer in time.
The other tenants who sign the new lease until 15 December should also benefit from a half-reduced February rent.
So far, "deregulatory switches" in Havířov, however, are anything but overloaded by demands.
"Of yesterday's 360 clients, only about 40 to 50 of the rents in 2011 were here," estimate the switch officials.
RPG offers individual rental agreements both on their websites as well as wall panels, where it seems necessary.
If, however, the rental parties do not agree on the new rent, it will be determined by an independent court.
"This should not be a threat, but is to be understood as information about the only possible and completely legitimate legal procedure," explains RPG on its websites.
"No one goes to court here," says one of RPG Byty's employees and a man on the street in front of the client center is at least partially right.
"I've got 13,000 (crowns) pension, of which I have 5,000 left for food, where should I get the money for a court dispute," he says.
There's when the employee becomes insecure.
"Let's see."
"Mr Zaorálek tells them via the media that they aren't supposed to sign and that's how people aren't waiting," she said.
Regulated rent expires in the largest part of the Czech Republic on New Year's 2011 and according to RPG Byty, the rent increase is an economic necessity.
"The existing amount of the rents is not even sufficient for the maintenance of the housing fund," argues Pavel Klimeš, the director of the housing portfolio of RPG Byty and - it is precisely the neglect of the housing fund criticized by the angry tenants is, according to the homeowners, the main reason for the rent increase.
While RPG was able to invest four billion in the apartments from the rents collected between 2005 and 2010, it is expected to be 5.2 billion in the four years from 2011 to 2014.
On the large-format LCD display on the ceiling opposite the upholstered armchairs in Havířov's waiting room, a few red-luminous information appears in between.
"The rent increases in 2011 by 15% and less affect 31 200 apartments, i.e. more than 90% of RPG's apartments."
"Always this means a maximum of 7.50 CZK per square meter per month," can be read on the LCD monitor.
Only with 400 apartments from RPG's portfolio, the rent increases by 38 percent.
"In these cases, it's about the increase of extremely low rental prices of 19.66 CZK per square meter," RPG argues.
With 2600 apartments, the rent will remain at the same level next year.
RPG Byty promises that deregulation will not skyrocket rents, but take place gradually within four and eight years.
What's more - the company imposes another commitment: all revenue from the rent increase (see chart) wants to put the company back into the apartments, especially in heat savings - into new windows and into thermal insulation.
But - as a precaution, these are intended to benefit those people who sign the agreements on the rent increase.
The architects saw - the office also includes a kindergarten
Avriopoht office building in Brno is the new headquarters of Arch.Design.
But who would expect a mini kindergarten here, with everything that goes with it: colorful walls, creative furniture, a lot of toys and even a mini playground on the outdoor terrace.
This kindergarten has been available since last year to the employees of Arch.Design.
The design and architectural office Arch.Design, from whose studios come a number of well-known Brno buildings, reckoned from the first designs for the new Avriopoint building with the premises for his company mini kindergarten.
In wise foresight, because most of his 90 employees (insides) are baby boom-aged and the company is concerned that the architects return to their workplace as soon as possible.
Together with the possibility to work shortened, the kindergarten is another bonus that Arch.Design has to offer its employees in the concept of their social program.
The civil engineers and architects have made it easier to reconcile their demanding profession and child care.
The Minikindergarten is an original idea of the employees (inside) of Arch.Design and with the support of the management they managed to attract funding from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs for their project.
The in-company mini-kindergarten is financed by the human resources and employment policy operations programme from the European Social Fund and from the state budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to this financing (grants amounting to CZK 2, 8 million), the necessary facilities and equipment could be procured and for a period of 2 years, qualified personnel and operations are provided.
After the use of the funding (2 years), the company Arch.Design is obliged to continue to operate the Minikindergarten for 3 more years at its own expense.
The preparation of this project took for a whole year, with Economy Rating providing active assistance in the processing of applications.
The children are taken care of fully qualified staff - a nurse and a kindergarten teacher, the nursery can visit children without age restrictions, although most of them are over three years old.
And since this is about a mini kindergarten, only 5 children are allowed to be present at the same time, but several children alternate each other during the day.
The nursery is flexibly adapted to the needs of the parents, the children of all employees have the same rights with regard to the use of the kindergarten - without regard to the company hierarchy and their respective position.
Although it is individual education and nursing, the program content in the mini-kindergarten is nevertheless comparable to the supporting program of pre-school education.
The children not only play here, but above all excellent lessons are offered - from basic findings to pre-school lessons, they also learn English.
The classroom is equipped not only with toys and creative games, but also with a computer available to the children.
What more - the presence of young children in the company contributes to a friendly and positive company milieu.
Parents can take a look at the children at any time and the stress is dispensed with the delayed pick-up of the children from a distant kindergarten.
Parents therefore use their working hours more productively.
No wonder that natural and loyal employer-employee relationships also emerge here.
Arch.Design was thus among the Czech companies, which offer clear benefits in the recruitment of new employees, which are family-friendly and strengthen their personnel marketing through such programs.
A bone house fascinates even after 100 years. Look at the interiors of Gaudi
The roof seems like the back of a prehistoric lizard, the stairs like the spine of a dinosaur and the balconies are ripped-off fishmouths.
This is Casa Batlló of the Catalan architect Antonio Gaudi in Barcelona.
From the local inhabitants, the house with its organic forms got the nickname "bone house".
Organic shapes, wild colors, the unusual use of stone, tille shards and mosaics, the façades reminiscent of scales and ubiquitous soft lines, all these are typical features of Antonio Gaudi’s Art Nouveau style.
The façade of the house plays with dreamlike lines, tender colors and textures reminiscent of nature and living beings.
It is only on closer inspection that one notices that everything is mastered by geometry, rhythm and well-thought-out order.
At night, the whole façade looks even more plastic, more dramatic and the special illumination enhances the shine of the colors.
The same sweeping lines and intertwined shapes also dominate the interior of the house.
Wave-shaped wood copies the window frames, railings, ornamental elements of doors, even peepholes and ledges.
House Batlló in the Eixample district on the bustling shopping street Passeig de Grácia No. 43 was built by the reconstruction of a house from 1877, carried out by architect Emilio Sala Cortese.
The reconstruction took two years and was completed in 1906.
The investor, the wealthy industrialist Josep Batlló, originally insisted on the demolition of the old and the construction of a completely new building, but in the end he agreed to Gaudí’s idea on an unusual and also cheaper conversion.
However, Antoni Gaudí did not design the Casa Batlló tenement house alone.
He collaborated with architect Josep Maria Jujol.
It was precisely on its drawing board that the decorations for the representative apartment of the owner of the realist and the use of mosaics on the façade was also his idea.
However, numerous other sculptors and artists also collaborated on the design of the façade.
The joint work on the designs for Casa Batlló was the beginning of a fruitful collaboration between Jujola and Gaudí.
Jujol also participated in the construction of Gaudí's Casa Milá house in Park Güell, which also houses other works by Antoni Gaudí.
Shortly after its completion, the house was nicknamed "bone house" by the local inhabitants of its organic forms.
Others, in turn, called it the "yawning house" because of the special window opening mechanism.
It is also said that at the top of the building is the sword of St. George, the patron saint of Catalonia.
But other figurative elements also symbolize hidden religious motifs.
Gaudí designed for the house a unique central heating system at that time with venting channels and typical fireplaces.
Somewhat bizarre, her 45-degree inclination already seems.
In his work, Gaudí consistently ignored all building regulations at that time and was by no means deterred by them.
Prior to the start of construction, he obtained from the city the corresponding building permit, but the information he provided about the planned reconstruction was so vague that the city fathers were so appalled by the result two years later that they demanded a remedy.
Among other things, the councillors also objected to the fact that the construction with its pillars even intervened in the public sidewalk.
However, there were no more changes, on the contrary - only fifteen days after the relevant request of the district, Josep Batlló submitted an application to authorize the rental of the two highest floors of the house - and it was promptly granted to him.
The homeowner himself moved into the lower floors.
In the gallery on the 2nd floor there was a salon, on the third floor there were the bedrooms and private rooms of the apartment.
The ground floor was leased to a jewelry store.
The entire six-storey building walks through a staircase all the way up to the roof terrace.
Due to the basic elements of the conversion, all right angles and straight lines were eliminated and replaced by curved and curved walls.
Gaudí also worked with a modern concept for the mutual connection of spaces.
The social part can be completely connected by the opening of the salon doors and so a larger society could comfortably chat without having to forego a certain intimacy during more confidential conversations.
In addition to a new façade, Gaudí’s conversion also gave Gaudí a rather unusual roof and two other floors.
Gaudí’s play with the light as well as the blue and white glazed tiles, with which the courtyard is clad and the atriums, is also impressive.
The play of these two colors gives all floors of the house the highest level of light.
In doing so, he simply took advantage of the principle that white reflects the light better than dark colors, which, on the contrary, absorb sunlight.
Casa Batlló, which was added to Unesco's World Heritage List in 2005, can visit anyone today.
On the first floor, the most spacious and magnificent apartment is opened to the public.
The homeowners decided only in the year to take this step on the occasion of the 150th birthday of the famous creator.
When it was determined that this is quite a profitable business, one maintained the visits, indeed they were also extended to other common and the attic rooms, including the roof terrace.
However, the price to visit the monument is not a cardboard stick, it amounts to 16.50 euros, students, pensioners, groups and holders of tourist passes get 20 percent discount.
This amount exceeds the entrance fees to other Gaudí buildings in the city.
If you want to get to know the real atmosphere of the house with its dragon towers, you should try to make friends with one of the residents and visit him at home.
The right mattress and the right bed rust? Women have different desires than men
Not the greatest bed frame, but the quality of the reclining surface - so mattress and rust make up the good bed.
However, these must complement each other harmoniously in order to meet the wishes of the specific user.
Many things play a role in the selection - the weight, the size, the state of health and, last but not least, the gender of the sleeper.
Just as important is the interior, in which mattress and rust must be subordinated.
Typical example?
In a weekend house where it is not heated regularly, latex mattresses are not well suited, as they take much longer to "get warm."
In addition, they are significantly heavier than usual polyurethane mattresses.
And - they should be turned regularly.
If they do not have enough venting channels in the core and are also lying on a solid plate, moisture accumulates: It is therefore not excluded that it forms under their mold.
Of course, it also plays a role in what you’re used to.
Those who have been abroad for a long time, where very high mattresses with a soft layer are common, will find it difficult to get used to a low mattress with a hard surface.
"Probably all the hockey players who have come back from the NHL have complained to me that they can't sleep here properly anymore."
"For their shackled shoulders, heels and hips, they simply need a soft layer of visco foam (Memory Foam - a shape memory polymer)."
"I also recommend these, among other things, toxic patients who suffer from a disorder of the nervous system, so that they can poorly receive the "information" from the joints and skin," explains Sylva Hadamcziková, who specializes in upholstered furniture.
That’s whhere I got many hockey players years ago, but also other customers, these mattresses (Memory Foam).
"Today this is no longer a problem, because such mattresses are offered, among others, the manufacturer Tempur, the Italian company Magniflex or the Czech manufacturer Tropico."
And you don't necessarily have to be a famous hockey player to appreciate the benefits of visco foam.
This "miracle" actually works quite simply.
The foam optimizes its hardness and shape according to the absorbed heat, in other words, it nestles on the warm body.
Nowhere does it press, at night you don't roll around like after a heavy fight.
You're just sleeping a lot better.
The viscoelastic foam also offers protection against decubitus (wound fire).
With the choice of mattress size, you should still add the length of the soles of the feet to the body size, i.e. about 21 to 34 cm.
Man and woman, who are about 180 cm tall, need such a 210 to 220 cm long bed.
Each of the sleepers should be at least 90 cm resting width available, i.e. 180 cm together.
For single beds, e.g. for singles, 120 to 140 cm are attached.
For the sake of comfort and also for eventual visitors.
Otherwise, the following rule of thumb applies to the ideal bed width: "If you lie on your back, there should still be enough space for the slightly angled arms," advises Ing. Helena Prokopová, the guildmaster of the upholsterers and interior decorators, who has devoted herself for years to the problem of healthy sleep and ergonomics of furniture and also presents this topic at technical schools.
With a double bed, everyone needs their own rust and their own mattress, but be careful when shopping, so that the mattresses are the same.
Otherwise, there is nothing left but to mount the grates at different heights and this is far from possible with every bed frame.
Foam mattresses should be at least 14 cm high, mattresses with feather skeletons 15 cm, of which at least three centimeters should be accounted for on the mold layer and the fine upholstery.
"By hand or when sitting down, you must not feel the feathers under any circumstances," warns Helena Prokopová.
Each mattress requires a special type of grate.
Whole-padded beds with mattress and underlayer such as assembled Siam twins have nothing to look for in the bedroom.
These mattresses cannot be ventilated and you cannot wash the cover, which is already commonplace today.
And since you lose between 0.2 to 0.75 l of sweat in a single night, it is clear that the mattress slowly becomes a huge (and clogged sieve) after five to seven years.
This is also why experts reject advertising slogans that promise a service life of up to 25 years for mattresses, even a lifetime guarantee.
Every thing has its ifs and buts.
And that’s why a practical advice from the dealers: For mattresses 120 or 140 cm in width, you shouldn’t shy away from the hygiene of the washable, removable covers, but it’s advisable to use a mattress protector, which you can easily remove and wash.
Even though mattress covers are usually made today with an all-round zipper, so you get two halves (immensely important for the washing process and the washing machine size!), the manipulation with larger mattresses, especially executed alone, often seems like the unequal battle between David and Goliath.
Do they have a solid plate in bed instead of a rust?
Get out with it!
Or at most for a bed for unloved guests and for a single night.
Even so, they should use the plate with ventilation holes (unless there are) and mattresses with spring skeleton and 15 cm minimum height with so-called pocket spring core and fine padding.
Polyurethane mattresses do not belong on such a base.
For non-adjustable slatted frames with 14 to 18 spring strips (slats), latex or sandwich mattresses (possibly also combinations of latex and coconut), rusts with a larger number of slats, i.e. 28 and more spring strips, with lumbar support in the lumbar area and the possibility to regulate the slat hardness.
There are no mattresses with feather skeleton on adjustable slat gratings, as they do not respond to the ergonomic adjustment.
In addition to adjusting the stiffness of the spring strips, special flexible shoulder modules are also offered.
This concerns a special attachment of the spring strips, which makes it possible to push in the spring strips or even to angle them in such a way that they enable the shoulder of the sleeper to sink selectively.
Especially for men with broad shoulders an important thing.
There are even mattress manufacturers who have started to use soft zones for the shoulder area in "men's mattresses".
It is also intended for the ladies - they are granted these soft zones in the hip area.
For the most demanding customers, point-elastic wing spring systems (Lattoflex) or grates with point-elastic contact pads with so-called multi-torsion suspension are intended.
The pressure is distributed over several points, which reduces the voltage.
But - if you use a bad (too big and stuffy) pillow for the right mattress and proper rust, most of the benefits are lost again.
Again, a rule of thumb: the pillow is supposed to support the head, its tips should only reach up to the shoulders, so that the shoulder muscles can relax.
Therefore, a 50 x 60 cm cushion seems ideal, as the Scandinavians, for example, like it.
In the sided position, the pillow (together with the shoulder relief zone in the rust or the soft zone in the mattress) should compensate for the shoulder height.
Our modern mattresses have something in common with the former (filled with hay) straw bags.
Modern manufacturers are increasingly using natural materials, or at least their admixtures.
For mattresses, too, the new magic word is "organic".
Instead of petroleum raw materials, for the production of polyurethane foams, more and more and more natural oils are being used - for example castor and soybean oil.
Soy is probably used by all well-known manufacturers.
"Bio in the case of mattresses means that their core consists of materials of purely natural origin," explains Petr Vašíček from Tropico Plus.
However, natural substances are also used in the production of mattress covers - one of the first was aloe vera.
But you can also treat yourself to microcapsules that spread fresh forest or lavender scent when sleeping.
Here, too, you make a difference between man and woman.
While a "cooling" plant extract of birch leaves is used in men so that the mattress cover can absorb the heat and cool the body, the references for women have "warming" effects.
Extracts of red paprika pods stimulate the blood circulation, so the weaker sex gets pleasantly warm when sleeping.
What about today's favorites?
Mattresses made of cold foam (it has larger pores with destroyed cell structure and is therefore more breathable than standard polyurethane and also much more flexible).
By combining the different stiffness (hardness) of cold foams and visco foams, multi-zone mattresses are created, which know exactly where the head, hip and heels are located.
For a high-quality mattress, however, you have 7000 crowns and more.
However, health mattresses are usually burdened with lower VAT, as they are considered to be medical aids.
Gumotex was the first Czech company to receive this advantage.
You can see to save on the mattress and on the rust is not so easy.
From time to time there are special offers from manufacturers and distributors that offer two mattresses for the price of one.
For example, the Magniflex brand offers the Duofreeze Mattress with upholstered visco-foam memory for 11,990 CZK.
You get the second for free.
It was the material Tempure that ushered in the era of viscoelastic foams with open cell structure and high density at almost cosmic tempo.
No wonder, they were developed for NASA, because the astronauts are exposed to enormous overload during the launches.
Tempour becomes soft with heat, in a cool milieu it solidifies again.
So it adapts perfectly to the human body and distributes body pressure in an optimal way.
It is therefore not only ideal for preventing pressure firing, but generally increases sleeping comfort.
Viscoelastic foams are used not only for mattresses, but also for other health products, such as special foot cushions, which are intended to provide relief to the legs and vessels.
Local manufacturers, for example, use the Foams Eucatherm C, Twinsaver or similar products.
More and more parents want people monitoring devices to have the kids under control
The fear for one’s own children is growing and so more and more parents are considering monitoring them via GPS.
The system, which was originally invented to search for cars, can just as well locate people, which would also be helpful in such cases, as in the recent disappearance of nine-year-old Anička Janatková.
The device is currently about the size of a cigarette box, but soon it should not be bigger than a wristwatch.
Many people have frightened the fall of the vanished Anička and so they look for means and ways to better protect their children.
Also for the price of an impairment of their privacy
"We strive to tell everyone that this is by no measure to spy, but an effective means of reducing the likelihood that tragedy will occur," says Petr Pilin of Macro Weil, one of the suppliers of GPS tracking devices on the Czech market.
"I admit that there may be misuse of such devices in individual cases, for example if you put it in someone's pocket without him knowing about it, but we haven't had such a case so far."
"Most customers really buy it for security reasons," Pilin adds.
In his opinion, however, the child should always know what purpose the "box" serves and how to use it in an emergency.
However, child psychologist Dana Bočková points out that if a child doesn’t like wearing such a thing, he’s just getting rid of it.
"Even very young children show great creativity when it comes to losing certain things intentionally if they don't want to be controlled."
"Already the mobile phones aroused great expectations from the parents at first, but they are just as easy to switch off as these new monitoring devices," says Ms Bočková.
In their opinion, it is also controversial whether such a facility actually helps in the event of an attack.
"In the case of planned abductions, the kidnapper practically always checks whether the child has any electronics with him that it could be located with."
"Today it's the phone."
"If sucharging devices found wider use, they'd also be looking for them, as their sale would surely be accompanied by massive advertising."
"In these cases, the criminals are always one step ahead of the police," says the psychologist.
However, she concedes that similar devices can be quite helpful in a sudden attack by a paedophile.
According to Roberta Čech from the company RC Monitoring, which offers location services for both automobiles and people, the existence of such a device can help even if it is destroyed immediately after the attack.
"The device can be adjusted in such a way that it reports the respective position at certain intervals."
"Even if it were destroyed immediately, at least the last position of the affected person is available."
"In the case of more expensive devices, even an inclinometer is integrated, i.e
In his opinion, similar services could also do intelligent mobile phones with navigation, you only have to install special software.
The problem of the current locating devices is that it is either concerned with equipment in matchbox size, which resemble pendants or smaller mobile phones.
There's always the danger that you'll lose it.
According to Mr. Pilin from Macro Weil, therefore, devices that look like normal digital clocks are being tested in the present.
"This has the advantage that the possible attacker does not have to recognize at all that it is a monitoring or locating device.
"Or he doesn't notice that until after a certain amount of time, which gives the police a time lead," he adds.
No matter in which direction the development goes - towards wristbands or trailers, the same technology is always used as in vehicle tracking.
So about the combination of tracking function using the GPS system (just as with the navigation in the car) and the function of a mobile phone that reports the current position.
The army has so many integrations that you could wrap around the whole of the Czech Republic but you only have two years
The Czech army has purchased 445 kilometers of ignition cords, detonated with explosive devices.
In the previous year, the Ministry of Defense had already delivered 224 kilometers of ignition cord to the army camps.
It's reassuring to know that the armed forces have lasted for 225 years.
The problem is only the durability of the ignition cords - two, but at most four years.
The army bought the ignition cords from STV Group, a. s. for lappy 40 million kronors.
In its justification for the purchase of this enormous amount of ignition cord, the general staff MF DNES replied that the soldiers would consume about 60 kilometers of ignition cord annually.
According to the research of army pyrotechnicians, whom MF DNES referred to, it is clear from this indication that the whole army would have to undergo extremely intensive pyrotechic training every year - from all generals and officers of the army squad, to the secretaries and cleaning women.
Defense Minister Alexandr Vondra and army general Vlastimil Picek not excluded.
Only in this way could the considerable annual consumption of sixty kilometres of ignition cords be reasonably justified.
By the way, this length corresponds to 25,000 detonations.
But even so, these army stockpiles would be enough for more than ten years.
However, if the old rule of experts still applies that two meters of ignition line are completely sufficient for the training of each new professional soldier, then the army has created a reassuring supply for the 225 years already mentioned by these new purchases - also from the point of view of the increased demand of pioneer and special forces.
Soldiers of the rapid deployment brigade recently complained to Defense Minister Vondra that they had almost no more cartridges for handguns, so that shooting drills are severely restricted because of the lack of funds.
It's not to mention the lack of underpants.
"I don't agree why they're buoying tons of flare-ups from us either."
But that’s ultimately their decision, they’ll already know what they’re needing them.”
"For us, the army is a good customer that we would like to respond to to the greatest possible extent as a dealer," says the sales manager of STV Group Jan Děcký.
Jan Pejšek, the spokesman for the Ministry of Defense, justifies the purchase of hundreds of kilometers of ignition line by the fact that the army would have covered itself with this "ammunition" for at least ten years.
Jan Děcký from the STV Group, however, only speaks of a four-year service life of the ignition cords and police and army pyrotechnists of only two years.
"With perfect storage, probably also the four mentioned years are possible," admits one of them, who, however, wants to remain unnamed in order not to be accused of criticism of the army as a soldier.
At the enduction of the service life, all ammunition must be thoroughly tested, whether it is still reliable and does not endanger the lives of the soldiers.
However, these tests are not in vain, but cost further money.
Another argument of the Ministry of Defense is the volume discount for the 445 kilometre ignition cord.
"They cost us sixty percent less than last year's delivery of 244 kilometers," Pejšek, a spokesperson, tells us the point of view of purchasers of the Ministry of Defense.
Only for comparison: The annual consumption of ignition cords of pyrotechnics of the police, which liquidate ammunition from the Second World War or the Soviet brother army, hardly exceeds 1,500 meters for several years.
And the police always only orders such quantities of this ammunition, which it can consume before the end of the service life.
Due to the safety of the pyrotechnicians, in addition, the possibility of flexible purchases of more modern and thus also reliable ignition cords is kept open.
The Ministry of Defence has enormous problems with the liquidation of tens of thousands of tons of ammunition from the time of the Warsaw Treaty, when the two-hundred-thousanded Czechoslovak People's Army was preparing for its campaign to the Rhine.
According to the pyrotechnicians, whom MF DNES addressed, the Czech armed forces expect in a few years the same problem in the liquidation of hundreds of kilometers of old fuses, which it has so "cheaply" purchased today.
To this day, I still have the contacts that I have won thanks to the competition
Lukáš Pytloun has helped Lukáš Pytloun to sell the franchise of his hotel.
Lukáš Pytloun from Liberec became the winner of the "Small Entrepreneur of the Year" competition.
The story of his success seems like a film scenario.
Lukáš Pytloun liked to travel.
It was only abroad that he became aware that there is a lack of good accommodation options at reasonable prices in Liberec.
From the savings from his holiday work abroad and with the help of a bank loan, he bought a dilapidated house with five apartments.
Bearing in mind that he was a university student in the sixth semester at that time, this was certainly a courageous undertaking.
"The price-to-quality ratio at that time annoyed me and so I tried to do something about it," says Lukáš Pytloun.
He rented the first reconstructed apartment to foreign workers.
He immediately reinvested all the profit back into the house, which he then rebuilt step-by-step to the pension with 54 beds.
Since then, in just seven years of entrepreneurial activity in Liberec, he has managed to open four guesthouses, two stylish restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of more than 300 beds of all accommodation categories.
The most comfortable of them until recently was the four-star hotel "Pytloun Design Hotel".
This year, another first-class hotel was added.
"We've just signed a contract with a hotel in Ostrov u Tisé, to which we've sold our franchise concept and other interested parties in the franchise of Pytloun hotels have already come forward," says last year's number one entrepreneur.
"Big share of this license sale to operate hotels just had the victory in the "Entrepreneur of the Year" competition," adds Mr. Pytloun appreciatively.
The victory has earned him greater notoriety, even popularity in Liberec and the surrounding area.
"The people recognized me on the street, often congratulations to me and I have to say that did really well," he recalls.
I really recommend everyone who wants to create something to participate in this competition.
You don’t have to win right away, every visualization is good.
He says that to this day he benefits from the contacts and acquaintances that would not otherwise have happened at all.
"With some people I've met thanks to the competition, I've had regular business relationships to this day."
In his opinion, the fame associated with success is also somewhat reflected in each of its employees.
And that's where he's got seventy.
"I notice with them greater loyalty and pride that they are part of something to which general acknowledgement."
I don’t just see this as a personal success, no, that’s the result of the good work of all of us.”
"My name has only come to the foreground," says Lukáš Pytloun modestly.
I wish my successors a quieter year when we had it in Farmet
Victory euphoria was quickly replaced by the consequences of the crisis.
Thanks to its determination, Farmet has the worst behind it.
Karel Žďárský, the company owner of Farmet, accepted the award for the first race of the Vodafone company of the year competition last year.
At that time, the jury paid tribute to the efforts with which he stomped from the small tooling in Česká Skalice a company that generates hundreds of millions in sales.
"I can say that this appraisal has encouraged not only myself, but also all of my employees and strengthened our self-confidence in the struggle with the competition," says the previous year's winner.
At the beginning of Farmet’s company history was the effort to do something useful.
And when the state offered his father Karel Žďárský as compensation for the property seized by the communists a small workshop for lease, he did not hesitate for long, leaving his career in the company in which he was employed until then and founded with a few comrades of his father his own company for the production of plastic tubs.
A short time later, they began to focus on vegetable oil presses.
Within 18 years, they have risen to become the largest manufacturer of automatic presses throughout Central Europe.
But it is only through the success in the aforementioned competition and its medialization that it has also entered into the consciousness of the non-professional public.
"Not only that our customers now perceive the Farmet brand much more intensively, our foreign partners also began to be able to work with a company that has achieved the greatest possible success in its country," says Karel ŽĽďárský.
The past year, however, has not only been marked by victory euphoria at Farmet.
The appraisal was based on the company’s economic results in 2008 and part of 2009.
And just 2009 was the hardest year for the sale of tool-making products.
The crisis, which at that time shaken even less the citizens, but above all companies, just reached its peak - many companies were forced to cease their investment projects and restrict the purchase of new facilities and technologies.
"Of course we couldn't escape this situation either."
"Sales declined by 40 percent, we had to restrict production and even resort to redundancies," explains Karel Žďárský
A viable company sometimes has to take such unpopular measures.
However, it was possible to get to grips with the situation and to keep the company in the black numbers, even if with lower gains than before the crisis.
For one, the company was unwilling to make smears - on investment in research and development.
"From the grant, we built a research and training center, strengthened our presentation at international trade fairs and earned main prizes at domestic exhibitions," says Žďárský.
Everything testifies that we've overcome the crisis.
Sales are growing again and with them the number of new employees.
"I wish the new winner a lot of joy in the victory - but in a quieter atmosphere," says Karel Žďárský.
An opinion poll that cleans up with nonsensical regulations
The opinion poll about the "absurdity of the year" is part of the entrepreneurial competition for the fourth time.
Some nonsensical regulations have already managed to come out of the world.
The dubious winner of the next-yeard of the "Absurdity of the Year" opinion poll advanced the obligation to create documents on authorities that are available online.
More than a third of not quite four thousand voting entrepreneurs decided.
Thus, the social security administration demanded a paper extract from the commercial register, even if the official could have retrieved it by two mouse clicks.
Thanks to the opinion poll, this is now changing.
"In the future, the Czech Sociation Administration will no longer require excerpts from the commercial register during checks of social security payers," ČSSZ spokeswoman Jana Buraňová said.
The meaning and purpose of the "absurdity of the year" opinion poll is to examine all regulations and orders that entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic have to fulfill and repeal the nonsensical.
For the fourth time this year, it is part of an entrepreneurial competition, which is organized by the tsch. Wirtschaftszeitung (HN) and enjoys growing popularity among the public.
If the three thousand people took part in the vote last year, there have already been four thousand this year.
No wonder, because there are still a lot of legal obligations that serve solely to impede corporate activity.
"As far as the number of income tax forms is concerned, for example, we occupy a front rank in the world," notes Karel Havlíček of the association of small and medium-sized enterprises and traders of the Czech Republic.
Simplification of entrepreneurial activity in recent years has been the proclaimed objective of all governments.
Compared to the world, however, the situation in the Czech Republic has hardly improved.
According to data from the World Bank, the Czech Republic ranks 74th in terms of corporate conditions, an eight-seater-seat ranking.
The Absurdity of the Year competition was proclaimed for the first time a year and takes place every year according to the same scenario.
Anyone can report administrative and bureaucratic nonsense from April until the editorial deadline of the competition at the end of September.
The web address www.firmaroku.cz is used for registration, after which the organizer of the competition selects seven candidates for the "anti-award" and lets the public vote via the Internet on the websites www.firmaroku.cz/absurdita and www.ihned.cz.
It wins the absurdity that gets the most votes.
More than a hundred, more specifically 105 pointless regulations and orders have already been registered for this year's opinion poll.
The tips come not only from entrepreneurs, but also from professional associations and journalists.
Among the nominated "finalists" of the competition are, for example, regulations that maintenance vehicles for highways and expressways have to pay tolls or that entrepreneurs with twenty employees, two offices and two toilets have to prepare every two years.
The listing of this year's "Seven" has been published on the pages of the competition.
The proclamation of the competition at the beginning of November on Prague’s Wenceslas Square is part of happening, which is also regularly attended by personalities of political and public life.
This year, Eva Svobodová, the director of the association of small or medium-sized companies and tradesmen of the Czech Republic as well as Petr Kužel, the President of the Chamber of Commerce of the Czech Republic at the culmination of the competition.
"Where the state throws sticks between the entrepreneurs, there can be no real economic upswing, that's why my goal is to repeal at least thirty paragraphs every year, which today still unnecessarily complicate their ventures to entrepreneurs," Martin Kocourek, the minister for industry and commerce, said in a statement.
Efforts to remove nonsensical orders from the world are taking effect.
In 2007, people most disturbed the employer-imposed duty to report to the trade office three days in advantage in advantishement at the place of their permanent establishment the intention to drive with an exhibition stand to an exhibition or funfair.
This obligation has been revoked by an amendment to the trade law.
The obligation, which received the second-largest number of votes in 2007 - the obligation to report to the employment office business trips of employees up to 30 days - was abolished by the law on them so-called "green card".
And - of all good things are three:
At the absurdity of 2009, the ordinance was chosen, which imposed on physiotherapists to have a mirror, a refrigerator and two personal scales in the consultation room.
This ordinance has been softened somewhat by the Department of Health this year - the refrigerator requirement fell away.
A new category pays tribute to socially responsible companies
Evaluation criteria are humanitarian, environmental, educational or sports projects
A novelty in this year's fifth year of competition of the business newspaper (HN) is the category "Responsible company of the year 2010".
The basic idea of this competition is to promote sensible and fair ventures.
"We would like to support such companies that are aware of the consequences of their entrepreneurial activities on the environment and support socially responsible initiatives," says Patrik Bartholdy, the regional manager of the mobile phone company Vodafon, which is the exclamator and guarantor of this competition, as well as the "Rebel of the Year" competition.
For this year's first vintage of this special subcategory of Vodafon Company of the year's main competition, companies registered, for which success is not the only long-term goal.
The story of each of them is extremely inspirative and provides enough material for an independent article.
Although they differ diametrically in relation to the object of their activity, their efforts nevertheless have a common denominator - support and consideration.
This is, for example, about handicapped people, because about personal problems or the health of one's own employees.
Among the fourteen finalists from all over the Czech Republic, for example, the Prague-based software company Et Netera appeared, which promotes the creation of non-formal sports teams among their employees, who then take part in numerous tournaments in various sports.
Another example of a responsible company from the Pardubice region is Isolit-Bravo, which has been the main sponsor of a foundation fund for handicapped children for seventeen years.
A paper mill from Poděbrady also pays exemplary attention to its health-disabled employees, who make up 70 percent of their employees, for which it was honored with the title "Responsible Company of the Central Bohemian Region"; in the Vodafone Company of the Year competition, it ranked third in the context of this region.
In the Olomouc region, the accounting firm MiRyz Soft prevailed, which, among other things, introduced working hours, which were reduced to an average of 35 hours per week.
At the presentation of the title, the jury also honored the circumstance that the company mainly buys from Czech manufacturers, supports citizens’ initiatives in its surroundings and works according to environmentally friendly principles.
"We strive to recycle as much as possible in order to minimize the consequences of our entrepreneurial activity and administration for the environment," explains her managing director Miluše Ryznarová.
The responsible IT company Enlogit from the Ústí nad Labem region, in turn, is interested in the health of its employees and has therefore purchased ergonomic armchairs and keyboards for all notebooks, in addition, it promotes home working for its employees and in company promotions it uses cartering services, which are offered by a home-protected workshop.
"In addition, we are supporting the "People in Need" foundation, which provides concrete assistance to the local Roma community," says Ondřej Suchý, the company's representative.
Last but not least, it is also worth mentioning the activity of the cleaning company "Praní a čištění" from the Karlovy Vary region, which contributes its employees in the form of extra bonuses to their pension insurance.
"We provide our employees with contributions to catering, the commute to work, to increase their qualifications and for holidays," says Josef Strapáč from the construction company Czasch from Bruntál, the winner in the Moravian-Silesian region.
All district finalists of the Vodafon Company of the Year competition were automatically registered for the category Responsible Company of the Year, which answered the questions of the questionnaire enclosed with the registration and sent it to the organizer no later than August 15.
Incorrectly or incomplete completed answers in the questionnaire have been excluded from the evaluation by the organizer.
"If the representatives of the competing companies left the questions unanswered, they were expelled from the "Responsibility-Conscious Company of the Year" competition, but they were not disqualified in the main Vodafone company of the year competition."
"A separate registration only for this competition is not possible, since it is only a subcategory of the Main Competition Company of the Year," explains Karel Goldman of the organising agency Bison & Rose.
Pilsner Bodenfonds - Arrests of the police at a public tender
In the building of the Bodenfonds in Pilsen, the anti-corruption police made arrests in recent days.
In the process, the local media entrepreneur and real estate dealer Luboš G. was handcuffed by the detectives.
He had allegedly tried to manipulate a current public competition for the settlement of restitution claims in such a way that the land offered in the competition occurred to a specific interested party.
He is said to have paid Luboš G. half a million.
"At this time, I can only confirm that the police prosecute a person because of the offense of fraudulent machinations in a public competition," Antonie Zelená, the head of the Pilsner prosecutor's office, told the Právo newspaper that it could not provide further details with regard to the ongoing investigations.
24 hours later, the 46-year-old entrepreneur was released by police.
In view of the sparse official information, one can only speculate on how he was able to manipulate public competition.
The spokeswoman of the Lubomír Černá Soil Fund cited Pr�ávo’s question in this regard that the police had actually made arrests at the Pilsen branch of the Soil Fund, during public competition.
"It was about a client and by no means about one of our employees," she concretized.
According to Ms Černá, public competition had the correct designation "call for the submission of offers claims for the transfer of land in accordance with the land law" and would take place in cases in which several applicants show interest in the land offered.
"These persons, within the framework of the solicitation, offer a higher value of their restitution claims to be settled by the transfer than the declared land value."
"According to the claims offered, the order of the potential acquirers is determined, after which the land fund transfers the property to the person with the highest offer," explains the fund's spokeswoman.
Luboš G. was obviously available information about who had registered at the request and since he himself was an authorized person, since he had purchased a number of restitution claims, he offered a landown interest that he withdrew from the competition.
Obviously against fat severance.
This would also be a sufficient explanation for why he had such a large amount of money with him when he was arrested.
Luboš G. has had all too good contacts with the Pilsner Bodenfonds in the past.
For example, he also played a role in the causa of the former head of the soil fund Václav Sankota, who was prosecuted because he handed out land in substitute restitutions, to which location decisions had already been made on construction projects.
At that time Luboš G. also acquired land from the land fund, for example, which was destined for the construction of public roads, more precisely where the construction of the access magistrale to the Borské pole industrial park was planned.
Later he exchanged these very favorably with the magistrate for a house directly in the historic center of the city, which far exceeded the value of the land.
At that time, the town hall had to give small things with a heavy heart in order not to jeopardize the construction project by blocking the properties.
Household housing costs are rising by a few hundred months
Householders will not only have to expect higher costs for all types of energy from January, but also higher water and sewage charges and for district heating and in some places rents are also growing.
Families will have to pull several hundred kroners a more out of their wallets every month from January than before.
Electricity will be 4.6 percent more expensive, gas by 2 percent and more, in some cities you will have to pay up to a tenth more for water deliveries and in some places district heating deliveries will also be more expensive.
Electricity control alone costs housing households a few hundred krons more each year.
The electricity prices have been reduced by all the main suppliers, so it is to blame for the significant promotion of electricity generation from renewable sources, namely from photovoltaic systems.
Although gas only becomes moderately more expensive, this is exactly when people have to heat the most and have the highest consumption.
Here, too, a few hundred crowns more per year are to be expected.
While electricity and gas are becoming more expensive throughout the Czech Republic, the prices of district heating vary from city to city.
Above all, larger places are planning price increases.
For example, households in Pardubice and Hradec Králové will have to pay up to 50 kronon more per month from January for district heating, more than 1000 kronors more per year pay families in Liberec.
The inhabitants of Prague, in turn, face 9 percent higher water and sewage charges and this in the event that the city increases the infrastructure rent.
If the rent doesn't change, it's only 6.8 percent.
Compared to the whole of the Czech Republic, however, the price in Prague is still among the lowest.
In North Bohemia, where prices are already among the highest, water and sewage charges are expected to rise by 7.4 percent.
Enormous rent increases are to be expected, for example, in 140,000 rented apartments, where the so-called rent regulation will be omitted from January.
For example, the housing company CPI Byty, which owns 2500 apartments in Ústí nad Labem, plans to increase rents by one hundred percent from January.
Whoever disagrees with this rent increase, the company wants to prosecute in court.
Regulated rents, which are still paided in nearly 300,000 apartments today, are expected to rise by more than 10 percent next year.
Czech children get drunk with Fernet and rum beer is out - it just seems too slow
Fernet, rum and vodka, for once a good whisky, which was stolen from the father from the house bar.
These are the drinks, after which drunken Czech teenagers under fifteen most often have to be emergency treated.
There is no excluding children who have to keep their hangovers sleeping in the hospital repeatedly.
This emerges from the experiences of physicians facing similar cases in the inclusion of the children's departments of clinics.
"Beer or wine are completely out of fashion because it just takes too long for you to be drunk."
"Today's trend is clearly hard alcohol," said Právo Luděk Pelikán, the pediatrician's chiever's physician at the central Bohemian Hořovice clinic.
"Whisky, if it succeeds in stealing a bottle from the father's house bar, and if not, then at least Fernet or vodka.
"Vodka has the advantage that you can mix him with Juice, then he doesn't smell so strongly," explains the chief doctor.
The spokeswoman of the Faculty Hospital Motol Eva Jurinová agrees with him.
"Most often, it's about Fernet or Rum."
"That's today's alcohol trend, especially at elementary schools."
"Occasionally it was also about Slibowitz, who you take from home," Ms. Jurinová said.
According to thirteen- or fourteen-year-old drinkers in the hospital reception are by no exception.
In front of the clinic in Hořovice, where he has been working since this year, he worked for ten years in the pediatrics department of Motol Hospital, where the ambulances delivered drunk students from near and far.
"A girl had to be hospitalized four times."
"She began her career at the age of 12, when she was brought in for the first time with 3.5 per million alcohol in her blood and also in combination with "extase," the doctor described a particularly severe case.
"She was at some dance party at the exhibition grounds in Holešovice."
"The interesting thing was that it didn't seem strange to her mother that her daughter didn't come home for 48 hours," the chief doctor wonders.
Precisely the lack of interest of the parents or their unbelievable tolerance when toasting with their own offspring is considered to be one of the main causes of the growing alcoholism of children.
According to experts, children are not interested in real dependency in the true sense of the word.
"Alcoholism refers to a long-term chronic dependence on alcohol, including all related phenomena, and this is not the case with these children."
Among those who repeatedly enjoy alcohol, a negative family-social background is usually characteristic," explains chief physician Pelikán.
According to Motol's spokeswoman, Ms Jurinová, by mid-November, about forty drunken children were admitted to the local department of pediatric and adolescent medicine.
However, only the cases with severe alcohol poisoning, which required hospitalization, go into the statistics.
Drunk children who are brought home by parents or, if necessary, by the police after outpatient treatment, will not be registered.
In addition to cases in which children only drink a bottle of schnapps in the school bench, according to Mr Pelikán, there are three seasonal amplitudes, which can be relied on one hundred percent: Walpurgis Night, New Year's Eve and the end of the school year.
"The latter for two reasons - either out of grief because of the bad testimony or out of joy for the holidays."
"On Walpurgis night, I've also seen seven drunken children at once in the recording," emphasizes Doctor Pelikán.
A drinking child who spends the night under medical supervision is paradoxically better off than a child struggling only with an awkward hangover the next day.
The headache in the morning afterwards, which stems from dehydration and glucose deficiency, can be expelled in the hospital with a hydratory infusion with glucose and nutrients that the organism lacks.
"They're clearly better off, because after the "glorious" experience in the evening, they wake up in a good mood without anything hurting somewhere."
"They're just a little confused about where they're," admits Doctor Pelikán, sometimes spoiling the patient's good mood at least by enumerating them the costs she're going to cost treatment.
Because the transport by ambulancy and the night in an acute bed come well and gladly on a few tens of thousands of crowns.
"Every now and then I used this as a means of education, if I directly calculated to the children at the visit, what cost their drunkenness and that it can certainly happen that they have to work throughout the holidays in order to earn the money for a single such party," added Doctor Pelikán.
In his opinion, however, the health insurance companies do not resort to recourse procedures in order to demand the costs necessary to heal the drunken children from the parents.
Two L-159 training aircraft in foreign hands for the first time - in Spain
Two light L-15-159 fighter jets from a total of five that the Czech Ministry of Defense exchanged for the Casa C-295M transport aircraft flew to the Spanish city of Sevilla on Tuesday, where the Spanish aircraft maker Casa - EADS settles.
It is the first time machines of this type have been in possession of a foreign army.
Abroad, they have so far only been seen at maneuvers or air shows.
It is about the airworthy two-seater version L-159T1, which was operated by the Czech army.
The remaining three aircraft are single-seater L-159.
These are currently still preserved in the Aero Vodochody, the intermediary of this business - the company Omnipol - took it over last week.
Jan Pejšek, according to the Ministry of Defense Ministry spokesman, is not yet known when she physically expedizes the planes from Vodochody.
The single-seater L-159 fighters, in turn, were separated from long-term stored aircraft without any further deposits and investments from the ministry's department.
"The double-seated L-159T1 have been exchanged in current condition and appearance," Pejšek told Právo newspaper.
He added that the army would not have to manage without two-seater warplanes, as the Ministry of Defense signed a 200-million contract with Aero Vodochody in May 2009 over the conversion of four superfluous L-159 to the two-seater version.
A new plane took over the army in July, the second wants to take it over by the end of this year.
According to Pejšek, EADS is committed to enforcing the warplanes into the equipment of one of the NATO countries.
"In the treaty it is clear that the EADS company is the customer of the L-159 aircraft and that these are intended to be used to train pilots of NATO countries.
"It is therefore a matter for the customer how he realizes the matter," Pejšek said.
The business newspaper (HN) had previously reported that the Spanish government has no interest in the L-159.
The barter of five L-159s for a Casa transport aircraft and the purchase of further aircraft for 3.5 billion crowns had been approved last year by the government of Mirek Topolánek.
At the time, the Ministry of Defense had not tendered a tender procedure for purchasing, but addressed EADS directly.
The defense office at the time argued that this exchange would support the future export of the L-159 abroad.
The Czech army purchased Aero 72 machines, of which it uses only 24, the rest it wanted to divest.
By the way, the purchase of the Airplanes Casa should also have a judicial aftermath.
The European Commission sued the Czech Ministry of Defense at the European Court of Justice. The reason? It would have had to select the aircraft due to an all-European tender and by no means by addressing a single manufacturer.
The army has already taken over three aircraft Casa, the last one is scheduled to land at Kbely Airport within fourteen days.
A prolonged forefinger than ring finger means a lower risk of developing cancer
Men whose forefinger is longer than the ring finger are obviously exposed to a lower risk of prostate cancer.
That's the surprising finding of a study published Wednesday by the British Journal of Cancer.
This is to be related to the level of the male hormone testosterone.
"Our results show that the relative length of the fingers can serve as a simple test for the risk of prostate cancer, especially in men to sixty," explained the study's co-author Ros Eeles, a professor at the cancer research institute at the University in Warwick.
The finger could thus help determine which men should necessarily undergo a prostate cancer test, especially if there are genetic risks or other risk factors, such as familial health burdens, Ms Eeles added.
In the 1994-2009 period, she pursued more than 1500 patients with prostate cancer with her colleagues in Britain, but at the same time 3000 healthy men.
In more than half of the participants in the study, the index finger of the right hand was shorter than the ring finger
For these men, there was the same probability of prostate cancer as for 19 percent of those men whose ring and index fingers were the same.
For men whose forefingers were longer than the ring finger, however, this risk fell by 33 percent, for men to sixty even by 87 percent.
The ratio of the length of the two mentioned fingers, which form even in prenatal age, obviously reflects the different levels of sex hormones to which the child was exposed in the uterus.
Less testosterone is equivalent to a prolonged index finger.
Previous research has shown that especially testosterone accelerates the growth of prostate cancer.
Václav Pačes, leading Czech geneticist and former chairman of the Academy of Sciences, told ČTK that this study was fleetingly known to him, but that he does not consider the relationship between finger length and cancer risk to be "completely proven".
Czech Republic in the snow - D1 and D5 are blocked
Just like that of the largest part of Europe, the Czech Republic is under a blanket of snow on Thursday morning.
On the streets and sidewalks there is mostly stuck snow, the winter servicers were in many places all night on the axis.
The D5 motorway between kilometer 89 and 90 in the direction of Rozvadov is closed due to some accidents caused by trucks and passenger cars and also the D1 at Brno in the direction of Prague "stands".
Novinky.CZ told the dispatcher of the Central Auto-Moto-Klub (ÚAMK).
At kilometre 91, a truck crashed, two trucks collided at kilometre 93, and there were injured in this accident.
There are also a few cars in the ditch.
There are also problems at kilometre 185 of the D1 motorway towards Prague.
Here, too, two trucks collided.
The drivers were supposed to leave just behind Brno at kilometre 190 or drive over Brno and drive up at kilometer 182 at the village of Kývalka.
Miles of traffic jams form there.
An accident also happened on the D11 and that immediately on the driveway to Prague.
In Prague itself lies a several-centimeter-thick blanket of snow and thermometer shows minus seven degrees Celsius.
Motor columns are formed, for example, in the district of Prosek, and Štěrboholská is also completely clogged.
The safest means of transport is the metro.
The trams run with delays, for example on the line between Karlsplatz and Friedensplatz.
At eight o'clock in the morning, the Prague crisis team meets.
There are also problems with the railway and on the airfield.
The Czech State Railways had to annulate seven express train connections due to snowdrifts.
There are problems, for example, on the route Prague - Kralupy or Prague - Beroun.
Travellers should definitely inform themselves about the current situation.
Ruzyně Airport has been closed until 5 a.m. since 11 a.m. on Wednesday.
Most roads in South Moravia are only passable with increased caution.
Around Vyškov three side roads are completely unpassable, in the vicinity of Brno snow drifts form in places and in the vicinity of Blansko you are forced to use snow blowers on some higher roads.
One of the most affected areas is Výškov.
Three roads are still completely closed: Rousínov - Slavkov, Bohdalice - Kučerov and also Šaratice - Milešovice.
Here, too, you will have to use snow blowers for snow removal.
Until now, it is not possible to estimate when they will become navigable again.
Caution should be exercised in the Hodonín region of the White Carpathians.
ČTK cited on the roads, which may only be sprinkled with inert scattering agents for nature conservation reasons, a blanket up to five centimeters thick, led ČTK.
In the Czech Republic from two to ten centimeters of fresh snow fell, most in northern bohemia - sometimes up to 18 centimeters.
Up to 15 centimeters of new snow fell in the Moravian-Silesian region.
In the Czech Republic, however, it slowly stops snowing.
U.S. soldiers have a new "intelligent weapon."
The American troops have used a new "intelligent" weapon in Afghanistan for the first time, which so far is only known under their code name XM-25.
The generals hope that this new automatic weapon will influence the conflict with the Taliban there and postpone the balance of power in their favor.
The new weapon is something like a super-accurate cannon.
Their projectiles are steered into the target by laser with an accuracy of only a few meters.
The special projectiles of caliber 25 mm, which were developed for this rifle and contain highly explosive explosives, function practically like small grenades.
The projectiles can be programmed in such a way that they can explode not only on impact on the target, but at any point in their ballistic trajectory.
This means that you can use it against an enemy that hides behind an obstacle - a wall or in the trench.
From above he is usually not protected, so the murderous steel shard rain from above can easily put him out of action.
According to Richard Audett, who participated in the development of the XM-25, the new combat rifle is the first infantry weapon to take advantage of "intelligent" technologies.
"The soldier basically proceeds like this: he targets the target and sends out a laser beam that determines the distance, so he gains an exact visor point, according to which he corrects the angle of elevation, then he only needs to push.
“Let’s say you measured 543 m.”
"When you push off, the gun recharges and shoots the projectile at a distance of 543 meters plus minus one, two or three meters.
400 jobs will be relocated
The Fujitsu Group is relocating its subsidiary in Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
In July next year, 400 employees will move their workplace from the previous office building at Marienbader Platz in Bad Homburg to Astro Park.
The Japanese group Fujitsu is relocating its office in Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
According to the industry giant, which specializes in computers and its maintenance on Wednesday, 400 women and men will move their workplace from the previous office building at Marienbader Platz in Bad Homburg to Astro Park in July next year, which is a large office complex on Lyoner Straße in Frankfurt's Niederrad district.
A spokeswoman for Fujitsu explained that in the medium term, an additional 100 jobs of the group should be housed there, which have so far been distributed among the region.
The rental offer was extremely attractive, in addition, the open-plan offices in Astro Park would better accommodate the operation of the employees.
Also in Bad Homburg, Fujitsu had only been tenants.
The managing director Bernd Wagner said, "with Frankfurt-Niederrad we have found a central location with excellent infrastructure".
The excellent connection to the airport is particularly important for a global company like Fujitsu.
The recent relocation of an important company within the region yesterday evoked joy among the winners and disappointment among the losers.
Markus Frank (CDU), Frankfurt's economics department (CDU), said that a "global player" had once again been gained.
This is a "greatiful commitment to the location."
Fujitsu fit the city, the settlement is an important impulse for Niederrad.
The mayor of Bad Homburg Michael Korwisi (The Greens) regretted Fujitsu's decision.
The city was informed early by the management about the intention.
"Of course, we offered our help to the company," Korwisi said.
However, the friendly contacts could not have changed the difficulties of the computer manufacturer with the landlord of the property at Marienbader Platz.
Ultimately, economic reasons would have been determined.
Now it is a question of preventing a long vacancy in such a prominent place.
"I hope the owner and landlord of the building shows up cooperatively."
With the other buildings on Marienbader Platz ten years ago, ten years ago, the house used by Fujitsu at Rathausplatz and the neighboring "Little Vertice" were one of the most controversial construction projects.
As a joint project of the city and a private project developer, a new city center was created between the station and the town hall, which caused criticism both because of the massive development of the areas previously partly used as a parking lot and the height of the buildings.
In 2001, the investment consulting and rating company Feri, the software manufacturer for travel distribution systems, Start Amadeus, as well as the computer manufacturer Fujitsu, which was then still affiliated with Siemens, sourced the houses.
The buildings used by Fujitsu were acquired in 2006 by the Frankfurt-based Deutsche Immobilien-Chances Group together with the Morgan Stanley Real Estate Fund and resold a year later to the Spanish Falcon Real Estate Investments SA in Madrid.
The Tokyo-based Fujitsu-Konuzer describes itself as the world's third-largest IT provider.
For example, it provides companies with computers and servers and also provides the necessary service.
The office in the Rhine-Main area is one of the largest in Germany, but Fujitsu has far more employees in Munich.
From Bad Homburg, the subsidiary Fujitsu Technology Solutions moves into Frankfurt-Niederrad, the other subsidiaries at this location should be Fujitsu Services and a company called TDS.
7000 square meters were rented in the Astro Park office building.
In the twenty-year-old complex - the name alludes to its star-shaped floor plan - is based, among other things, by the Central Association of Electrical Engineering and Electronics Industries.
Amazon suspends servers - threats against Assange
WikiLeaks is becoming increasingly distressed.
The Internet service provider Amazon has banned the disclosure platform from its servers.
Possibly there is political pressure after the publication of hundreds of thousands of secret diplomat peaches.
WikiLeaks founder Assange is reportedly staying in England.
WikiLeak's disclosure platform is coming under more and morse fire in the United States.
The internet activists lost access to their previous American server and are now looking for an Internet home in Europe.
Celebrate American Senator Joe Lieberman, meanwhile, called on other countries and companies to boycott Wikileaks.
And WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who is on Interpol's manhunt lists, is said to be in Britain, according to a newspaper report.
"Wikileaks displaced from Amazon server."
"Free speech in the land of threes," the disclosure activists announced via short message service Twitter on Wednesday (local time).
Shortly before then, the Internet company Amazon had banned the Wikileaks documents from its servers.
The Internet activists had resorted to the Amazon Web Service (AWS) during the publication of the secret diplomatic cables in order to cope with the hight access to the documents.
WikiLeaks tweeted: “Primary – our dollars are now being spent on employing people in Europe.”
If Amazon has such problems with freedom of speech, "they should stop selling books."
Amazon has stopped the use of its servers by WikiLeaks aftend employees of Senator Joe Lieberman to investigate, CNN reported, citing Lieberman's office.
The chairman of the Senate Committee on Homeland Security had threatened Amazon with a boycott, Britain's "Guardian" reported.
"I'd have wished for Amazon to take this measure sooner in light of WikiLeak's previous releases of classified information," the senator said.
Lieberman, meanwhile, called on to deny the platform the use to servers in other countries.
"Wikileaks" illegal, outrageous and ruthless action jeopardizes our national security and endangers lives around the globe," he said.
“Not a responsible company—whether American or foreign—should help WikiLeaks in its efforts to spread stolen material.”
WikiLeaks founder Assange, who is wanted on suspicion of rape by authorities in Sweden, is on Interpol's manhunt list.
According to a report by London's "The Independent" newspaper, the 39-year-old Australian is staying in the U.KYO, the exact location is known to Scotland Yard.
The Internet activist has always described the investigation in Sweden as intrigue of his opponents.
WikiLeaks spokesman Kristinn Hrafnsson said there had been threats in London.
"There have even been calls following an assassination of Julian Assange."
That's why Assange rightly fears for his security, Hrafnsson said.
The WikiLeaks founder will therefore continue to hide.
Hrafnsson apparently reacted to statements from North America.
In America, the former Republican governor of Arkansas, Mike Huckabee, had demanded that the person responsible for the WikiLeaks revelations should be indicted and executed for treason.
In Canada, an adviser to Prime Minister Stephen Harper - apparently ironically - said on television that Assange should be "killed" and Obama could "use a drone."
Between the White House and Assange, meanwhile, an exchange of blows flared up.
U.S. President Barack Obama's spokesperson, Robert Gibbs, called it "ridiculous and absurd" that Assange had demanded the resignation of Secretary of State Hillary Clinton if she is responsible for recent espionage calls on diplomats.
“I’m not quite sure why we care about a guy’s opinion with a website,” Gibbs said.
"Our foreign policy and the interests of this country are far more significant than its one website."
The American government is now comprehensively examining the protection of its databases.
Russell Travers, vice-director of anti-terrorism measures, had been appointed as special envoy, the White House said in a statement.
Travers was responsible for developing "necessary structural reforms" that had become necessary after WikiLeak's disclodination of message reports.
The White House also wants to review the ways in which the entire administration exchanges and protects information.
The U.S. government also announced that it intends to ensure the safety of dissidents and the most active, should they be in jeopardy by the WikiLeaks publication.
"We've done everything we can to get in touch with them," foreign ministry spokesman Philip Crowley said.
"We stand ready to protect them if that should become necessary."
Ice-University
At "Gelato University" near Bologna, ice pastry chefs are prepared for better seasons.
The three-day basic course for future gelatieri has the largest influx and visitors from all over the world.
So where does all the ice cream come from?
From the gelatier and the gelataio.
The gelatiere, in German somewhat ancient the ice pastryer, produces the cream according to all the rules of Italian icemaking art.
The Gelataio takes the ice cream from the refrigerated counter with a spatula, strokes it into the waffle and then sells it.
To know this, you don't have to go to Anzola dell'Emilia in the province of Bologna.
But if you want to know even more about ice cream, you can't get past the "Gelato-University" of the Italian company Carpigiani.
Ice ice-university was set up by the maker of ice machines in 2003, so that the ice cream from its machines everywhere meets the high Italian ideals.
The three-day basic course for future gelatieri has the largest influx and visitors from all over the world.
However, there are also multi-day special courses about chocolate ice cream and internships in the in-house ice cream parlor.
Some people are dreaming of a new career in the mid-life crisis, others just venture on the smooth ice of their vague longings.
This time there are 40 participants from 19 countries from all continents - from Brazil to Hungary to Saudi Arabia.
Jacky, an art teacher from California, loves the slow-food movement and wants to offer a European quality product in the land of industrial ice-cream.
Anke from Berlin is a management consultant and wants to bag something of her own after years in a nerve-racking job.
Two young Egyptian women dream of opening an ice cream parlor in Cairo.
A man from Malta and one from Brazil would like to improve the quality of the ice cream in their bars - and Canadian housewife Julia arrives because her children love Italian ice cream.
Maestro-Gelatiere Luciano Ferrari first explains to the participants the ideal properties of the perfect ice cream.
Crucial is not only good taste, but also the silky suppleness and good consistency.
Luciano does not forget the icing on the icing on the icing on the icing of the national and southern superstructure: "Italian gelato is simply the best - and then there is still a lot of romance."
The first step of production seems quite simple.
Luciano dumps the basic ingredients of milk, cream, milk powder, sugar and stabilizers into a large mixing device, which heats everything to 85 degrees with an electronic program and then immediately cools down to four degrees.
Then he turns up a rooster on the machine, and out comes a pasteurized white base cream, base for all dairy ice creams.
Immediately, the lecturer is stabbed with questions: Can ice cream only be made with natural substances without a stabilizer?
How can you let one’s desire to experiment run free?
Luciano warns against being misleaded by the zeitgeist.
If you talk about ice cream "only from natural ingredients", you may still have used stabilizers - usually a natural product, such as guark flour from the guar bean.
Of course you can also make ice cream without a stabilizer, only it has to be sold within a day, otherwise it collapses, and the customer bites on noisy ice grains.
Certain variations on the base mass are allowed.
It becomes more exciting with the flavors.
For the taste in the ice, Maestro Luciano distinguishes between the ingredients that you buy in the fruit department or with the sweets of the supermarket.
In addition, there are powders and pastes.
"Suppliers will all try to swit you dozens of flavoured additives."
"But you're supposed to experiment."
Either to the fruit sorbets with sugar and fresh fruits.
Or just with the ice cream from the white base mass, which becomes a yellow cream with eggs and with cocoa powder as the basis for all sorts of chocolate seductions.
Now the students are allowed to go to the ice
Luciano makes them four different types of vanilla, chocolate, mocha and hazelnut ice cream - with or without eggs in the basicle, with more or less vanilla aroma or other natural additives.
The same experiment is subjected to fruit ice cream varieties.
More flavoring substances, more fruit puree - it's not necessarily better in terms of taste.
Hazelnut ice cream doesn't necessarily taste nuttier if it contains more hazelnuts.
The most popular kiwi ice makes the working group, which uses more fruits, less water and slightly less stabilizer.
Only a balanced mixture correctly highlights the taste of a gelato, says Luciano.
Ice production is a computational task: If peach puree is used with a lot of sugar, the sugar content must be reduced in the basic recipe.
An extra portion of sugar is required for lemon ice cream.
Luciano pulls out a device with which it determines the sugar content of the fruit varieties (winemakers thus measure the Oechsle in the grape must).
Luckily, tables make computing easier.
Making ice doesn't require as much bodily effort as it used to.
Not only the device for the automatic production of the base mass helps.
There is also a device in which the finished ice mixture is creamily beaten at the same time and cooled to sub-zero temperatures.
Despite all automation, small breakdowns aren’t missing.
Brazilian Gustavo spills himself and his course colleagues from top to bottom with fruit puree as the oversized handblunder gets out of his control.
"In every course," says Maestro Luciano, "it's only a matter of time before something like this happens."
Instructors in Twilight
The European food authority Efsa was subverted by the industry experimenting with gene plants.
This accusation is now formulated by the Testbiotech expert group, which sees itself as an independent institute for the impact assessment of biotechnology.
"It sounds like a conspiracy theory, but we've documented it well," Testbiotech chief executive Christoph Then said in Munich on Wednesday.
The head of the Efsa expert group on genetically modified crops, Harry Kuiper, and another Efsa expert have also been under contract with the Ilsi lobbyist association for years.
It is paid for by corporations such as Monsanto, Nestle or Bayer.
Kuiper was instrumental in the Efsa test regulations for gene plants and was thus in a clear conflict of interest, criticizes Then.
The Efsa regulations are analogously identical to Ilsi position papers and thus bear the handwriting of the industry, which is actually to be controlled by the authority in Parma.
It is the second time that Testbiotech criticizes a collision of interests.
For example, a high-ranking Efsa manager has recently joined Syngenta without conditions or a transitional period - and has thus changed sides.
Mitteldeutsche Rundfunk (MDR) had also recently unveiled personnel connections between the controlling EU authority and the industrial lobby, which made the Federal Environment Agency as well as EU parliamentarians clairvoyant.
Efsa, meanwhile, can't spot a conflict of interest.
All serious data, including those of the industry as long as they are of high professional quality, it said in a statement.
Experts who work for Efsa aren't asked that they have no contacts with the industry.
However, they would have to disclose them.
Kuiper admits his work for the International Life Sciences Institute (Ilsi) in the USA.
This describes itself as a neutral forum for experts from science, industry and politics - among other things on the risk assessment for biotechnology without lobbying mandates.
In the matter, independent experts such as Then criticize that genetically modified crops are currently approved without any feeding test in animals and the reaction of gene plants to environmental influences is not tested.
"We want to know, for example, what's genetically modified corn doing on the field," says Then.
Current practice is to test only isolated proteins, but not the entire gene plant, which does not provide reliable results.
"If you don't seek the risks, how are you supposed to find them?" Then asks accusatively.
Angelika Hilbeck sees it that way.
The scientist has been researching genetic engineering since 1994 and is currently working at the Technical University of Zurich.
The industry in this area, as in the USA, is now increasingly trying to influence or discredit critical scientists in Europe.
There are therefore hardly any researchers who are not intertwined with corporations.
As a rule, critical studies for companies would not be published.
"Industry controls security research," Hilbeck claims.
The trace of the stones
Uli Gsell has also taken care of some, a total of about 400 kilos.
On Monday evening, he stood in front of Stuttgart train station, the tuba on his back.
It was demo.
It's snouting.
The story is well known: in September, the excavators had approached and had begun to abort a large piece of the north wing.
The Stuttgart world was no longer the same: tens of thousands demonstrated against S21, the billion-dollar lowering of the station.
When it came to the construction stop, much of the wing lay in ruins.
Broken into 6000, 7000 pieces.
Crailsheim mussel limestone, a soft coarse stone, installed between 1914 and 1920.
Gsell is a sculptor in Ostfildern, 43 years old.
He's secured some of the chunks.
"These stones are relics."
"Youse mustn't disappear."
He enthuses: "A cheap and poor stone, simple and earthly."
When he sawed one up, he smelled of cigarette smoke.
He doesn't yet know what he's going to do with the chunks.
Stones have time.
Uli Gsell too.
The trace of the Stuttgart stones leads into a forest near Tübingen to a former ammunition depot of the French army, where Manuel Rongen has erected his stone empire.
The 44-year-old trades in old stones.
He has bought all those who went to the demolition in Stuttgart.
"It would be a shame they're ground into gravel or disappear into the landfill," he says in his office, a wooden shop that was once a waking cottage.
He was insulted and threatened, he says, when it was written in the newspapers in September that he had bought the remains of the north wing from the demolition contractor.
But he's been talking to the S-21 opponents for days.
And he explained to them that he couldn't do anything for demolition.
And if it happens, then it would be the most sensible thing if the stones were reused.
Approximately 200 S-21-opponents then took one of the 80-kilo cuboid, unit price 6.50 to 14 euros.
As a souvenir, but also in the hope of being able to give it back during a reconstruction.
Rongen had bought 500 tons, 25 large truckloads.
About half are still stored in the forest, the rest has long since been sold.
Garden owners, landscape gardeners, house builders.
From everywhere, they had come and towed away what trunk space and trailers could carry.
The Stuttgart stones of nudge have long since turned into "Mäuerle" or into boundaries of herbal beds.
"Actually, this stone is nothing special," Rongen says.
"Wouldn't be the political component."
He climbs into his off-road vehicle, does a tour of his 20-hectare empire.
He graduated and dropped out of law, later biology, then became interested in computers and eventually became a stone dealer.
And stone lovers.
It stores 40000 tonnes of stones.
From all over Germany, he carts his treasures together.
He says he's the Republic's largest parlied stone trader.
He's braking.
There are artfully hewn sandstones in the snow.
"They once belonged to Dresden's Marienbrücke," he recounts.
Elbe sandstone, yellow, warm, he raves.
“My favorite stone.”
It's going up the road through the forest.
He points to a heap: red sandstone, formerly part of the Karlsruhe railway station.
And further on.
"Beautiful travertine."
Once the quaders belonged to the grandstand of the Stuttgart Neckarstadion.
Everywhere in the snow stones and stories, Cannstatter travertine from the Stuttgart police headquarters, reddish granite from the Cathedral of Sankt Blasien, Tuff from the Heilbronn Hospital.
What work, what efforts, what sweat.
When Rongen thinks about how the stones used to be carved by hand and moved with simple pulleys and ox-carts, he becomes quite reverenting.
"It would be a scandal if you shred something like that."
He didn't become rich, he didn't get rich at all.
But 14 people are employed by the stone merchant today, in addition, stonemasons, a sculptor, a blacksmith, a glassblower and a horticulturalist have settled up in the forest.
"We're working hard, but it's satisfying," says the remnant recycler.
Soon he'll have to go to Cologne.
There are stones to save, too.
Talent struggle for high-potentials
There's a lot of crowds at the buffet.
16 students crowd around salmon rolls, tomato soup and sweets.
The Technical University of Braunschweig and the car manufacturer Volkswagen have invited the mechanical engineering and design students to a workshop.
In a kind of idea competition, they are supposed to talk together with VW developers about the car of the future - and get to get to get to know Volkswagen.
In the competition for the smartest minds, meanwhile, many companies go into melee and, like VW, rely on cooperations with the universities.
Their mission: to recruit the most gifted graduates as early as possible in the lecture halls before they come to the job market and are courted by others.
And because the universities do not publish lists about the achievements of their students, one tries to speet and bind the exceptional talents in practical projects.
On the second day of the workshop at the TU Braunschweig, the car company advertises the talents.
A young lady from the human resources department distributes ballpoint pens, gummy bears and folders with VW logo.
It tells the hopeful young talents of internship programs and student jobs and shows them ways into the Group.
Nico Selle from TU Braunschweig supervises the project with VW.
He sees the benefits for both sides: "The students come into contact with the industry and gain experience."
"VW can get to know students at the same time, take a closer look at interesting candidates and think about who would be eligible as employees."
Such collaborations, according to Selle, are not uncommon.
It is new that the students are competing so aggressively.
"Otherwise, the focus was more on the professional project," says Selle.
The fight for the newly educated will probably be even harder.
In many industries, companies can no longer choose their employees from a large number of applicants.
But they have to advertise themselves and are particularly balted by highly skilled workers.
Especially graduates from the engineering disciplines are in an excellent negotiating position.
According to the Association of German Engineers VDI, more than 40,000 engineering positions remained vacant in October.
And the so-called engineering gap will become even bigger, according to VDI forecasts.
Roman Dykta goes on a talent hunt for the auditing and consulting company KPMG.
He organizes elaborate recruiting events such as the KPMG race, a kind of modern scavenger hunt, in which the participants have to find their way based on GPS coordinates.
The company intends to send eight promising students across the United States next year.
By the way, participants can look at KPMG stores and get to know the company.
Who is allowed to participate, decides in two selection rounds in advance.
Only the crème de la crème of the many applicants flies to the USA.
According to Dykta, the guns are getting bigger and bigger by punching and stabbing around the high potentials at the universities.
“We want to stand out from the crowd with the race.”
"Only a simple sailing trip was too little for us."
For talent hunter Dykta, the bill seems to work out.
"To date, we are still in contact with all participants of a similar action last year."
"Some are about to graduate and want to apply with us soon," he says.
In addition to the direct booing on the campus, especially small and medium-sized companies go on the prowl at professional fairs.
"We're trying to reach graduates at trade fairs."
"Approximately at Intersolar," reports Sitha Stübe, human resources manager at the solar technology manufacturer Solarworld.
The Bonn-based company employs around 1500 people in Germany.
Stübe knows about the problem of young talents.
There are currently 35 jobs advertised on the company website.
"It's very hard to fill vacant positions right now."
"That takes a lot of time and money," she says.
Another way is the contact through the university professors.
Solarworld cooperates with various universities, such as the university in Freiberg.
"The professors give indications that you can do internships with us or write your diploma thesis."
Expensive promotions aren't trending at the solar specialist.
For this, the company tries to promote itself with other advantages: personal atmosphere, flat hierarchies and easy communication.
"A large corporation often can't offer this," says HR manager Stübe.
Money no longer plays the main role in the job search.
Flexible working hours, childcare or good promotion opportunities are other decisive factors.
Solarworld ranked 55th among Germany’s 100 best employers in a study by the Great Place to Work Institute.
The institute draws up an annual leaderboard of German employers.
For this purpose, it asks employees how satisfied they are with their work and their job environment and looks at the framework conditions for the employees.
Personnel manager Stübe is also hoping for plus points among job seekers from the placement.
"I think applicants are particularly looking for external reviews about a potential employer," she says.
Wolf Reiner Kriegler from the German Employer Branding Academy advises companies that want to establish a good reputation as an employer with a long-term strategy.
He advises his customers for more authenticity.
And that, according to Kriegler, means especially internal measures.
"The best way to make yourself attractive as an employer is through one's own employees," he says.
In industry jargon, this means internal employer branding, i.e. employer brand formation within one’s own workforce.
"Satisfied employees are the best ambassadors."
If you treat your people well and they identify with the company, they would advertise their employer to friends and acquaintances by themselves.
"It's like a game with a gang," Kriegler says.
Obama halts new oil drilling
There will be no new oil drilling off the U.S. state of Florida in the Gulf of Mexico in the coming years.
A corresponding ban has been issued by the government in Washington - and thus definitively revoked its earlier plans.
Even off the Atlantic coast, new drilling remains prohibited for the time being.
This was announced by Interior Minister Ken Salazar on Wednesday.
The trigger for the strict ban, which is to apply at least for the next five years, is the explosion of the "Deepwater Horizon" drilling platform in April.
Before the misfortune, which craved the lives of eleven workers and led to the largest U.S
Supporters want to reduce U.S. dependence on oil supplies from abroad.
Who bears the responsibility for the devastating oil spill in the Gulf has not yet been conclusively clarified.
At the center of the criticism is the British energy group BP, which is confronted with billions in demands.
An investigative commission on behalf of Obama is due to present its findings over the course of December.
In October, Interior Minister Ken Salazar lifted the drilling freeze imposed after the explosion.
Subsequently, various oil companies immediately submitted applications for new drilling projects.
With tighter controls and stricter security regulations, Obama and Salazar want to rule out a renewed disaster.
At present, the competent control authority, which had fallen into disrepute after numerous allegations of corruption, is being fundamentally reformed.
In parallel, Obama has announced plans to close lucrative tax loopholes for oil and gas companies.
The industry now publicly warns that the production volume in Gulf would be significantly lower than before due to stricter regulation.
"The government's plans are the biggest brake for the growth of the oil and gas industry," is the result of a recent industry poll by consulting firm BDO - even before Wednesday's drilling ban.
"Nobody knows exactly what's going to change with regulation."
Investments in new funding projects would be deferred until further notice - at the expense of growth.
Ogoniland burned down
The water in the rivers is oil-brown, it smells like at the gas station: billions of liters of leaked crude oil have destroyed nature in Nigerian Ogoniland.
People live from theft, corruption and illicit oil cooking.
Celestine Akpobari thinks it's good.
"Don't worry," the eco-activist reassures the journalists: "You're still going to get enough oil sludge in front of the lens today."
The 37-year-old Nigerian has pursued the attempt to photograph a boy wading up to his chest in the water with the streaks surrounding him: in the rainbow-coloured water shine, the scene looks almost pretty.
It is impossible to see that brown oil swaths float beneath the surface.
In the past, shrimules used to be collected here, Esther Badom says on the banks of the Bodo Jetty River.
In the meantime, the sluggish flowing body of water is completely dead.
Five years ago, tons of crude oil poured out of a bristled pipeline into the huge wetland.
Fishermen from the nearby town of Bodo now have to row for six hours to get into fish-containing waters: Ogoniland, located in the heart of Niger Delta, is considered one of the worst polluted regions in the world.
On the way through the mangrove swamps crisscrossed by streams, the full extent of the devastation becomes apparent.
Instead of bright water, the motor boat digs brown broth, those who dive their hand into the water pull out oily fingers.
The roots of the mangrove trees protrude from the ground like black licorice sticks, sadiously leaving isolated palm trees hanging their fronds.
Swarms of birds, as are otherwise customary in wetlands, are sought in vain: As soon as the outboard is silent, spooky peace spreads in the former paradise, which smells like a gas station.
"What's happening here is ecological warfare," Celestine Akpobari sinister says: "We're living in constant danger to health."
The comparison of war is appropriate.
The Ogoni settlement area is a battlefield that has been contested for decades: more than 60 years ago, the first explorators from Europe invaded, where the first resistance was organized in the 1980s with the later executed writer Ken Saro-Wiwa, where the Shell Group had to cease production in 1993 because it became too dangerous for its oil workers.
Even today, the company is denied its return to Ogoniland: While more than two million barrels of black gold are pumped every day from the entire delta region of the triple size of Switzerland with over 5000 drill sources and 7000 kilometers of piping, the oil fields in Ogoniland are broken, the situation is too unstable for the Group.
Every a few hundred meters, steer racks protrude like bizarre sculptures from the water: "Christmas trees" the heads of the boreholes are called far too tender.
They are up to 50 years old, most of them corroded to the marrow.
Again and again, such a drilling head breaks apart, says Akpobari, then a constant stream of crude oil runs into the water.
More than two billion liters have flowed into the ecosystem of the delta in 50 years, according to expert estimates: year after year the amount of oil as it penetrated into the sea off Alaska during the accident of the supertanker "Exxon Valdez" 21 years ago - one of the biggest environmental disasters in the history of humanity.
Whoever is responsible for the ongoing eco-GAU is disputed.
While the Shell company blames sabotage and organized oil theft for more than 90 percent of pollution, environmentalists see burning drill heads and leaky pipelines as the main polluters.
Shell set significantly lower safety standards in Nigeria than usual in the world, the U.S. scientist Richard Steiner accused the company in 2008: The rate of defects in the lines alone is "many times higher than elsewhere."
In a place that remotely looks like an abandoned, oil-smeared workshop in the open air, figures emerge from the mangroves: they are splinter-naked and oil-smeared up to the neck.
Under one of the many barrels, which are partly connected with tubes, partly arbitrarily scattered on the premises, a fire flickers: an illegal small refinery.
In the barrel, the crude oil is heated over the fire for several hours, Michael, the group's chief executive, explains.
At first, gasoline penetrates through the pipes into the collecting pot, later dripping kerosene at the end of the line and finally diesel.
Michael and four employees produce up to four barrels of diesel à 300 liters per day: "This earns us 34,000 naira" - around 22 dollars.
The delta inhabitants use the chronic fuel scarcity in the largest petroleum state in Black Africa.
Niegia has only one refinery.
The illegal cookers can still undercut the diesel price, which is ridiculously low with just under 40 euro cents per litre: because they get their raw material for free.
It comes from "bunkers" who tap the pipeline network across Ogoniland: According to experts, at least 16 million liters of crude oil are lost to the country every daily throughout Niger Delta.
However, only a vanishingly small part of the provisionally temporarily stored raw material is illegally refined: organized criminal networks, which should also include politicians and high-ranking militaries, fill the lion’s share into tankers and smear it on the world market.
The activist Akpobari thinks that the debate about whether the environmental disaster is due more to the activities of the population or the lax mode of production is purely academic.
"If the oil companies were not perceived as invaders and if they had not so thoroughly destroyed the livelihood of the fishermen, there would be no sabotage, theft and illegal refineries."
In the meantime, however, Ogoni’s relationship with Shell in particular is so destroyed that only a withdrawal of the company would be in question: “Perhaps other nationalities such as the Chinese turn out to be better.”
That's when Michael gets nervous.
"Highest time that you disappear," the muscular oil man says.
"Every minute the soldiers can come."
If the illegal oil refiners are caught by the special unit of the armed forces, they are not threatened with arrest, but a painful bribe.
Like all Nigerians, the soldiers, who are mainly from the north, want to benefit from the raw material.
"This fucking stuff has put our country on the dog in every way," Akpobari says.
Investigators discover dangerous construction defects in A380 engines
The A380 apparently has a major security problem.
Investigators have discovered a possible construction fault during the investigation of an emergency machine, which could lead to a "catastrophic engine failure".
Qantas airline now wants to sue the engine manufacturer.
The passengers of the emergency-landed Qantas-Airbus in early November apparently had much more luck than previously known: The Australian Aviation Safety Authority has identified a major safety problem with the engines of some A380 aircraft - and now warns of a possible construction error.
The potential defect would have serious consequences: In the case of an oil hose connection, he could generate fatigue tears and leaks, it said in a statement published this Thursday by the agency ATSB.
This could have led to "a catastrophic engine failure."
Affected by Rolls-Royce engines in 20 aircraft of the A380 series are affected.
Among them is the Qantas-Airbus, which had to make emergency landings in Singapore on 4 November.
The Australian airline has now taken legal act against Britain's turbine maker.
She filed a substantiation with an Australian federal court in Sydney that would allow him to take further legal action against the company.
Qantas chief executive Alan Joyce said his airline will seek damages for the incident in early November.
In the fire of the engine, parts had loosened and damaged the wings.
Rolls-Royce accounted for an oil fire as the cause of the damage.
After the incident, up to 40 engines worldwide now have to be replaced.
Australian investigators announced they would carry out further safety tests on the superjumbos equipped with the affected engines.
The safety recommendation is the result of cooperation with Rolls-Royce, ATSB spokesman Martin Dolan said.
The safety problem was considered sufficiently important to inform all those affected.
After the Qantas plane's emergency landing, the airline had kept its A380 fleet on the ground for weeks.
Singapore Airlines temporarily left about three of its eleven A380s on the ground.
Lufthansa replaced the engine on an A380 as a precautionary measure.
The double-storey aviator is the world's largest passenger plane.
Nationwide sleek triggers traffic chaos
Germany all in white: Overnight snow has fallen almost all over the country.
There is chaos on many roads, trains and flights are cancelled.
At least three people were killed in car accidents.
Winter frosts Germany: In freezing temperatures, almost the whole republic is snowed on at night on Thursday - which leads to traffic chaos in many places.
In the north and to the east there are 15 centimeters thick sneaks.
On several highways and many roads, traffic is severely restricted.
On the A7 at Flensburg, the winter service was in continuous use, but was not finished with the constantly blowing snow.
In addition, violent gusts swept over the Baltic coast.
The Federal Maritime and Hydrography Office (BSH) expects storm surges on the Baltic coast of Schleswig-Holstein from the morning with water levels of 1.2 meters above the normal level.
In Saxony, snowdrifts on the highways and federal highways caused considerable problems.
According to the Meteomedia storm control center, therefore, alert level purple was required in Saxon Switzerland - the highest level ever.
In some cases 30 centimeters of fresh snow fell there, fresh and strong winds caused drifts.
Several trucks and cars got stuck there during the night.
Also in Saxony-Anhalt, the police authorities reported major disturbances on the streets.
On the A2, snow and wind led to stalling traffic and traffic jams.
In Thuringia, snowfall affected the A4 and A9.
According to Deutsche Bahn, there are considerable disturbances in Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia and northern Bavaria.
Snow on the tracks, fallen trees on the rails or destroyed overhead lines and turnouts are the main problems.
In Saxony, long-distance traffic from Leipzig to Nuremberg had to be stopped.
In Saalfeld, Thuringia, an ICE had to stop its journey to Munich.
The 165 passengers were provided by firefighters and rescue responders with wool blankets, food and drinks on the train.
Snowwinds swerved across the streets.
At major German airports, the snow winds lead to numerous flight cancellations.
According to a spokeswoman for the flight information center, some cancellations have already been reported in Munich.
40 flights have already been canceled in Frankfurt am Main, according to a Fraport spokeswoman.
Winter service is in continuous use.
Although there are only a few stranded passengers at the airport, there are still almost 400 camp beds available for them.
Accidents occurred on many roads on Wednesday evening.
In the evening, a truck driver died in the collision with a train on a level crossing in Ostholstein.
The engine driver was slightly injured.
There was a similar accident near Nuremberg due to snow-covered roads: Here, too, a truck was captured on a level crossing, because the vehicle got stuck.
The driver died.
Four passengers were injured on the train - the route from Bayreuth to Nuremberg was therefore closed until the morning hours.
In addition, a 30-year-old woman died after squeaking with her car at Aschaffenburg on a smooth road.
The meteorologists have good news: Although the snow will still be loose and pulpy and therefore swirl in light gusts.
But the wind will weaken.
Only the Baltic Sea is hit by fast-paced and storming winds.
However, it remains very cold - locally up to minus 15 degrees.
Deutsche Bank is looking for a buyer for their twin towers
Deutsche Bank wants to separate itself from its landmark: According to a newspaper report, Geldhaus is looking for buyers for the two office towers at its Frankfurt headquarters.
The property could contribute up to 500 million euros.
Deutsche Bank has just made its corporate headquarters pretty: the twin towers in Frankfurt's banking district have been modernized; meanwhile, bankers have been allowed to return to their old workplace.
But now the financial group apparently wants to recover the costs for the renovation - and is looking for a buyer for the two high-rise buildings, as the "Financial Times Deutschland" writes, citing a spokesman.
The spokesperson did not give reasons for the planned sale.
"We're always looking into whether it makes more sense to own or sell property itself and subsequently rent back," he said.
As a result, Germany’s largest credit institution will return its headquarters after a short time.
The bank had only taken over the double towers in the middle of 2007 for 272 million euros from the investors of an in-house fund and then extensively refurbished them.
The energy conversion devoured according to around 200 million euros.
A sale would probably bring a lot of money to the bank.
According to industry experts, the value of the towers, which have now been renamed "Greentowers", is estimated at around 500 million euros.
Whether the buildings will be sold again to a fund owned by the bank or to an independent investor is currently still open, the spokesman said.
Thousands mourn dead buddies
More than 10,000 people in New Zealand have thought of the spilled miners who were pronounced dead after several explosions at a mine.
The corpses could still not be recovered, because the security situation in the misfortune shaft is still precarious.
Almost two weeks ago, there was a violent explosion at the Pike River Mine near the city of Greymouth.
29 miners between the ages of 17 and 62 were spilled underground.
One probe had been lowered into the accident shaft, but had discovered no trace of survivors.
Due to the high concentration of toxic gases and other subsequent explosions, no rescue workers were able to descend for safety reasons.
After the second violent detonation, the authorities and experts had assumed that there was no longer a chance of survival for the buddies.
The corpses could not be recovered until now.
Now relatives and friends remembered the dead with a commemoration.
Tens of thousands of people had gathered in bright sunshine in Greymouth on New Zealand's South Island.
They were silent for two minutes.
In honor of the spilled miners, 29 tables were set up on which helmets, lamps and the names of the buddies could be seen.
Pictures and personal belongings, including garments and surfboards, also found their place here.
In front of the country's government buildings, the flags wept at half-mast.
New Zealand had hoped the miners "from the depths of the earth" would come back to the surface, New Zealand's Prime Minister John Key told attendees at the memorial service.
"But they've never come home."
He could not conclude with the death of his son as long as he had not seen his corpse, said Lawrie Drew, whose son Zen was spilled in the pit.
In addition to the prime minister and relatives of the victims, several foreign diplomats attended the commemoration ceremony.
In the first explosion on 19 November, miners of British and South African origin had also spilled.
Rescue workers began extinguishing a fire inside the coal mine on New Zealand's South Island.
As soon as the explosive gases are displaced, it will begin to cool down the glowing temperatures inside the mine, police chief Gary Knowles told journalists.
To hell, warriors!
Does splatters fit the art cinema?
Director Nicolas Winding Refn re-explores old genre borders with his fighting epic "Walhalla Rising."
Which audience may the Danish director Nicolas Winding Refn have thought of when he directed "Walhalla Rising"?
A captured Viking warrior with only one eye (Mads Mikkelsen) frees himself from his tormentors and kills his opponents with almost superhuman powers - sounds ideal for young male spectators in search of bloody action, precisely because our hero likes to use rabid killing methods and tears his enemies out of the living when he is in a bad mood.
On the other hand, Einauge, as he is actually called, does not say the whole movy, and even the other characters can be carried away to one or two sentences or a few agonizing screams of death.
Otherwise is kept silent and revelled in Scotland's spooky hilly landscape, which provides the backdrop to the Viking world from about a thousand years ago.
Ideal rather for adult cineasts with an interest in existentialism and very quiet camera rides.
It's nice if a film has several target groups, it's only a pity if they exclude each other.
Director Winding Refn has been considered a kind of Danish version of Quentin Tarantino since his wild and styled drug dealer trilogy "Pusher," but stoically refuses to make his works accessible to the mainstream or somewhat less brutality to the arthouse with a little more humour.
Most artical cinema friends are likely to be deterred by the splashing brain mass and guts.
The action fans die with boredom at the latest when, for freely interpretable reasons, an eye with a troupe of violent Christian missionaries sets off via ship to Jerusalem and an eternity is stuck in dawn and fog, before the crew apparently arrives in America and continues to remain silent there.
No wonder, then, that "Walhalla Rising" has not found a cinema rental in Germany and has now only appeared on DVD.
This is especially because of the magnificent pictures, but also because behind the pretentious to blood-dripping façade hides a sometimes exciting piece of cinema that deserves some attention.
This film is not about dialogues or a stringent plot, it's all atmosphere, it's a fever's dream.
And there's no escaping from that for the viewer.
Refn hardly grants him a moment of relaxation and constricts his throat with his icy images and the sinister trance-like soundtrack, but above all with the unbelievable hatred that beats him from the hearts of the characters.
Only a little blonde boy who stands faithfully aside is assisted something like innocence, everyone else is driven by wrath.
Most of than the one-eyed hero, whose motives are never explained, but who, for enemies and temporary allies at some point, becomes a warrior from hell, possibly an ambassador of the devil himself.
It means ruins, but perhaps only for those who are spoiled anyway.
"Walhalla Rising" is an enigmatic film that you can hate and admire for annoyance, excitement and amazement.
An experience.
Mysterious earthworks
The Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) has new indications of a secret nuclear program in Syria.
However, the country refuses to comment on this - it still allows checks.
Around midnight from September 5 to 6, 2007, at least four Israeli F-15 I warplanes attacked a mysterious, cube-shaped building in Syria.
It stands on the shores of the Euphrates; the nearest larger town, 30 kilometers away, is Dair as-Saur.
Its name appears in International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) documents when it comes to the reactor that Syria allegedly built in the cuboid with the help of North Korea, hidden in a valley that goes off the electricity.
According to a briefing by the U.S. foreign intelligence agency CIA from the end of April 2000, the reactor, which was also known under the name of the nearby village of el-Kibar, would have been well suited to produce plutonium - from which nuclear weapons could be built.
For other purposes, on the other hand, the technology from the fifties is hardly suitable.
The CIA presented a video presentation with blurred satellite images.
By computer animation, it blended in where which components of the reactor were supposed to have been before the destruction, which was a scaled-down copy of the North Korean model from the Yongbyon nuclear center.
In addition, she presented photos showing the building up close or even the interior.
The reactor was close to commissioning, it said, but had not yet been equipped with fuel rods.
Today, more than three years after the bombardment, little more is known than it was then, yes the puzzles became rather larger over time.
Former U.S. President George W. Bush recently revealed in his memoirs that the CIA was quite sure before the attack that Syria built a reactor in el-Kibar, but saw little evidence that there was "an active nuclear weapons program" in the country.
Some intelligence officials in Europe would rather be even more cautious and only speak of an "alleged reactor," even if they consider the CIA presentation "plausible."
They are lacking hard evidence, as is the IAEA.
Asked how safe he was to have a reactor ahead of him, a senior U.N. diplomat in the summer responded only lapidarily that the IAEA had "enough material to ask questions."
The debris of the alleged "untapped military building" was hurriedly set aside by the Syrians and thoroughly planned the site.
Nevertheless, in June 2008, an IAEA team under former Chief Inspector Olli Heinonen in el-Kibar found traces of uranium made by humans.
In addition, via a member state, the IAEA received satellite images of commercial providers from the period immediately after the attack, which are likely to be more meaningful than those presented by the CIA.
However, these recordings are unavailable on the market, because the unknown client - perhaps Israel - bought them exclusively.
The IAEA therefore cannot independently verify its authenticity.
Syria has always claimed to have never operated a secret nuclear program or built a reactor.
Uranium comes from Israeli weapons, Damascus said.
But the IAEA rejected this as improbable after analyzing the particles.
Damascus has since refused to discuss it - as well as satellite images.
In addition, it denies the inspectors a second inspection in el-Kibar and access to the debris of the bombed building.
Israel, too, remains silent.
"We will never comment on this," Ehud Olmert, who took office at the time of the attack, reaffirmed the official position.
For example, the investigation of the case has been dragging up inconclusively since June 2008 and "increasingly develops into a serious credibility problem for the authority," as a senior Western diplomat at the headquarters of the IAEA in Vienna admits - after all, there is a serious violation of the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, over which the IAEA monitors.
As a key to advances in enlightenment, the IAEA apparently sees three other facilities that are supposed to be "functionally related" to the suspected reactor - and contrary to what still exists.
So far, the inspectors have not disclosed which systems they are or what function they have or what makes them seem suspicious.
According to information from the Süddeutsche Zeitung, they are internally referred at the IAEA as Marj as-Sultan, Masyaf and Iskandariayah, again derived from nearby cities.
The independent Institute for Science and International Security in Washington (Isis) has identified these locations and located a building complex at Masyaf on satellite images, which is most likely one of the suspicious objects.
It may be located in or directly next to a military base - it is precisely with this justification that Syria denies the IAEA access to the three places.
In Vienna, it is said that the Syrians feared a fishing expedition - that the IAEA would be sent forward by the U.S. to spy on military installations.
Because the nuclear detectives had become aware of the facilities through a tip from the U.S. intelligence services.
The IAEA has indications, including satellite photos, that material has been transported back and forth between the three locations and the cuboid on the Euphrates.
A senior Western diplomat in Vienna said at least one of the plants could be "related to fuel production."
However, there is "no evidence of sensitive activities", i.e. uranium enrichment or reprocessing; processes that are necessary for the construction of nuclear weapons.
The reactor type is operated with uranium, which is not enriched.
But where the uranium should have come from and where the plutonium for the bomb was to be severed later, there are plausible assumptions about these central questions.
It is also unclear whether debris, which could contain valuable traces, was stored in the three locations.
As if the hundreds of truckloads had disappeared from the ground, no one wants to have information about where the rubble has remained.
Located just a few kilometres from el-Kibar on the other side of the Euphrates, the al-Tibni salt mine, which examined Syria in 1998 for its suitability as a warehouse for radioactive waste, is considered unlikely by the IAEA.
Former IAEA Inspector Robert Kelley had recently linked them to the reactor in an analysis of satellite imagery for the renowned Jane's Intelligence Review magazine.
Footages from orbit, however, show that Syria did earthworks in the three suspicious locations and removed large containers after the IAEA demanded access.
This can be a coincidence, but has hardly helped remove the suspicion.
In addition, diplomat circles say that the IAEA spent several months developing a plan for inspections there that should allow Syria to maintain possible military secrets - only the hassle shown how great the interest of the inspectors is.
Damascus, meanwhile, also ironed out that proposal.
Western diplomats in Vienna had appeased a year ago that the reactor was destroyed, so the matter has no high urgency, for example U.S. Ambassador Glyn Davies has been bringing so-called special inspections into play for a few months, one of the sharpest funds that the Vienna nuclear authority has to enforce controls.
IAEA CEO Yukiya Amano, who would have to propose these inspections, has already spoken of "an option for which I am open in the future," but he is still hesitant.
There is a high risk in this way: On the one hand, a majority in the Board of Governors of the IAEA is needed, and some of the 35 countries represented there are anything but eager for escalation.
On the other hand, as a diplomat points out: "What if in the end they find nothing?"
Love in times of war
Since Angelina Jolie started working on her nameless directorial debut in the summer, there has been trouble.
She has written the script herself, a love story between a woman from Bosnia and a Serbian man.
You don't know much more about it - but that's enough for restlessness.
Persistent is the rumor, the woman, a Muslim woman, falling in love with her Serbian rapist during the Yugoslavian war.
Bosnian women's federations immediately protested, whereupon some days of filminging planned in Sarajevo were moved to Hungary at short notice.
Meanwhile, the film is shot in Hungary and in post-production, but it is still being protested in Bosnia.
Bakira Hasecic from the organization of female war victims wrote about the U.N. refugee agency UNHCR to Jolie and demanded a meeting with the actress, has reported a Bosnian newspaper now - Jolie is herself an ambassador of the U.N. refugee agency.
She isn't diplomatic - she's offered the women a meeting in Hungary weeks ago, but they refused.
The meeting must take place in Bosnia, Hasecic said.
There were no love stories in rape camps, and Jolie’s attitude towards the victims tells her enough about the script.
Her organization has, according to the U.N. Refugee Agency, recommended that Jolie be sacked as an ambassador, the Guardian reports.
But what exactly did Jolie write for scenes?
The Bosnian Ministry of Culture, which got its script to read, granted a filming permit for Sarajevo after some back-and-forth - it's not entirely clear why Jolie didn't come anyway.
The young author herself asserts that she pursues no political intentions with her film, has worked her way into the Yugoslav conflict with a lot of effort, wants to show the terrible consequences of the war and give a voice to all sides involved.
However, it remains vague in terms of content.
So another synopsis circulates to the film on the net, which sounds quite different: The lovers knew each other in it even before the outbreak of the war and meet again when she, a Bosnian Muslim woman, is deported to a Serbian camp, where he works as a guard.
Which sounds considerably different than the variant "rape Victim has a crush on Torturknecht".
That Jolie's film actually starts even before the war - that's for sure.
The pressure on them, according to Jolie, is "unfair and based on misinformation."
Now only the hackers of Hollywood can give her peace: by putting the script, like so many before, illegally on the net.
Nothing like getting out of here
This evening, it’s finally really round for the Irish.
No sign of sadness.
But the home team dominates its guests from Sweden, Finland and the USA too much.
The fans yell with joy with loud music, they stomping with their feet.
The Bailey pub quakes in southern Ireland's Cork because the players are throwing table tennis balls over a three-metre distance into beer cups - and scoring.
"Beer pong" is the name of the bizarre spectacle, there is 500 euros victory premium plus plane tickets to Las Vegas for the beer pong world championships in January.
Cian Tanner is a quiet guy; this shrill drinking game and the restrained computer scientist doesn't really fit together.
And yet the 26-year-old is trying to amuse himself in distracting himself from the drilling question of what the future will bring.
On his plan isn't gaming fun in Las Vegas, he'll hold job interviews in New Zealand in January.
"I've got a queasy feeling about it," he says.
"It's so far away from home and I don't have any work there yet."
After all: His girlfriend is already waiting for him in Auckland - "she had jerk, zuck had her desired job as event manager, maybe a good sign," says the young man and smiles.
Due to poor prospects at home, two of Tanner's three siblings have already sought their luck abroad.
His sister Yvonne, a social worker, is in Sydney, and his brother Colin now has a job as an IT consultant in London.
The boys are leaving: As with the Tanners, it looks on the island in many families.
10,000,000 Irish will probably leave the country in the near future, experts at the economic and social research institute ESRI in Dublin predict.
By 2015, they even expect 200,000 emigrants.
For the small country with only 4.5 million inhabitants a hefty loss.
If the forecasts are true, an emigration wave is comparable to the one which Ireland suffered in the 1980s.
After a decade of upswing, which brought great prosperity for the Irians, drearynessis is now spreading again.
The economic crisis unsettles especially the young, well-educated Irish, for whom until now it was only uphill.
Job loss and social descent seemed absurd in the boom years.
Everyone was needed, there was money in abundance.
In the meantime, however, three out of ten women and men under the age of thirty
In the Irish Times, there are a lot of reader letters from young emigrants who complain: "Everything goes down in Ireland."
"There should be an uprising against politicians and bankers - but who can, goes away and our country loses an entire generation."
"The last one then please turns off the light."
Law student Leanne Caulfield, president of the Irish Student Union, sees a bleak futurene emerging because the government only engages in eye-wiping and no longer offers young people future prospects.
"They're reducing minimum wages, but they don't have a strategy on how to create new jobs," the 20-year-old says.
She observes how her fellow students hang themselves from one unpaid internship to the next.
Her biggest concern, she says, is to have to emigrate herself in four years.
"This thought is sad and depressing."
Men like Stephen McLarnon are trying to fill the issue positively.
McLarnon organizes the "WorkingAbroadExpo", a trade fair that offers an all-round service on the topic of working abroad.
"For beginners, working abroad is a great opportunity to take the first steps on the career ladder," says the manager.
On its website, photos show surfers in Australia and snowboarders in Canada.
The message is clear: working abroad is not a galley service, but also brings freedom and fun.
Seán O'Donovan has known for half a year.
Because there was nothing to do for the 29-year-old engineer in Ireland, he is now working with 15 Irish counterparts on construction in Germany.
In Gelsenkirchen, they're pulling up a fashion house right now.
In January we will continue to Hanover or Berli, the next project.
O'Donovan rarely sees his homeland in the meantime, because he makes extra shifts in order to be able to pay off the loan for his house at home.
"The situation is already a bit crazy," he says as he steps over the threshold of his house for the first time after a long time.
"I earn money in Germany for a house in Ireland where I'm almost never again."
He laughs a bit embarrassedly and walks through chilled rooms.
The small house stands in a fine residential area with well-kept front gardens, in the driveways large, expensive cars are parked.
Together with a sister, O'Donovan has bought the house for four years ago for 380,000 euros - 100 percent credit-financed.
"It's been crazy times, there's been simple money without a lot of questions," he says.
Is he afraid it might go wrong with the loan?
O'Donovan ponders for a while, pours hot tea and says, "I don't have children that I have to feed through and I'm young; even if everything goes wrong, I can start again."
The civil engineer does not like the lamentation that he often hears at the moment.
"No one has to go hungry - our situation is far from as dramatic as in earlier times here on the island," he says.
"Working abroad for a while isn't the worst thing that can happen - maybe we'll only feel like Irish."
He laughs and then talks about his father, who went to England in the last major economic crisis.
"After seven years he was back and life went on in Ireland, quite simply."
Cian Tanner likes this thought.
Leaving the future open and just looking at how it goes with life elsewhere.
He’s been working in London for a few years and why shouldn’t things be good for him and his girlfriend in New Zealand?
But there is still one thing that worries him these days: the celebration of his 27th birthday on Friday in Dublin.
He's afraid it could degenerate into a farewell party.
"Just not too many emotions," he says, drinking a big sip of his beer.
"Farewell, that sounds scary."
"Let's call it a walking party, quite loose, with a few friends, a few drinks, a few games."
The Democatric Routine
The democracy movement in Latin America is getting stronger, just as the support of private companies.
Crime is a bigger problem than unemployment.
In many parts of the region, Brazil's influence is greater than that of the United
These were followed by the Latinobarómetro survey, which was conducted in 18 countries and published exclusively in The Economist.
This survey has been conducted regularly since 1995 and thus shows the change of opinion in this region.
Two facts are particularly eye-catching in this year's September and October poll.
Firstly, the good mood in Latin America
and secondly, the growing interest in democracy and its most important institutions.
In numerous countries on the South American Pacific coast, the democratic movement has increased sharply (see Table 1).
In Peru, for example, where the economy has been growing by about 6% annually since 2002, the number of pro-democracy advocates rose from 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
Also in Mexico, where the economy has been recovering since the sharp slump last year, such a trend can be seen.
Yet only half respondents in Mexico and Brazil, the region's most populous countries, are staunched Democrats, lowering the regional average (see chart 2).
Approximately 44% of respondents said they are satisfied with their country’s democratic practice. That’s as many as last year, however, 25% more than in 2001 (Diagram 3).
About 34% trust their country’s congress and 45% of their government. Both values correspond to those of the last year, but show a significant increase compared to 2003 (17% and 24%, respectively).
A decade ago Latina's economic crises undermined efforts for more democracy.
That this continued even during the Great Depression may be due to the fact that the countries of Latin America experienced only a short period of recession and quickly recovered.
In addition, the poor were absorbed by a stronger social network.
(According to reports by the U.N. Economic Commission for Latin America from this week, the poverty rate in Latin America increased only marginally in 2009 and fell again by 32% this year.)
Yet there are strong mood differences from country to country (see chart 4).
Brazil's strong economy and the popularity of outgoing President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva is reflected in widespread progress thinking.
In Mexico and Central America, where the recession was more noticeable and violent crimes have increased, this is less the case.
Only for the second time since the start of the polling, many respondents in Latin America see crime in their country as a bigger problem than in unemployment (see chart 5).
Approximately 31% say that either they themselves or a family member were the victims of a crime in the last year. However, compared to 38% in the last year, values have declined, thus reaching the lowest level since 1995.
Rising economic output is reflected in the stronger support of the private and market economy: about 71% of the respondents said that the private sector is indispensable for development, compared to 56% in 2007 (see chart 6).
Approximately 58% explained that only the market economy can bring progress to their land, compared with 47% last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela have retained this view (and 80% are in favour of a private sector), even though their left-wing president, Hugo Chávez, nationalizes many private companies.
Brazil has the largest weight in the region, according to 19% of respondents (18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, unchanged since last year) and Venezuela (9% compared with 11% last year).
Respondents in Mexico and most countries in Central America, on the other hand, see the most influential country in the United States, taking on this role in the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua from Venezuela.
An election slouch
No one had expected the November 28 elections in Haiti to be perfect.
But the facts far surpassed the fears of the election workers rushing to help the battered country.
Overcrowded ballot boxes, votes cast several times, ballot slips in the Rinnstein, destroyed polling stations and intimidation were only part of the problems.
Polling stations opened with an hour-long delay.
Many Haitians, many of whom had become homeless as a result of the earthquake in January, roamed around in search of a choice.
Some eventually gave up altogether.
A dozen presidential candidates, including some suspected front-runners, demanded the lifting of election results because of alleged fraud.
There were partly violent protests across the country.
Still, Edmond Mulet, the head of the U.N. mission in Haiti, described the election "as quite good in many respects."
According to a prudent statement by observers from the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community, "these irregularities, however serious, do not necessarily invalidate the process."
In other words, the show goes on.
Electoral authority, which is supposedly committed to unpopular outgoing President René Préval and his nominee Jude Célestin, will announce the preliminary results on December 7.
The expected run-off election is scheduled for January.
There's still hope in the election.
The two leading opposition candidates, rap musician Michel Martelly and former first-lady Mirlande Manigat, have withdrawn their initial invalidance lawsuit.
If Jude Célestin, who finds little or no approval among the voters at all, declares the winner of the run-off election from the election authorities, this could lead to problems.
Due to the cholera epidemic and the fact that more than 1 million of earthquake victims still live in tents, a low turnout has been feared.
On election day, many Haitians were hindered by a lack of organisation.
Shortly before the start of the election, about 250,000 ID cards, which are needed for the election, had not yet been distributed.
There's a lot at stake.
Donor countries regard a legitimate, recognised government as an important step in Haiti's reconstruction.
The Haitians themselves are of the same opinion.
Secretly to the middle
In the weeks after her husband's sudden death, Argentina's president, Cristina Fernández, experienced a wave of sympathy that drove her popularity rate up 20 points to over 50%. That's the highest level since her election in 2007.
However, whether this will help her re-election next year depends on whether the visibly grieving Cristina Fernández is more willing to compromise than her late husband, her predecessor and political partner Néstor Kirchner.
The signs of this are inconclusive.
Néstor Kirchner, who steered economic policy even after his wife's election, often drew up budgets that did not take full account of tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the presidential couple to spend the extra revenue at their own discretion.
On paper, the Fernández administration lost a majority in Congress last year.
But the opposition is divided and Kirchner's death after a heart attack on October 27 led to further quarrels.
Some regime critics from the ranks of the ruling Peronists stood behind Cristina Fernández.
Yet after chaotic sessions (at which one parliamentarian slapped another in the face and others accused the government of bribery), Congress failed to pass the 2011 budget.
"I won't be discussing it," the president said.
The 2010 budget now also applies to the next year, which gives the president even more opportunity to spend money at her own discretion.
The economy is running hot: it is likely to grow by up to 9% this year.
The official rate of inflation currently stands at 11.1%, but numerous private estimates - and wage demands of the unions - drive the value to almost 30%.
Kirchner, who took office in 2030 when Argentina was shaken by economic crises, declared growth and poverty alleviation to its main targets, rather than inflation reduction.
He refused to cooperate with the IMF, harassed the private sector and purged the INDEC National Institute of Statistics and Censuses, which then highlighted the official inflation statistics.
Can Fernández take a more moderate path now?
It has called on the private sector and union leaders for talks.
And according to official statements, wage increases are to be limited to 18% next year.
The high world market prices for Argentina's agricultural products have given the economy a strong boost.
But inflation undermined the growth spurt through a major devaluation in 2002.
Cristina Fernández may want to rejoin the rest of the world financially.
Earlier this year, the government entered into an agreement with securities owners who had rejected the country's earlier rescheduling in 2001.
Now Fernández appears to be pushing for an agreement with the funders from the Paris club, to whom she owes about $6.7bn.
The Kirchners had always rejected a review by the IMF, as is customary elsewhere.
However, last month, Economy Minister Amado Boudou announced that the fund will provide "technical support" in creating a new national consumer price index. It looks like this is the first step to purge the INDEC.
It is not known whether Fernández will be politically active even without her husband's support, even without her husband's termination.
If yes, it needs the support of the middle class, which for the most has had enough of Kirchnerism.
If Argentines return from their summer vacation in March, the wave of sympathy that has been shown to her will have ebbed away.
If she wants to win another election, she has to decide if she quietlyny overturns many of her husband's political decisions.
Weak applause for China
Taiwan's voters are slowly approaching a closer connection to the mainland.
What does Taiwan think of its big brother beyond the strait?
On November 27, more than 7.5 million voters, that's 70% of all eligible voters, set out to elect mayors and city councillors in the five city districts. This is seen as a broad assessment by President Ma Ying-jeou.
By establishing groundbreaking business relationships, including a partial free trade agreement, Ma himself is a symbol figure for relations with the mainland. His policy has permanently changed the relationship with the Chinese motherland for the first time since 1949.
Ma has passed the exam, but only narrowly.
Although his Kuomintang party (KMT) represents three of the five mayors, it did not receive a majority of votes.
Instead, the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), which advocates greater Taiwan's independence, received nearly 50% of the vote, the KMT received 45%.
In a presidential election, this would have meant problems for Ma.
Since his election two years ago, his party's share of votes has fallen by about 8%.
For the first time, the DPP even moved into the city councils together with the KMT.
During the election it came to the usual incidents in Taiwan such as truck convoys with riot makers, flag-waving mass parades and only sparsely clothed dancers.
There was even the obligatory, mysterious assassination attempt.
Sean Lien, the son of Lien Chan and honorary chairman of the KMT, gave a speech on a KMT candidate on the eve of the election,
when a man from the crowd shot in his face. Lien was wounded and a bystander was killed.
Bizarrely, it's not clear if the shooter wanted to kill Lien or settle a dispute with the candidate.
Separately, according to Tsai Ing-wen, the leader of the DPP, the attack affected the election and put the DPP in a bad light, which has given the KMT more votes.
China may have been the biggest winner of the campaign.
Remarkably, relations with the mainland have not been addressed.
Instead, the candidates debated plans on social housing and job creation.
The Taiwaneses love their de factual independence and oppose China's military threat.
For this reason, the KMT does not like to focus on its relations with the government in Beijing.
But this time the DPP also avoided stoking the anti-Chinese feelings too much.
She responded to the new awareness that Taiwan's economy cannot survive without China.
For its part, he would like to confirm that the majority of Taiwanese are in favour of his China policies.
The government in Beijing will continue to pursue its strategy of luring Taiwan back to the community with economic incentives.
China immediately sent a negotiator to Taipei to prepare a summit on investment security and medical cooperation.
Surely it also wants political integration, but the most important thing first.
It prefers to wait until after the presidential elections in 2012.
The DPP has since reinvented itself.
Two years ago, she was severely weakened: former party leader Chen Shui-bian was in prison for corruption.
During his presidency from 2000 to 2008, he vehemently advocated absolute independence.
But under Tai's regency, a new generation of moderate politicians developed and the DPP fights for the votes of professionals and youth.
Tsai found great endorsement as presidential candidate of the DPP, such as Su Tseng-chang under Chen.
Her party is now doing everything it can to show that she, too, is capable of building close ties with Beijing.
Tsai is planning a think tank as a point of contact between the DPP and China. However, this presupposes prejudice-free discussions.
Tsai publicly stated that her party offers a "trustworthy alternative" to Ma's rash approach to China.
Chen seems very upset about this change.
From his cell, he declared that the candidates of the DPP had done better to insist on Taiwan's independence.
He even urged pragmatist Su to forego the presidential candidacy.
Even without Chen's interference, the DPP struggles to choose its possible candidate.
KMT will have to undergo its own self-analysis.
The values are bad, despite the rapid economic growth; the KMT candidates wanted to run their election campaign without him.
The main reasons for his unpopularity seem to be unemployment and the ever-increasing scissors between rich and poor.
According to a KMT spokesperson, he will redesign his cabinet as well as the local facilities.
The DPP's strong demeanor suggests that Taiwan advocates reapproximation only to a limited extent.
At the same time, most Taiwaneses see economic cooperation with China as inevitable.
According to Antonio Chiang, a prominent columnist, "there's already no turning back."
Grammy nominations: Jazz singer Esperanza Spalding was surprisingly honored in the Newcomer of the Year category
If you perform at the request of a president, people will notice.
President Obama himself asked Esperanza Spalding to perform at the ceremony on the occasion of the 2009 Nobel Award Ceremony. Now the 26-year-old jazz bassist and singer has been surprisingly nominated for this year’s Grammy in the Newcomer of the Year category.
Given that Spalding has already been an acclaimed star in the jazz scene since her 2008 album "Esperanza," the nomination appears a little uncommon. Yet she equates to the Grammy rules, according to which an artist is allowed to have released at most three albums prior to the release of the album, with which he became known to the general public.
Her ambitious 2010 album, "Chamber Music Society," is certainly a step forward. And with her exhilarating voice and a good flair for pop, funk and Latin music, you can quietly compare Spalding with Norah Jones 2003, whose great success comes closest in 2003 as a jazz performer in this category of Spalding's nomination.
Furthermore, the album "Historicity" of the Vijay Iyer Trio was nominated in the category instrumental albums. This favorite of the critics experienced a huge success.
Competitors Iyers include "Moody 4B" by veteran saxophonist James Moody and "Positootly" by keyboardist John Beasley.
Trombone Shorty, a robbery favorite from New Orleans and frequent guest in HBO's "Treme," has been recognized in the Contemporary Jazz category, which is otherwise more likely to feature albums by Stanley Clarke, John McLoughlin and Jeff Lorber.
The Secret Societ by wild steampunk musician Darcy James Argue received well-deserved attention in the large-group category. He thus competes against the Billy Childs Emsemble with his chamber jazz and the adventurous Dave Holland and his octet, whose life-ablum "Pathways" is a good example for a bassist as a Democratic bandleader.
Grammy nominations: Michael Daugherty, Steve Mackey lead in the classic categories
The classical nominations for the 53rd Grammy Award include a colorful mix of musicians and composers from all over the world.
Michael Daugherty's "Deux Ex Machina," for piano and orchestra with five voices, and Steve Mackey's "Dreamhouse," an eclectic orchestral work, with three voices, is headed.
Among the nominees with connections to Southern California is Estonian composer Arvo Pärt with his symphony No. 4 "Los Angeles." This piece was commissioned by the Los Angeles Philharmonics and premiered at Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
L.A. based composer Avner Dorman has also been nominated for his mandolin concerto.
Classical nominations are divided into 13 categories, from orchestra, opera, choir to solo recordings.
In the category of classical music producers, five artists were nominated this year - Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
Among the nominated classic albums are Bruckner's Symphonies No. 3 and 4 by the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; Daugherty's "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" played by the Nashville Symphony Orchestra; Steven Mackey's "Dreamhouse" by the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castrati" performed by various artists;
In the operatic category were Berg's "Lulu of the Orchestra of the Royal Opera House; Adolf Hasse's "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho's "L'Amour de Loin" by the German Symphony Orchestra Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin's "The Enchanted Wanderer" by the orchestra and chorus
Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina", Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionamente Plus", Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer" were nominated for Grammy.
Pärt's Symphony No. 4 premiered and recorded on January 4 at Disney Hall by the L.A. Philharmoniker led by Esa-Pekka Salonen.
The approximately 30-minute piece is titled "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to former oil magnate Mikhail Khodorkovsky, who is in prison in Russia.
Dorman's "Mondoline Concerto" received a nomination in the instrument solo category with orchestra.
The composer from L.A. stated that he had written the 15-minute piece especially for the mandolin artist Avi Avital, who performed the concert with the New-Yorker Metropolis Ensemble.
"I've used sounds from the Middle East and numerous North African tones and rhythms," Dorman explained in a telephone interview.
Conductor Pierre Boulez, 85, received a nomination in the orchestraling category for a recording of Stravinski's works with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra under his direction.
As instrumental soloists, the pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin, the organist Paul Jacobs, the violinist Julia Fischer and the harfinist Sarah Schuster Ericsson were nominated.
Only women were nominated as vocalsolists this year, including Anne Sofie from Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchonová, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the classical crossover category, soprano Jessye Norman was nominated for her recording "Roots - My Life, My Song."
Mosque in Temecula wins planning committee approval
The construction of a mosque in Temecula, after a five-hour, often heated public hearing, found the unanimous commitment of the local planning committee.
Critics accused the project of promoting both terrorism and traffic congestion.
But according to a statement from the commission on Wednesday evening, the new religious center complies with the local regulations and will become an aesthetic eye-catcher.
"The project is supported by a lot of good people - by Christians, Jews, Bahais, and many others," Hadi Nael, chairman of Temecula Valley's Islamic Centre, declared on Thursday morning.
"As an American, that makes me proud."
"It shows that justice prevails."
"There are few opponents," he added.
"They're just very noisy."
“There are wonderful people living in this valley.”
The session began at 18:00 and the discussions for and against the construction of the center lasted almost until midnight, with the voices on both sides distributed fairly fairly evenly, according to Press-Enterprise.
The debate continued on the newspaper's website.
"There's no America anymore," was one comment.
"It's a different planet of the apes, and the Statue of Liberty lies dead in the sand."
"America is finished."
Planned Islamic Centers have also led to protests in other parts of the country, e.g. near Ground Zero, the scene of the terrorist attacks on the City of New York on September 11, 2001.
In the Temecula Valley, Imam Mahmoud Harmoush has called for nonviolence.
His community of about 135 families has lived peacefully in Riverside County for years. But they wanted a bigger and better place than the camp they rented together with a manufacturer of water filtration systems.
The new, about 2,300 m2 mosque in the Mediterranean style should have two minarets and become about 13m high.
It is to be built on the approximately 17,500 m2 plot on Nicolas Road near Chaparral High School.
Opponents can still object to the projection at the city council.
Arsenic-resistant microbe could enable new life forms on Earth or elsewhere
The discovery of an alien species of bacteria, which can live on arsenic, among others, opens up new possibilities for new forms of life on Earth and elsewhere.
Although scientists discovered this unusual bacterium here on Earth, in their opinion it is a sign that a life is possible even without the elements previously considered essential for life.
"This organism has two options."
"He needs either phosphorus or arsenic to grow."
"This makes it very special, even though it's almost a kind of 'foreign' way of life," explained Paul C.W. Davies of Arizona State University and co-author of the report, which appears Thursday in the online issue of the journal Science.
So far, six main elements have been considered vital - carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulphur.
But as the scientists found out, the bacterium grows even further if it replaces phosphorus with arsenic.
"That's when one wonders what else is possible," said co-author Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University.
Discovery is an important step in the search for extraterrestrial life, as scientists need to recognize life and its forms, according to Anbar.
The study deals with microbes found on Earth.
Announcing a press confession in which no details of the discovery were disclosed gave rise to widespread speculation on the internet that the report could be about the discovery of alien life.
That's not the case.
"The discovery shows that in other planetary systems organisms, with the help of other elements, can initiate biochemical processes and that the 'standard elements', which in our opinion are absolutely vital, are not necessarily firmly predetermined," explained Charles Cockell, a professor at the Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute of the Open University of Milton Keynes in the United Kingdom.
Cockel wasn't part of the research team.
"This work is a novelty, because it shows that in basic biochemical processes and structures one element can be replaced by another," Cockell added.
This discovery wasn't coincidental.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA Astrobiological Institute, who led the study, chose Mono Lake due to high arsenic levels.
Because arsenic and phosphorus are chemically the same, it assumed that microbes exposed to both could potentially replace one element with the other.
"The toxicity of arsenic relies primarily on the fact that it behaves chemically like phosphorus."
"As a result, organisms find it difficult to distinguish one element from another."
"But the difference is so great that arsenic acts differently than phosphorus. It enters our biochemical system, where it smeares virtually everything," explains Anbar.
The researchers suspended the bacterium GFAJ-1 of rising arsenic concentration. It adapted and grew.
The microbe grows better in phosphorus, but that it can also survive in arsenic shows that an arsenic-based life form can occur naturally, either on Earth or on another planet or moon where arsenic is more common.
According to Jamie S. Foster, lecturer in microbiology at the University of Florida, the idea that phosphorus can be replaced by arsenic isn’t new. But so far there hasn’t been a working example yet.
Arsenic occurred more frequently in the early Earth years, she says, that's why science has assumed that early life forms used it.
"This gives reason to believe that life may also have developed differently than in the early Earth age," said Forster, who was not a member of Wolfe-Simon's research team.
The research work has been backed by NASA, the Department of Energy and the National Health Authorities.
Other stolen exhibition dogs found in Compton
The remaining two of the four exhibition dogs stolen in front of a Bellflower motel were found Thursday, according to the Los Angeles County Sheriff.
The four exhibition dogs - two Akitas and two Pembroke Welsh Corgis - disappeared on Tuesday when unknowns stole the 2006 Chevrolet Express-Van, where the animals were housed outside a Bellflower Motel 6 at night, according to authorities statements.
Two of the dogs were spotted on Wednesday afternoon, and the last two on Wednesday evening, explains Steve Whitmore, a spokesman for the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department.
"The good news is that the dogs are well up," Whitmore said.
"They're in good condition."
"They were returned to their rightful owners."
Whitmore did not provide precise information on the location of the dogs, nor about the circumstances that led to their discovery.
"The investigation is still ongoing."
"We don't want to say more about that," Whitmore explains.
"We're chasing some leads."
Dog owners, Kristina Rickard and David Peek, were not available for further information on Thursday.
They received their two dogs on Wednesday afternoon at a distributor shelter in Downey.
Representatives of the sheriff found the van and the two dogs at different locations in Compton.
Akitas - Trace, a 3-year-old male, and Didi, a 10-month-old bitch - were unharmed, according to authorities.
Yet the Corgis Bunny and Peter, whom Rickard and Peek planned to introduce this week on behalf of their Japanese owners at the competition, remained missing until Wednesday night.
Peek and Rickard, who live in Redding, were in town this weekend because of the AKC/Eukanuba National Championship in Long Beach because Trace was supposed to take part in the contest.
As Peek told the Times on Wednesday, the other dogs were supposed to participate in other competitions on the days leading up to the AKC/Eukanuba show.
Southern California is a stop on the couple's nearly month-long tour from dog show to dog show.
On Monday evening, Peek and Rickard checked into the motel.
They often stay in motels and park their van in front of their room windows.
They took three other dogs - a boxer puppy, a Parson Russell Terrier and a Brittany - into their motel room.
The fat-skinned Akitas and Corgis stayed in the van because it was cooler there.
They left the window a gap wide open, so the dogs had fresh air.
Peek and Rickard reported their cars as stolen at 6:00 a.m. on Tuesday.
Peek promised anyone who could give an indication of the corgis a finder's wage. The animals were implanted microchippes for identification.
On Thursday, it was not yet clear whether a finder's wage would be paid.
The lion roars again after court approval of the MGM restructuring
After 18 months of turbulence and uncertainties, MGM finally got the green light for a fresh start.
A New-York bankruptcy judge on Thursday officially blessed the "prepared" gliberation plan to repay $$5bn debt and pave the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company for new movies and television shows.
According to Thursday's statements, the plan meets the requirements of U.S. bankruptcy law, so that nothing stands in the way of the restart of MGM under the direction of Gary Barber and Roger Birnbaum, the founders of Spyglass Entertainment. Barber and Birnbaum will preside over the somewhat slimmed-down studio as chief executive.
The number of employees will be reduced from over 400 to about 320.
For MGM, Chapter 11 doesn't actually come into effect until JPMorgan Chase has completed a planned $500 million bond to finance the new MGM productions.
At the moment, a consortium of the investment bank, which itself contributes $75 million, is managing the company. The financing is to stand by mid-December.
The studio's restructuring began in May 2009 with the contract to investment bank Moelis & Co, the company's management, which struggled with $300 million/year interest payments to help solve its financial problems.
In August, managing director Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former CEO at Enron and restructuring experiencer who became known through his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Efforts by Moelis & Cooper to resolve MGM's financial concerns and pay out the studio's more than 140 faults took longer than expected.
After months of discussions, the parties decided at the end of 2009 to offer the company for sale.
But the auction attracted few bidders and none wanted to pay the $$2 billion demanded by the creditors.
In the spring of this year, it all started all over again when the MGM team began the restructuring plan, which was finally signed by creditors at the end of October after months of debates and internal disputes.
If MGM has officially weathered bankruptcy in the next few weeks, Moelis and Cooper will receive a multi-million-dollar bonus for their work.
The share for Moelis & Co., which has already received $1.2 million, amounts to $9.5 million.
Cooper, who was paid $375,000 a month for managing the company, will receive a "success bonus" of $4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum are planning to resume production at MGM. This year, the studio released only one film and created an annual plan with seven or eight films that are scheduled to hit theaters in early 2012.
In addition, they are planning two films after "The Little Hobbit" in co-production with Warner Bros.
The studio needs a separate bond between $265 million to $275 million for its financial share of these two films, which are to be created under the direction of Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who funded about 18% of the MGM loan, attempted a merger of the studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, whose largest shareholder he is.
But the last negotiations between the two companies led to no result.
Tech Toys keeps your gift budget in balance
This year's favorite among the gifts is the iPad, possibly followed by a Microsoft Kinect video console. Both gimmicks can tear a sizeable hole in your gift budget.
An iPad costs as much as five BMX bikes for $99, while the price for a Kinect game is equivalent to about 10 classic skipping ropes or about 48 cans of Play-Doh.
But you can save your gift budget.
Recently, looking for three electronic toy toys under $80, I visited three national toy merchants - a Wal-Mart, a Toys "R" Us and a BJ's Wholesale Club.
Immediately I realized that Moore’s law had come into force. This year, the technology toys are more powerful and at the same time cost slightly less than last year.
And although fewer new toys appear with their own website, in the best-knowned of its kind, Webkinz and Club Penguin, there is a broader offer.
There are more and more button cell-powered toys on the market, and many contain rechargeable batteries.
Here are some recommendations.
(Unless otherwise stated, are all prices of Toys "R" Us.)
Sing-a-ma-jigs (Mattel, $13, for children from 3 years) are plush figures as big as guinea pigs, who sing a note when pressed on their belly.
Once pressing corresponds to a note, by pressing several times you can play a song.
Several Sing-a-ma-jigs sing in harmony.
Attention: Once you have started pressing, you cannot stop.
For a combination of sentience and dance, dance star Mickey (Fisher-Price $60, for children from 2) is the best choice.
Based on Elmo live technology developed last year, the animatronic doll offers interplays such as 'Simon says', and thanks to the novel hinge feet with wheel blocking, it dominates the moon walk.
Children who tested the device use Mickey as dance teachers, but their parents complained about the lack of volume control.
If you're a child of the '80s, you're certainly reminiscent of Alphie, one of the first computerized educational toys.
Now he's back (Hasbro/Playskool, $40, for children from 3), with a whole bunch of question cards with which a car ride can become a lesson, with a know-it-all with autocorrect function as a teacher.
If you don’t want to entrust your smartphone to your child, here are three independent alternatives.
MobiGo by VTech ($60 for children from 3 to 8) is the cheapest and most user-friendly solution, equipped with a qwerty keyboard. However, the games contained on it aren’t as instructive as those on Leapster Explorer ($70, for children from 3 to 12), equipped with a new, brighter screen after Leapfrog’s first major overhaul of the Leapster series.
None of the versions is compatible with older cassettes.
iXL by Fisher-Price ($90 for children from 3 to 7) uses a system similar to iTunes for the games and eBooks, and thanks to the SD card drive family photos can also be viewed on it.
Last but not least, there is Scrabble Flash ($30 by Hasbro, for children from 8). The five blocks are no bigger than a matchbox and have their own LED screen.
The game is all about putting the blocks together into words before time runs out. It’s great for an impromptu spelling contest.
More and more toys have some kind of online connection.
If you go alone according to the offer in the toy stores, Webkinz and Club Penguin are the most important.
For example, in order to give your child an online animal at Club Penguin, you can buy a Disney Club Penguin 2" Mix 'N match figure package ($$5 for children from 6 to 12), which contains a coin with a secret code.
If you enter the code online, your child will receive enough credits for two more parts in the Club Penguin Store.
These can be roller skates or a puffle stuffed animal.
The newest head cell-powered microrobot, Hexbug Nano ($10 for children from 5) is no bigger than your toothbrush's head.
The beetle moves quickly on 12 vibrating silicone legs in any direction.
It's fun sending him through a maze out of blocks.
And he's enduring a lot.
Our fell from the kitchen table and even withstood for some time in our dog's mouth.
Police deployments in China get softer
Like a training officer, Tang Shenbin walked over an urban square, checking his nervous subordinates and issuing orders with military authority and subdued voice.
He wanted his female members of the Chengguan - China's robust guardians of the urban order, feared and despised for their arbitrary raids and their penchant for violence - impress spectators.
"Stand right now!"
"Look straight ahead!"
"Show them," he whispered to them, "how pretty girls are like that!"
Four girls, barely outgrown the teen age, with white gloves and identical olive-green jackets and trousers stood tight.
Four pairs of black pumps were in a straight row.
Four neat hats sat perfectly on the hair kept with blue-and-white streaked bows.
"To me they look average," Mr. Tang observes disdainingly.
"Models are pretty."
Several governments have already tried to polish the image of China's city inspectors.
In one city, all new applicants must have a college degree.
Guangdong province introduced a luminous blue uniform instead of the grey-green ones.
Wuhan in Central China replaced violence with rigors: In 2009, according to 50 officials encircled an improperly set-up food truck and stared at the operator until after half an hour and left the square.
Xindu, an urban district in Chengdu with 680,000 inhabitants, wants to fundamentally change the image.
Since 2003, 13 women have been reinforcing the municipal police patrols. They have been selected for special ones based on their appearance, good figure and youth.
They are supposed to add a softer, more feminine aspect to the crude police.
However, Scarlett Johansson, too, would hardly succeed in improving the bad image that the Chinese have of these city commands.
Right, according to the critics.
Unlike the police, these officers are only allowed to enforce municipal laws using fines or other administrative penalties.
However, the Chinese media routinely show a different picture.
In January 2008, officials in Hubei province beat a spectator to death when he filmed her with his mobile phone, as they crushed a protest action against a rubbish dump.
Last year, a training manual of the Beijing officials was stolen and published on the Internet. It describes how offenders can be beaten up without bloodshedding.
This year, a Shanghai watermelon dealer suffered brain injuries following a scuffle with five officers.
A video game brimming with violence that can be downloaded from the internet shows trained officials in China in an attack on street vendors.
"Chengguan scares the government," lamented last year's national newspaper China Daily, following further controversy regarding the tactics.
The newspaper demanded a "really thorough purge."
Skepticists find the current approach clearly inadequate.
When the district searched for eight female recruits by advertisement in October, an editorial in the Beijing Evening News raised the question of whether the women should have real tasks or only serve as an optical distraction.
Probably both of them.
According to vacance advertisements, women aged 18 to 22 were searched for with good figure and "harmonious facial features."
You should be above-average - over 1.5m or 5 feet, 2 1/2 inches.
The employment relationship ends at the age of 26.
According to civil servants, the activity for women over 25 is too physically strenuous.
"The most important thing is their image," an unnamed district official stated on the quasi-government website Rednet.com.
"Whether the candidates get the job first depends on their outward appearance, such as height, weight, facial features, etc."
Then come temperament and "inner qualities."
"Female Chengguan are like flower vases," he said, adding, "yet beyond that, they still have other duties."
Zheng Lihua, deputy head of the district's city administration, does not agree with this description,
However, it admitted that, in China, symplices are not uncommon in vacancies for both sexes.
The same is true for uniform facial features.
Whether this equates to good looks is controversial in China.
But people with disabilities or bodily distortions don't need to apply.
"Unfortunately, we can't use lames or hunchbacks here," Mr. Zheng said.
"Your image wouldn't be good."
Liu Yi, who patrols Baoguang Square near a convent, is 22, has apple cheeks and a well-shaped mouth.
She didn't perceive as sexist that so much emphasis is placed on her appearance.
"Don't you think I look sexy in this uniform?" she asks with an ironic look.
Her colleague with the dimple, 21-year-old Xu Yang, comments, "We're committed to representing the image of the city."
According to them, they do not regret the short attitude, as they would have more ambitious career plans than simply scare traders through the alleys to prevent them from doing business.
Every morning the patrol deals with about twelve street vendors who try to sell as many rolls and tofu dishes as possible on foot or on a bicycle before they are chased away.
"Mr. Wang, get out of here, please."
"We've told you this so many times before!" explains Ms Xu to a merchant who had escaped on foot while temporarily abandoning his noodle kitchen attached to a bicycle cart.
According to the officials, the activity is monotonous rather than exhausting.
"It's the same almost every day," explains Huang Jing, 20, who is studying sales at leisure.
"All routine."
One reason for this is that female officials are not allowed to confiscate the traders' goods, like their male counterparts.
They can only threaten offenders with reporting them to their male superiors.
This is intended to protect them from public hostility, to which officials in China are frequently exposed.
This year, hundreds of residents of Kunming, the capital of Yunnan province, rioted after rumor circulated that Chengguan officials had killed a trader.
More than a dozen polices and Chenguan officers were injured in the nocturnal incident, with 14 government vehicles knocked over or set on fire.
Xindu has so far been spared from such outbreaks of violence.
But riots cannot be ruled out.
Just two blocks away from the quiet Baonguang Square where the female officials patroulated that morning, over 50 people gathered on a street corner.
Officials had confiscated a motorcycle that was being repaired contrary to regulations on the sidewalk rather than in a workshop.
The owner shouted Zeter and Mordio.
After 15 minutes of hesitation, the officers made their way to their vehicles with a grim face and continued with the motorcycle and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a coordinator of the male patrol, remained behind. In the process, he turned over his white name tag to remain anonymous.
Like her colleagues, the coordinators - men over 40 - play a purely supportive role.
But they weren’t trained to present a clean image.
"Sometimes we're proceeding verbally."
"Sometimes but also physically," Mr. Li explains soberly.
“Most of the time, people start fighting.”
Prosecutor won't accuse Senator Ensign
Prosecutors from the Department of Justice acquitted Senator John Ensign of the blams raised against him after his affair with a former electoral worker and his attempt to lobby her husband, the senator's lawyers announced Wednesday.
This decision ends the most serious legal allegation against Senator Ensign, the Republican of Nevada. Until the scandal, which made headlines nationwide and put a damper on his career, he was considered a possible presidential nominee of the Republicans for 2012.
As Senator Ensign's office announced, he was "highly pleased" with the verdict after the one-year investigation.
The Senate Ethics Committee, however, could still impose disciplary penalties.
According to Ensign's lawyers, they have been informed by the Department of Justice that investigations against Senator Ensign have ceased and that there are no further charges on this matter.
The Ministry of Justice declined to comment on the case.
The verdict was a surprise to many ethics lawyers outside the Department of Justice. In their opinion, there has been sufficient evidence that Ensign disregarded the lobbying ban.
Interviews, e-mails and other documents first disclosed in The New York Times in October 2009 gave rise to presumption that Ensign Douglas Hampton, one of his election workers, helped lucrative assignments to buy his silence about an affair with Hampton's wife Cynthia.
Cynthia Hampton served as treasurer during an election campaign Ensigns.
Ensign contacted some political friends to get Hampton work. According to the documents and interrogations, he took lobbying for them after hiring.
Since Hampton was recently still on Ensigng's electoral team, he wasn't allowed to take on any lobbying work for his former employer or the entire Senate for a year.
According to ethics experts, the unusual agreement between Ensign and Hampton, who were closely friendly before the affair, violated the so-called ban on revolving door lobbycracy.
According to the circumstantial circumstantial circumstantial evidence, "it appears to beneach an agreement and a violation of revolving door statutes," according to Stanley Brand, who specializes in defending clients targeted by the government.
While the Department of Justice officially declined to announce the reasons for the decision in the Ensign case, Mr. Brand and other ethics lawyers said the ministry appears very cautious in recording political charges following a variety of defeats pursued by the media with great interest, including last year's decision to drop the indictment against former Senator Ted Stevens for misconduction on the part of the prosecutor.
Prosecutors "are obviously terrified," Melanie Sloan, the chairwoman of the "Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics Association in Washington" (an organization whose aim is to combat corruption in U.S. parliamentary and government circles), said a group of lawyers who have brought various charges against Mr. Ensign to justice.
"This basically means that high-level government officials can now assume that the laws don't apply to them," she added.
Paul Coggins, chief defender of Mr Ensign, said in an interview that he had received a prosecutor's phone call on Monday and another the next day he was told that the ministry would not file any lawsuits against his client.
Mr Ensign's defense lawyers had partnered with prosecutors to convince them that the senator "acted in accordance with the law," as his office reiterated in a statement Wednesday.
Mr. Coggins declined to explain in more detail the legal arguments he had put forward to prosecutors.
"It's not good for me to go into the conversation details between the Department of Justice and me. Only so much is said: We think we have made the right decision," he said.
Last month, the Federal Election Commission decided to close the investigation into a separate lawsuit by the Ethics Group, according to which a payment of $96,000 by Mr. Ensign's parents to the Hamptons had violated the federal election law.
The Electoral Commission said there had not been enough evidence that the money had been something other than a gift.
NV Energy, a big company in Nevada that for a long time supported the senator, was one of the firms where Mr Hampton found lobbyist work thanks to the help of Mr. Ensign.
An December 2008 e-mail proves that, after the company has taken Mr. Hampton as a lobbyist, Mr. Ensing's chief of staff, John Lopez, had a conversation with Mr Hampton in which he told him that he was "knocking" at the Home Office because of one thing.
Mr. Ensign was also scheduled for lunch with Mr. Hampton and another Nevada customer, Allegiant Air, and asked The Transport Secretary to arrange a meeting on behalf of the airline after it hired Mr Hampton to lobby.
When, for the first time, information about this affair became public last year, Mr. Ensign apologized to his constituents and gave up his position in the Senate, where he had been in fourth place in the ranking of Republicans.
After failing to notice for many months, two weeks ago Mr. Ensign told reporters that he was aiming for a senate term.
Announcing that Mr. Ensign is no longer the target of policy investigations, his press secretary, Jennifer Cooper, the senator, said the senator hopes that the Ethics Commission will very quickly follow suit by the Department of Justice and cease his investigation.
"Senator Ensign is looking forward to continuing his hard work in serving the people of Nevada," she said.
Cheshire appeal draws attention to Twitter
Half a dozen of these tapped away in the riots in the courtroom.
Every minute, they sent their reports of grisly witness statements via Twitter.
In the end, according to the defense, there were more than 140,000 Twitter messages about the trial surrounding rape and bed-chained children in a house engulfed in blaze.
On Thursday's closely-haunted trial by Steven J. Hayes, who was accused of killing three members of a family from Cheshire, Connecticut, will come to an official end when the judge's verdict about the death sentence the jury pronounced falls.
Yet Mr Haye's lawyers have already filed motions to the court setting out reasons for an appeal and which the courts will employ for years.
One of the most provocative reasons is that the intensive coverage of the trial - also due to the extensive use of Twitter by average reporters - has resulted in a "circus atmosphere" and such a widespread, immediate penetration of riotous details, so that the jury had been inadmissibly influenced by public indignation.
This objection could prompt the appeals courts to deal with the question of whether the new technologies make new rules necessary in the courtroom.
"Just as I've witnessed it during this process, I believe it's really a danger if the entry of the new technologies into the courtroom affects each individual's right to a fair trial," Thomas J. Ullman, Mr. Haye's main defense attorney, said.
The presiding judge, Jon C. Blue of the Supreme Chamber Court, dismissed the motions by decision last week succinctly when he said that the new media would be "carefully controlled," and that there was no proof that the jury had been driven by passions.
However, this was certainly not yet the last word on this matter.
For decades, dictiffications have found that reporting notorious cases raises doubts about the fairness of a trial.
In its 1966 historic decision, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the verdict against Dr Sam Sheppard, an osteopath from Cleveland who is said to have killed his wife, and said in his decision that there would have been a "carnival atmosphere in the courtroom" because of the new media.
Some people believe that the television series and the feature film "On the Run" is based on this crime.
Some lawyers say court objections, which Mr. Haye's conviction looks at half a century later, could follow the argument that new rules have become necessary to regulate the lines of communication from the courtroom that were still unimaginable at the time of Dr Sheppard.
"There's a good reason to be troubled about the influence of the new media in trials persecuted by a general public," said John J. Donohue III, a professor at Stanford Law School who has investigated the death penalty in Connecticut. According to his report, it is arbitrarily imposed.
Professor Donohue said some appelation courts might be fascinated by the idea that restricting immediate communication from the courtroom could make the trials fairer.
The Twitter matter is just one of many that Mr. Hayes's lawyers set out to discuss for years and thus delay a conviction.
The defense lawyers, for example, are also claiming that Mr. Hayes may have already been prejudged when Judge Blue had not allowed them to test the reactions of potential jurors by showing them gruesome crime scene photos during juror selection.
Some of the jurors have said photos of the victims, Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48 years, and their dumbrers, Hayley, 17, and Michaela, 11, would have filled them with revulsion.
The defense lawyers say they are also questioning the constitutionality of the death penalty constitution, and argue that the judge mistakenly exchanged a juror who had been a critic of the prosecutor's office, and that he improperly exercised pressure on the verdict when he made the uncommon decision to release the jury over the weekend.
Jurors weren't isolated.
Yet her arguments about the influence of in-depth coverage of the proceedings attract perhaps the greatest interest.
In a verdict contest they submitted last month, they found that the case had dominated the news in Connecticut and was debated by the candidates during the election campaign for the governor's office and for the American Senate.
They argued that the New Media had continuously rekindled the flames of emotions over the case.
"Such a day-to-day rush of media beepers, cameras and reporters outside the courtroom," the lawyers wrote, "haven't been experienced before in New Haven, and maybe even nationwide."
Her emphasis on reporting through Twitter is controversial.
In interviews, some journalists said that the allegation that this direct news was more incisible than reports, which will be refunded more slowly, is an anti-tech view that imprisong coverage of future processes.
However, some lawyers said appelation courts were often confronted with the argument that social and technical changes were in need of adaptation by the courts.
Mr. Hayer's lawyers amplify this claim.
In their appeal, they referred to the 1966 Supreme Court's decision, in which Dr Sheppard's sentencing was overturned due to the reporting and made it clear that the New Media requires new rules.
"This case," they wrote, "represents Sheppard against Maxwell of the 21st-century."
Can politeness connect the separating?
After a campaign of swear words and negativity, little courtesy broke out in Washington this week.
Is it the omen of a real two-party cooperation, or a moment of illusion, before you quickly return to the struggle of the parties in the capital?
Tuesday's meeting between President Obama and congressional leaders did not yield a breakthrough on the big issues one had to deal with during the so-called "lame duck session," the session at the end of a period of government in which decisions are no longer taken.
According to all that one hears, it was neither a polite discussion between two different sides nor an observancement of their shared accountance to govern, but rather an empty episode of partisan point collecting.
The public meeting of the president's deficit commission on Wednesday was also civilized, despite the political differences that continue to exist between the members.
It seems clear that a close interaction over a period of months gives rise to mutual respect, obscuring the ideological divide that continues to separate members from each other.
That's hardly enough to say it's a new era of cooperation dawned.
Both sides are now in a testing phase - in which one demands from the other sign of willingness to cooperate and compromise, while trying to weigh the limits of public opinion and the patience of voters after a renewed party warfare.
Therefore, Tuesday's meeting itself and its assessment by attendees were limited to a minimum of demands or blames.
The philosophical differences between Obama and the Republicans are too big and too deep to expect a rapid change in the atmosphere that has prevailed over the past two years.
Whether it's taxes, donations, health or entitlements, the two sides are miles apart.
Last but not least, the November election of Washington voted mildly, at least for a short time.
"There's no question the tone has changed," said Pete Wehner, an official representative of the Bush administration who now works for the "Ethics and Public Policy Center."
"It's inevitable after elections.
Elections create partisan passions that die again.
And that’s a good thing.”
On some issues, especially those that are on the "lame duck session" agenda, Republicans and Democrats have little choice but to find a solution."
Bush's tax cuts end at the end of the year, and no one advocates that.
Both sides want to find a way to preserve some or even everyone, whether temporary or permanent.
Likewise, a compromise on the increased unemployment benefits is to be expected, albeit only because Republicans cannot afford to believe the favored tax cuts for the riches while denying support to unemployed workers.
After Tuesday's meeting at the White Hous, optimism has risen that one can agree on these issues during the "lame duck session" and at the same time also ratify the New START Treaty.
Yet William Glaston des Brookings Insitution warns that successful cooperation on urgent issues of the lame duck session" does not necessarily have to lead to permanent cooperation of the parties when the new 112th Congress is convened in January.
Instead, he says, a phase of disagreement is more likely, perhaps even unavoidable.
"After the things that need to be done, this short-term psychology will turn into confrontation," he said.
Wehner is also skeptical - and this skepticism is shared by many - the courtesy leads to true cooperation.
"I see a lot of what's happening from today until 2012, as pre-fighting, or shadow boxing, for the main event of 2012," he said.
The reasons for this have a lot to do when it comes up with the new Republican majority in the White House and with the tea-party voters who served their education.
How worried Republican leaders may also be that they poker too high, they know they need to try to keep their promises to cut government spending and override health care reform.
These party lines are already evident in the first reactions to the recommendations of the Deficit Commission, which include a tax reform and the raising of some taxes, as well as the raising of the retirement age and the lowering of government spending.
Vice-chairwoman Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have taken on one of the most difficult jobs in Washington and, according to their own assessment, have pushed so much to put the deficit issue on the public agenda that these can no longer be ignored.
But within the Commission they need 14 of the 18 votes to make their plan the official recommendation and they are very struggling to even get close to that number.
The greatest resistance can be found in the elected representatives of the body, since there is little room for manoeuvre or little sympathy for compromise on financial issues, at least today.
Galston predicts two phases in the battle between the White House and Republicans in Congress.
The first fight will take place next year in the form of party clashes, when Republicans challenge Obama with regard to the state budget, taxes and the health care system, later followed by a period of greater two-party co-operation.
What will lead to this change?
public opinion.
It has been proven that, despite the partisanship of the current election, the majority of Americans still want the two parties to work together.
If confrontation leads to an impasse, as happened in 1995, then both parties will have to pay careful attention to the public reaction.
Whoever is condemned to lose will have to change course and seek compromise.
In fact, this may be an optimistic view.
Partisance and polarization are greater today than in the mid-1990s, and they appear to be a greater ideological divide between Obama and the congressional republicans than between President Bill Clinton and the Republicans at the time.
At the same time, the activists of both parties see 2010 as a pure prelude to a far more far-reaching 2012 election.
Seen in this way, the politeness displayed this week may be greeted by many Americans.
But it will require much more effort on both sides to find a common denominator in the questions that still separate the two from each other.
Leaked U.S. document portrays Moscow as port of corruption
The control of the city is in the hands of "kleptocracy" and it passes on part of the bribes and the protection money it collects to the Kremlin. Thus, in February, the American Embassy in Russia reported in a memo.
Both police and the state security service extort high protection money so that they can maintain their expensive standards of living in Moscow, it says.
They collect money not only from legitimized businesses, but also from organized criminal gangs.
Every stratum of bureaucracy - which Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has described as "verticals of authority" - takes its share of the money the ladder is migrating upwards.
The memo, which was sent to Washington under Ambassador John Beyrle's name, was posted by WikiLeaks on its homepage on Tuesday.
The memo is based on sources whose names have been edited.
It was written while Yuri Luzhkov was still Moscow's mayor, and it accuses him of corruption.
He was fired by President Dmitry Medvedev in October, but there is little evidence that in the meantime it would have been cleaned up.
Luzhkov and his wife, Yelena Baturina, who owns a well-known construction company, have repeatedly denied allegations of corruption for years, and he has won numerous defamation lawsuits against such accusations.
In September, he explained to a Russian television station when asked about a documentary suggesting he was corrupt: "It's crazy, it's dirty, it's dirty."
> An incredibly large number of Russians believe their society is corrupt. That’s according to surveys.
But it is uncommon that, as happened in the ambassador's report, individual persons are named, and it's unheard that this happens through a diplomat from a foreign country.
"The Russian government's direct connection to crime has led some to call it "disturbed" and come to the conclusion that the government is acting like a kleptocrat rather than a government," according to the memo.
"Criminal elements enjoy a "kryshna" (a term from the world of criminals/mafia, which literally means "roof" or protection), which extends over the police, state security (FSB), the service oversight authority (MVD) and the prosecutor's office as well as the entire Moscow government bureaucracy.
Experts have discovered a three-layer structure within Moscow's criminal world.
"Luzhkov is paramount."
"FSB, MVD and the militia are on the second stage."
"Lastly, the Ordinary criminals and the criminal inspectors at the lowest stage."
"This is an incompetent system in which criminal groups close a gap in some areas because the city offers no help whatsoever."
The report goes on to say that the FSB concedes money from the biggest firms and that the police arrest small businessmen.
One source, it is said, "explained that Moscow's business owners understand that it is best to get protection from the MVD and FSB (and not from organized crime) as they not only have more guns, resources and power than the criminals, but also because they are protected by the law."
"For this reason, protection through criminal gangs is no longer as in demand."
The memo notes that, while collecting the funds is comprehensive, the protection itself is very modest; even those who pay are sometimes arrested.
Those who don't pay find their business closed very quickly - due to one or the other pretext.
Medvedev has talked several times about fighting corruption, but at the same time noted that he has made little progress.
The memo of the embassy contains descriptions of men going into the Kremlin with suitcases that were presumably full of money.
"In his fight against corruption, Medvedev has to rely on the bureaucrats," Georgy Satarov, director of Indem, a Moscow commission of experts, said in an interview recently.
"But he's part of that bureaucracy."
"It's not a part of the political class because a political class no longer exists in Russia."
Without politicians, without an opposition, without a separation of the forces, he says, corruption is inevitable.
Nationwide, it estimates Indem, corruption costs Russia more than $300 billion a year.
The country ranks 154th among a more recent investigation into the global perception of corruption by the non-governmental organization Transparence International (countries are listed by "least corrupt" to "most corrupt").
Revealing the memos of the American embassy, which happened late at night after Moscow, will certainly cause displeasures within the Russian government, even though, since Luzhkov can be accused for Moscow’s mistakes, it can also be a way for the Kremlin to claim that one solves the problems.
In an interview with Larry King for CNN, Putin claimed the WikiLeaks documents could be a forgery and reacted angrily to the revelation that American diplomats called him Batman and Medvedev Robin.
"The truth is that it's about our interaction, which is an important factor of domestic politics in this country," he said.
"But to be honest, we didn't expect this to happen with such arrogance, with such pressure and in such an unethical way, you know."
Khmer Rouge veterans forced to confront the past
In a distantly lit, concrete classroom with dirty and flaking walls, the director of Anlong Veng High School is reminiscent of the man who built it the later Khmer Rouge guide Ta Mok.
"Everyone here loves Ta Mok."
"He was a good leader and he took care of his people," Sreng Kor Ma said, 42.
Due to its brutality during Khmer Rouge rule known as "the butcher," the commander remains popular in this remote former Khmer Rouge stronghold in northwest Cambodia, where he built hospitals and bridges, developed the infrastructure and where thousands of former soldiers still live.
Yet this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge's surrender to the government, longtime supporters in Anlong Veng are being challenged.
In April, a local truth and reconciliation forum allowed the victims the public confrontation with the people who once backed the regime.
In June, the government handed out a textbook that for the first time tells children of former soldiers the story of the Khmer Rouge.
And in July, for the first time, a Community Tribunal of the U.N. and Cambodia denounced a former member of the Khmer Rouge when it sentenced the former head of the notorious Tuol Sleng Torture Chamber, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as Duch, to 35 years in prison.
With each of these developments, fear grew under the Khmer Rouge veterans of Alnlong Veng, complicating their efforts for reconciliation and reintegration into Cambodia's society.
"There is still rejection and fear in the former Khmer Rouge, but they have no power to do anything," Chhang Youk, head of Cambodia's documentation center, an independent organization researching the regime.
"For them, life under the Khmer Rouge was glorious, but the regime has become a symbol of evil."
"It divides even families."
During Khmer Rouge Rule in Cambodia, between 1975 and 1979, an estimated 1.7 million people were executed or they died of disease, revision or starvation.
When the Vietnamese invaded and overthrew the Pol Pot-Ied government in 1979, the regime's remnants and his military fled to Cambodia's border with Thailand.
Here they spread unrest until December 1998, when the last movement surrendered.
When Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary fought the guerrilla war from their base in the west of the mountains and jungles of Cambodia, Ta Mok approached the next generation in Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s, when an UN-funded peace agreement led to the country's first democratic elections in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began to overrun the government, resulting in the surrenders of Ieng Sary in 1996 and Nuan Chea and Khieu Samphan.
Pol Pot died of a natural death in 1998, and Ta Mok, who had not yielded, was arrested by government forces at the Dangrek Mountains in 1999.
He died in 2006 in Phnom Penh prison, where he waited for trial.
As their patrons no longer protected them, most former Khmer Rouge fighters escaped into the grueling poverty of rural Cambodia and many kept the movement in nostalgic memory.
Even if a few Khmer Rouge's elitist officials retained their local government posts because they laid down their arms, the ordinary soldiers remained poor, uneducated farmers.
"These people have benefited very little from the surrender," said Sok Leang of the Centre for Justice and Reconciliation, which holds public forums throughout Cambodia, also in Anlong Veng
"They are anchored in the utopian agrarian ideology of the regime."
"They grew up without any professional knowledge."
Sor Lim, 55, who had joined the Rhmer Rouge in 1974 as a teenager, struggled in the jungle for Anlang Veng before settling down in 1998 to lead a life as a poor rice farmer.
"Life under the Khmer Rouge was good," he said.
"Ta Mok gave everyone something to eat."
"But now life is hard because we have to take care of ourselves."
The ongoing Khmer Rouge Tribunal has also evoked worries here.
Earlier next year, the court will try to bring Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, as well as Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister, to justice.
The mandate of the court is to convict the old leaders and those who are mainly responsible for the atrocities, but it is not told whether it will pursue further acts beyond these four cases.
That didn't contribute much to mitigating fears in Anlong Veng.
Recent media speculations have focused on Im Chaem, 64, who had been district chief in Manteay Meanchey province during Khmer Rouge rule in the late 1970s.
In 2007, she told commissioners of the Documentation Center of Cambodia that she had led the construction of the Trapeang Thma Damm, a project in which thousands of forced laborers probably died.
On a hot evening, Im Chaem returned from work in the fields to her wooden house on a dirty road outside Anlong Veng.
As the sun cast long shadows on the withered weeded grass Im Chaem declined to discuss her past at the Khmer Rouge.
If the court summoned her, she'd refuse to leave, she said.
"Cambodia lives in peace and stability," she said.
"If there weren't any persecution, there'd be war here."
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who escaped from the Khmer Rouge in 1878, has also repeatedly warned that the situation would become unsafe, the court would indict more suspects, even though experts and historians are discussing this.
"Cambodia must dig a hole and bury the past," he said.
But confrontation with the past is what Cambodia needs to do in order to move forward, said Chhang Yuk of the documentary center.
"Reconciliation with the Khmer times means gathering the broken pieces," he said.
"It's our duty to put these broken parts together, so that like everything can be understood."
The center created the first government-approved textbook about the Khmer Rouge, the "History of Democratic Cambodia."
It distributed the book in June in Anlong Veng as an appendix to the history book for the Department of Education's High School, which contains fewer than four pages about the Khmer Rouge.
As is often the case in Cambodia, the young people in Anlong Veng know very little about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city's links to the regime.
Touch Valeak, 19, a student at Anlong Veng High School, said the new textbook helps students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted skeptically as he brought the book home to read it.
"My family rejects a lot of parts of the book and the dish," he said.
"They're suspicious because they're not sure how many people are convicted by the court."
Such resistance makes the reconciliation process complex and complicated, Sok Leang said.
But the public forums, the textbook and the dish gradually gain influence," he said.
The Khmer Rouge still has a powerful attraction here.
At the top of the Dangrek mountains, a path overgrown by shrubs and covered with plastic waste leads to a rectangle of black soot, covered with a rusty tin canopy.
Pol Pot's body had been burned on a tire heap here after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a mobile phone saleswoman on the side of the road, said she didn't know much about the man who had been burned here.
"But he has a strong spirit and a lot of people here go to him to pray," she said.
It was Nuom Sothea's birthday and later in the day she wanted to go to Pol Pot's final resting place, where she's going to put down a bush of ripe bananas - hoping he brings her happiness.
Belarus agrees to no longer accumulating uranium
In a surprising victory for President Obama’s campaign to secure nuclear materials worldwide, the government of Belarus announced on Wednesday that it will no longer collect highly enriched uranium, which is a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The deal was announced in a joint statement following a meeting of Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton with Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov in an additional article from the security summit.
The U.S. government had tried for years to convince Belarus to no longer collect highly enriched uranium and appeared to have hurdled last spring.
But U.S. government officials told the Washington Post that in the past two months, two secret agreements have been reached with Belarus to remove 187 pounds of weapons-grade uranium from a Belarusian research institute.
That set the stage for Wednesday's agreement to remove the rest of the material - according to an official co-worker who remains anonymous due to sensitivity of the issue, about 500 pounds.
The deal appears to be one of the key findings of Obama's "Nuclear Security Summit of the 47 Nations" of April, which had barred Belarus.
And it was obviously good enough to secure the former Soviet republic's invitation to the nexisting summit in 2012.
"I've been really thrilled by the fact that they want a seat on the Nuclear Safety Summit (from 2012)."
"This proves to me that it works," Ken Luongo, president of the Global Security Partnership, told a group to promote nuclear safety.
Clinton called the agreement "a very significant, important move" by Belarus.
Obama pledged in Prague in April 2009 to secure all precarious nuclear material within four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of their highly enriched uranium.
Belarus will be the seventh.
A dozen countries did it under George W. Bush's administration.
At the time of Obama's nuclear safety summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko has declared that the nation will never give up its uranium.
"How keep highly enriched uranium - hundreds of kilos, which is fundamentally capable of weapons, and less enriched uranium," Lukashenko said at the time.
“That’s our raw material, we’re not going to make dirty bombs and we won’t sell it to anyone.”
"We use it exclusively for research purposes, that's all."
Nevertheless, Belarus approached the US a few months ago when it indicated interest in the meeting of an agreement.
The first attempt to remove the uranium of Belarus in October was particularly complicated because the material was slightly irradiated, officials say.
It has been loaded into a special train that is more than 1,300 miles to a Russian factory for storage and disposal for, officials say.
The second run-up was decided on Monday and was carried out by plane, according to the officials.
The U.S. government pays $14 million for these efforts, with much of the money flowing into Belarusian research with weakly enriched uranium, which cannot be used for nuclear weapons.
"This was extremely carefully coordinated and coordinated to build trust," which led to the agreement signed by Clinton, said Andrew Bieniawski, a representative of the Department of Energy working on Global Hazard Reduction.
The James Martin Centre for Nondispensing Studies estimates that Belarus had at least 88 pounds of gun-capable uranium this year, as well as hundreds of pounds of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus wants to dissolve its camp by 2012, according to the joint agreement.
Relations between Belarus and the U.S. have been strained for years, primarily because of Lukashenko's harsh disregard for human rights.
But Wednesday's statement suggests a possible rapprochement.
"Welcoming the progress of these global security issues, the United States of America and Belarus announces that increased respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains a central issue in improving bilateral relations and it is essential for the progress of the country and its citizens," she explains.
No objections from the Spanish government to CIA flights
The Spanish government had no objections to the CIA flights with Taliban and al-Qaida prisoners over Spanish territory to Guantánamo, according to a telegram of the U.S. ambassador to Madrid.
This is reflected in the documents unveiled by WikiLeaks and forwarded to the El País (by-cable) newspaper. It is added that former Deputy Prime Minister María Teresa Fernández de la Vega demanded that the Spanish government should be informed "to prove that foreign planes flying over Spain are adequately supervised."
In June 2006, following the publication of a report by the Council of Politeria indicting aid to human rights practices in a dozen countries, including Spain, former North American Ambassador Eduardo Aguirre and Fernández de la Vega discussed.
The deputy head of government admitted that the Council's report "met Zapatero in the offside" and reiterated that Spain had nothing to hide on this issue.
"De la Vega underlined that Spain had no objections to the intelligence flights over Spanish territory."
"They just want to be informed" so as not to be "unpreparedly surprised," Aguirre reports by cable.
Former Foreign Minister Miguel Ángel Moratinos also pointed out to the ambassador that the Spanish government "does not want to pay much attention to this matter, even though the government has little influence, as it is in the hands of the judges."
Prosecutor Vicente González, for his part, told the embassy in January 2007 that he would not bail out the judicial initiative to request the declassification of the documents of the Spanish intelligence headquarters (CNI) and the defense minister via the CIA flights.
"These reports do not contain any incriminating or sensitive information," the ambassador explained after a conversation with the prosecutor.
Even so, the U.S. was more concerned about the "apparent coordination between the Spanish prosecutor and German prosecutors" than the declassified information of the CNI and the Ministry of Defense.
"This coordination between independent investigators will complicate our efforts for discreet handling between governments," Aguirre laments.
At a lunch in March 2007, attended by U.S. Ambassador and Spanish Interior Minister Alfredo Pérez Rubalcaba, the latter commented that "although the Spanish judges are insanely focused on their independence and doing what they deem appropriate," he has given instructions to his staff to "avoid inflated rhetorical comments to abate the tension."
The ambassador also voiced his concern about the PP's approach to this matter.
"Despite their generally pro-American stance, our experience teaches us that the PPP will not doubt it, pointing out that Zapatero's actions by the U.S., which could be interpreted as possible human rights violations, will capitalize, even if these revelations could have a negative impact on the U.S. government," he said.
The child killer confesses that she complained on the Internet about the "tediousness" of motherhood.
The young woman spoke of the child when she was already dead and she had a blog: "Terror in a Galician way"
Mónica Juanatey Fernández is "Muki" on the Internet.
Under this epithet, which she carries from an early age, she maintains contact with a cousin and at least one friend. Both come from Noia, a village in the province of La Coruña where she was born.
Notifications are from 2008.
In order to understand these, you have to know their love life, which the daily newspaper ABC reconstructs on the basis on the basis of witness statements of their family members.
The 30-year-old Galician woman established a relationship with Iván Túñez, an industrialist, in 1998.
She lasted a year.
Shortly after disbanding her relationship, it was revealed that she was pregnant.
He wanted to know if the baby was from him, but his alumni never let him carry out DNA analysis.
Shortly afterwards, she entered into a different relationship.
The chosen one was Alberto, a young man from Lousame, a neighboring community.
She lived with him for a long time.
César lived with them.
When the young woman went to the Balearic Islands to look around in the labour market, the little one stayed with her partner, who even accompanied her to Palma for a week.
But from the contents of their messages, it can be concluded that their love affair shattered and they separated.
In March 2008, she is alone in Menorca and writes: "Alberto is very peculiar and pissed because I came here to work."
"When I told him, he thought it was good, but now, of course, he realizes what it means to be alone with the boy all day long and not being free to be able to do and leave what he wants."
Later, she mentions a message she just received on the phone: "I want you to come back this month or send me an address where I can send you the boy."
"If he doesn't like that I'm leaving the boy with my mother, I'll pick him up when I'm back."
In May, she speaks of returning to her village: "This week I'm going there, but I'll only stay there until the 19th, because I have to introduce myself to a job on the 20th if I don't want to lose them."
She declares that she takes her first-born if all goes well.
"He's going to be where he has to be, that is, with me. Never in life will I leave him again with anyone except my mother," a dubious intention considering their bad relationship.
There's another e-mail in July.
When she writes these, it is assumed that the boy, who came to the island on 1 of the month and was allegedly murdered on the 10th, is dead.
"On the one hand, César is satisfied, on the other hand."
"On the one hand, he's with me and he likes the place a bit, but on the other hand he has to go to school every day."
She's talking about moving to Mallorca.
"That's where he (the boy) is going to attend a summer course, so I have less spending on the nanny."
On the 17: Memories and Complaints.
"A huge kiss from César asking me when you're coming."
"Alberto says he've sent me some videos on the email. See, if he wants, he'll find me, a crap."
On the 30th another: "The boy's doing well. He's going into a summer course to learn Catalan."
From Víctor, her current partner, there's one: "He'll shoot me money forward."
On the Internet, in anime and manga blogs, there are messages from her.
She even had her own blog in MySpace, which is now blocked, and in which she called herself "Muki, the prison dismissaler."
"Terror a la gallega (headline) ".
The last man who lived with her, a sworn-in-the-way hunting supervisor, declares to have remorse because he feels guilty.
He can't believe everything happened because she hadn't told him she was a single mother.
Mónica was transferred yesterday by the Centre for Prisoners of Justice in Menorca to Provincial Prison in Palma.
The defender, Carlos Maceda, explained that she could not remember the course of the facts, "and she had no intention of causing harm to the little one."
Previously, however, she had confessed to drowning César in the batten and stowing his body in a suitcase.
Defense and prosecutor's office requested a psychiatric assessment.
Partido Popular won 4 seats in Congress and PSC lost 9
With the results of November 28, the PP could play a decisive role in the cities of Lérida, Tarragona and Barcelona
After the significant increase in votes and deputies in the last regional elections, the PP is already turning its attention to the parliamentary elections - brought forward or not. Their expectations are more than positive, because the number of votes that this party scores in these elections is usually twice as high as that in the elections in Catalonia.
The Catalan People's Party received 610,473 votes in the 2008 parliamentary elections, while this digit was only 316,222 in regional elections in 2006.
Last Sunday, the PPC scored 384,091 votes.
If this ascending tendency - the result of redirecting the PSC's share of votes and the attachment of the PP's electoration - should continue, the PP's analysts are predicting that the PPC could rise from 8 to 12 congressmen in parliamentary elections, while CiU four (it now has 10) would also gain and the PSC would lose at least 9 deputies (out of 25).
These calculations prove the fundamental importance of Catalonia for PP and PSOE.
According to the People's Party's analysis, the ERC would lose an MP - currently it has three - while ICV could hold on to its MP or gain one.
However, should the electoral calendar be maintained, the next date with the municipal elections in May 2011. Even then, the People’s Party (PP) can be the tongue on the scales to put an end to socialist supremacy in four provincial capitals and with its majority in Barcelona’s city council, one of the city administrations with the largest budget: 661 million euros for 2011.
Both the nationalists and the People's Party agree that the PSC will lose the cities of Barcelona, Tarragona, Lérida and Gerona, in which the Socialists can neither achieve a majority nor draw on their traditional allies (ERC and ICV).
According to the Republican debacle, it is not absurd that this party will lose its representation in Barcelona's local council, in which it would have to achieve 5% of the vote.
The PP, on the other hand, recorded an increase in its share of votes in the four provincial capitals and could become the decisive force in Barcelona, Lérida and Tarragona.
The nationalists are convinced that they can play the lead role in Tarragona's turnaround, where they had ruled in front of the current mayor, Josep Félix Ballesteros, together with the PP.
En Lérida, the PP lies only 500 votes behind the PSC. The mayor, Àngel Ros, maintains good relations with Mariano Rajoy - during the last election campaign they met several times.
Also in other important places, such as Badalona, Castelldefels or Reus, the People's Party recorded a significant increase.
The PP is currently represented in 34 city administrations of Catalonia and seeks the formation of lists in as many places as possible.
The Catalan cell worried Al Qaeda thousands of passports
In addition to the seven arrests in Barcelona, there were three others in Thailand
The seven Pakistanis and the Nigerians arrested by the policemen of the Barcelona Police Department formed the jihadist cell controlled from Thailand, the various Al Qaida groups, including the "Movement to Liberate Tamil Eelam" (LTTE) and "Lashkar e-Taiba" (Let) - the latter was the author of the attacks in Bombay, which demanded 106 deaths in 2008
The Spanish interior minister assigns great importance to these investigations, which goes so far that he assures that "Operation Kampai" has "weakened" Al Qaida's counterfeiting apparatus worldwide and "thus its ability to act".
The researchers assume that in the two years in which the cell was active in Catalonia, thousands of passports were sent to Thailand, where they were falsified and then sent to the groups from which the Al Qaida criminal galaxy is composed, so that their "fighters" could cross the borders of European and other western countries without impunity.
The uncovered cell is not the only of the jihadist network that has been active in Europe. Therefore, in addition to the Thai investigative bodies, other European police units have also participated in the investigations. It cannot be ruled out that there will be similar assassinations on the territory of the EU in the next few days, such as what was carried out in Barcelona last Tuesday.
In coordination with this operation (the Pakistanis Junaid Humayun, Atiqur Rehman, Jabran Asghar, Malik Iftikhar Ahmad, Mohammad Saddique Khan Begum, Tanveer Arshad and Nigerian Babatunde Agunbiade were arrested) another was carried out in Thailand, in which the Pakistanis Muhammad Athar Butt and Zeeshan Ehsan Butt were arrested
They had directed the cell established in Spain and other European countries from Bangkok.
The Catalan network - part of which had been undone in 2009 - stole passports, in particular, according to the requirements of the so-called "International Islamic Front."
This means that the documents must be issued in the names of men aged 25 to 45 years and must still be valid for several more years; they were not allowed to have visa stamps.
Investigative circles underlined that until now there have been no connections between those arrested in Catalonia and the assassination in Bombay, even though the author of this assassination, "Laschkar e-Taiba," was one of the recipient groups of passports stolen in Spain and subsequently forged in Thailand.
Nevertheless, it should be remembered that a few days before the assassination, several thousand dollars had been sent by means of a money transfer company, which were "invested" in the purchase of prepaid cards for mobile phones, which were later used by the members of the "command," which put an end to the lives of 106 people.
Police have seized extensive documentation.
PP calls on Mas to adjourn the tax agreement
The chairwoman of the PPC, Alicia Sánchez-Camacho, took the opportunity yesterday to personally present Artur Mas with her conditions in support of his inauguration as Prime Minister of the Generality. These are no different from those already faced during the election campaign and allow themselves to come together with the primacy of the economic resurgency and the omission of regionalist independence adventures, such as a financial equalization agreement
The leader of the Catalan People's Party turned with certainty to Artur Mas, who awarded her the 18 deputies she won last Sunday - a digit that is more than sufficient to feel indispensable. Because the candidate from the bourgeois-regionalist party CiU relies on at least one of the opposition parties abstaining from the vote when he takes office.
The future alliances, which the regionalists want to enter into in the upcoming parliamentary elections in Congress, depend on a possible duck with the PP. However, the talks that Mas is currently having with the other party leaders revolve around Catalonia.
As far as the party leader of the PPC is concerned, it demands that, in support of taking office, "all discourses concerning the sovereignty and independence of Catalonia" as well as an agreement on the financial balance between the state and the Autonomous Region - similar than in the Basque Country - and that the party leader of CiU even wants to subjudge to a referendum.
It is common to both political leaders that they want to slender the exaggerated political apparatus of the regional administration and to abolish the inheritance and gift tax.
The details of the Negotiations on Mas taking office are dealt with by PPC Jordi Cornet and Dolors Montserrat.
Mas has already met with Joaquim Nadal (PSC) - who announced tough opposition - and Joan Herrera (ICV). Today, he will come together with Joan Puigcercós (ERC), Albert Rivera (Ciutadans) and Joan Laporta (Solidaritat Catalana).
The nursing home reiterates that the deaths aren't due to "lack of action"
The owners of the retirement home in Olot do not rule out that their caregiver killed over eleven elderly people
Those in charge of La Caritat's nursing home in Olot (Gerona) gave a press conference yesterday highlighting the damage the criminal actions of Joan Vila Dilmâó, who had killed at least eleven old people for whom he was responsible during his work.
The head of the nursing home, Joan Sala, accompanied by his lawyer Joan Cañada, appeared in court and announced that "La Caritat would like to indictate those responsible (for the deaths)," but "the right and our liability force us to do some the process and civil libertion."
Not only does the institution have to accept a loss of societality because it had not determined the criminal actions of the caregiver in time, but it now also pays a large amount of compensation, which is calculated by the legal representatives of the family members of the victims.
Joan Vila Dilmé confessed that the first crime dates back to August 2009 and that he has continued to murder since then.
The judge ordered the exhumation of eight bodies - former patients of the aged care home - because he suspected that no doctor had personally examined the deceased, but the formalities due to their age and diseases were handled by telephone.
The head of La Caritat roundly denied this point, explaining that "missing measures may not have been the reason for the incidents at no time."
La Caritat's lawyer added that "there is no circumstantial evidence of negligent acts of a person other than the defendant in the litigation."
Baptized as a "angel of death", eleven murders confessed the day before yesterday and declared to have committed the first crime on August 29, 2009 and murdered the second old lady a month and a half later.
In this case, he killed with an overdose of insulin, in contrast to the first crime in which he had administered a mixture of various sedatives.
The head of La Caritat explained that the staff's access to the drugs was difficult to control.
The insulin injections and sedative cocktail used the caregiver in six out of nine crimes committed to free the patients with death from their suffering.
But the results of the lab tests will point out whether Vila is lying and actually choosing a less cruel death for his first victims, or whether he burned it out from the inside by administering bleaching liquor to them, as he had done according to the confession to three women, whom he murdered between 12 and 17 October.
In La Caritat, one does not believe the statements of the keeper, not even in terms of the number of confessed murders, which could well be more. This attitude is thus justified that Vila had made very different statements in court and that it makes no sense to suddenly change his course of action after eight successful murders.
They say they made themselves available to the judiciary for this reason and to clarify the scope of these incidents.
European Central Bank (EZB) threatening gestures
Investors reacted sharply yesterday to ECB President Trichet's threat, which warned "markets shouldn't underestimate our responsiveness."
The risk premiums of the euro-peripherals have risen dramatically and the shares of European banks by more than 5%.
Nevertheless, we should not underestimate the possibility that the ECB can be mistaken and that the euro has barely increased at points, prompting concern.
Yesterday's events are typical of an illiquid market which, to the extent that it is collapsing, but as a result of the closure of short and highly speculative positions.
The institutional investor is far from losing his fear.
A month ago today, the European Central Bank urged us to undo our liquidity measures, according to which the ECB will have to pull back tomorrow for the fourth time in two years, and probably several times in the future.
The Bundesbank demanded the termination of the plan to buy sovereign debt, since these were ineffective, as well as the cancellation of all extraordinary fixed-term liquidity measures. Only a one-week indefinite access should be maintained.
Following the Ireland crisis, we expect the current liquidity measures to be extended "at least until March" and the quarterly tenders will be maintained with unlimited access at quarter-end to ensure liquidity until at least June.
We also expect that the program to purchase bonds will continue and issue a strengthening signal.
If the created expectations are not met, the excellent reaction of yesterday’s markets will be thrown back again, and if we insist that we take back our liquidity measures, only God can help us!
Just as at the ECB meeting in May, at which the bank reaffirmed failing to consider the purchase of sovereign securities and markets subsequently collapsed, that history could repeat itself tomorrow.
The ECB needs to be credible and persistent.
In May she showed her teeth and on Wednesday she said she had already bought a lot, provoking a new collapse of the market.
Yesterday's auction of one-year-long government titles in Portugal was quite weak.
State asset management spent only 500 million at 5.28%, up from 750 million to 4.81% in the previous auction.
Nevertheless, interest rates in Portugal fell by 30 basis points throughout the curve, reflecting a change in the perception of the markets according to Trichet’s words.
In Ireland, the debt hole's debt hole has been released by Allied and Bank of Ireland.
The sum is estimated at about 10 000 million short-term and possible another 15 000. There is time until February to procure private capital or these banks are nationalised.
Although Ireland has completely lost its credibility after failing to recognise the problem with July's stress tests, the figures under the bailout plan are acceptable and will help reduce uncertainty.
However, the government will have to receive parliamentary approval of its new budget next Tuesday. Since it still does not have sufficient support, Ireland continues to be a source of uncertainty.
Angela Merkel has moderated her discourse with regard to the summit of heads of state and the government on December 14, where political unity is needed to show that together we play the same score.
The problem is that Germany wants to play Wagner and the other countries the ninth symphony of Beethoven.
The President of the Spanish Government yesterday presented the action plan, which is to be adopted by the Council of Ministers on Friday.
Retirement reform, transparency in autonomous regions such as financial institutions, reduction of government deficit, liberalisation, privatisation and labour procurement measures. Measures that sound in the ears of investors such as Vivaldi's Four Seasons.
The double effect Zapatero and Trichet explains why the Spanish stock market has gained 4.5 percent, twice as much as the rest of European stock exchanges.
Compared to February, the reaction from the government and Spain's central bank was quick and convincing, and we are benefiting from the surprise effect.
Today there is an authoritative auction of a three-year government bond, which will benefit from the trichet effect. That is why we are counting on strong demand, which will remove all doubts about our rescheduling capability.
For Spain, applying for an aid plan would be self-sacrifice.
We'd get liquidity that we don't need. The Treasury has 40 billion euros available, three times as much as in March. Interest rates are exceedingly expensive and access to the markets would be closed without notice.
Nevertheless, our strategy should correspond to Caesar's wife: "Now not only being, but also appearance counts."
Just courage, that's what we manage!
At the request of his children, the Chairman of the Executive Board of Eulen contests Vega Sicilia’s shareholders’ meeting
The storm in the open conflict of the Álvarez family, owners of Eulen and El Enebro companies, does not fluff.
David Álvarez, founder of the company group, will contest El Enebro's shareholders' meeting, which will take place at the request of five of his seven children on the 16th, as he considers them "absolutely annoyed." His children will try to obtain a majority to control the company's administrative bodies - owner of Bodegas Vega Sicilia winery.
David Álvarez and his children Jesús David and Mar�ía José are preparing the legal measures for a new judicial chapter in their dispute over the control of part of the group of companies.
The founder of the largest holding company for corporate services does not appear ready to give in in his litigation overment to the control of El Enebro's bodies.
He was driven out of the Board of Directors by five of his seven children, who were appointed members of the Board of Directors at the shareholders’ meeting meeting on 18 January this year, which has already been contested in the Commercial Register.
El Enebro's general meeting, convened for the 16th by the judge of Madrid Commercial Court No. 2, sparked a new thunderclash in the Álvarez family's lap.
Invoking the judge for the arrangement of the assembly is the only legal path that ensures the convening of the shareholders’ meeting.
Juan Carlos, Pablo, Emilio, Marta y Elvira Álvarez Mezquiriz's final strategy is interpreted by David Álvarez as a "desperate means of applicants - his five children - in order to be recognized by means of legal ingenuity, fraudulent machinations and legal abuse," according to official speakers.
This reasoning is the basis for contesting the assembly in hindsight to declare it "absolutely void."
Same protagonists, who shudden themselves into discrete silence, tried unsuccessfully to convene an assembly on a judicial path in June.
At El Enebro - asset company and holder of Bodegas Vega Sicilia, among other assets - David Álvarez' seven children control 100 percent of capital.
On the other hand, the founder of the service group, according to family protocols, demands the right to use more than 51% of capital.
This is the real background of the matter on which the judiciary has to decide in the hearing on the handling of the appeal lawsuit, for which no date has yet been set.
Red Bull wins in the F1 with a secret weapon from Siemens
The trolleys are improved in real-time with software.
Since Red Bull entered the Formula 1 world in 2004, its story in this exquisite universe is marked by surprises and successes.
Initially, no one paid attention to the newcomer and, despite his steeply ascending career during the last season, everyone relied on Fernando Alonso's victory in the final sprint last November.
Without wanting to reduce the capabilities of the German pilot and world champion Sebastian Vettel or the performance of Renault's engine, the "secret weapon", which the Multi Siemens Red Bull put in his hands, and thus created a winning team, has become a legend.
In reality, it is a powerful software for the simulation of processes that the German company launched in 2007 for purposes that are not related to car racing.
Called PLM, this computer science program has been used for the development of assembly chains in factories around the world, utilities and high-precise instruments.
The purpose of a faithful reproduction or simulation of a product or process before it is actually built in order to avoid errors and unnecessary costs.
PLM has facilitated the development of Canon's cameras, Sukhoi's aircraft and Nissan's most famous commercial vehicles.
Siegfried Russwurm, head of Siemen's industrial division, explains that with the use of this software in Formula 1 factory Red Bull "in a few hours, almost in real time," the changes and improvements that are detected at the races and during the tests can design and execute.
Russwurm explains that in 1992, an average of ten technicians worked in the racing stables, who were able to introduce a maximum of three or four improvements in one season.
Red Bull’s team now employs 180 highly qualified young people. With high-performing IT systems, they carry out analyses and all possible changes to the racing cars.
The Siemens manager stated that the PLM software was able to analyse and improve all the components of Red Bull's racing cars, with the exception of the engine, for which only Renault is responsible.
Materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety elements are changed.
Thanks to advanced production facilities and high-tech autoclaves, the changes can be transferred from the virtual reality of the simulator into reality within less than 24 hours.
The only limit set by this crazy technical perfectionism is the 40 million dollars that a Formula 1 team is allowed to spend on his races in a season.
Red Bull has built its high-tech factory for the development and manufacture of its racing cars in a location 50 kilometres from London.
These are two buildings in which visits - especially by journalists - are severely restricted.
Everything that happens there is Top Secret.
The workforce includes 580 people, including the two pilots, world champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
But Red Bull's true Formula 1 - hero is undoubtedly the latest world champion Sebastian Vetell.
Behind the enormous technological resources that the energy-drink manufacturer places in the services of his participation on the racetracks, there are other protagonists whose support is invaluable and whose notoriety is zero.
Steve Nevey, the plant's head of technology, stresses that five years ago it was still possible to say that R&D's progress in racing cars contributed 50 per cent to victory and the pilots were responsible for the rest.
Today, the share values have changed. The technology accounts for 85 percent of the success, while the contribution of the pilots is estimated at 15 percent.
Nevey acknowledges that Vettel and Webber can never be replaced by EDP, because they "are unpredictable gladiators."
HP will open twenty stores with local distributors in two years
Hewlett-Packard (HP) inaugurated his first exclusive store in collaboration with his partner Ront Reyes in Barcelona yesterday.
It is the first one of twenty-store sales network, each with 80 to 100 square meters, which the Multi intends to open in Spain over the next two years.
The company agrees on this initiative with the already existing 16 sales divisions in multi-brand operations, such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
HP España's vice-chairwoman, Helena Herrero, cited as an example the converted shop on the Gran Vía in Barcelona, property of Pont Reyes, with whom HP has been cooperating for thirty years.
The North American Group intends to set up 200 sales outlets in Europe by 2010, of which 20 are offices in Spain.
The first were inaugurated last week inaugurated in Bucharest (Romania) and Belgrade (Serbia). Barcelona is the third business.
Herrera didn't want to reveal the upcoming re-openings but didn't rule out Madrid could be the next target.
The vice-presidentiary, who is also head of HP Person Systems, assured that the shop opening philosophy is always the same. She relies on a local distributor with experience and industry knowledge.
The North American group of companies advises them and takes over the exclusive shop fittings (with interactive products to touch). In Europe, all stores will look the same.
HP is also responsible for the training of shopping staff.
Helena Herrero said it was not a franchising system and emphasized that with the exclusive sales outlet network it is intended "to optimize the customer service and the advice of small and micro-enterprises when shopping and thereafter, even the packaging."
The store in Barcelona has a pioneer pilot program that solves the technical problems of the HP devices with warranty.
An online service establishes a connection to the technical headquarters in Maastricht (Holland).
"In this way, we can solve problems in ten minutes that sometimes take up to two days."
"This system has been developed in particular for small and micro-enterprises as well as private customers, because the large companies already have their service contracts for technical customer service," Helena Herroro explained yesterday.
Sabadell wants to 'claw' his rival one million customers in three years
Banco Sabadell's executive boarder, Jaime Guardiola, is more optimistic than other managers. He believes 2001 will be a tough year for the banks, but initially dismisses a new liquidity crisis and predicts the end of the debt war.
Despite this, he foresees a slump in interest rate margins, which banks are trying to offset with increases in fees and prices for loans.
Sabadell expects to capture one million private customers in three years - a yet-to-existing subject.
Sabadell has found the way to exhaust the next three years and thus survive in a very complicated economic environment.
The bank will introduce a new three-year guide plan in 2011.
The goal is not only to survive without taking advantage of the public aid from the FROB, but also to increase the market share by one million private customers - 700 000 if you deduct the customers that the bank will lose. This is the big challenge this bank faces, because until now it has paid more attention to its business customers than to its private customers.
This year it has added 250 000 private customers and 50 000 companies.
Its market share for private customers is 3.5 percent, while business customers account for more than 10 percent.
Another principle of the 2011-2013 business plan is to increase productivity, i.e. austerity, cost reduction. The challenges facing the bank will be presented together with an annual financial statements report.
Few banks dare to set and publish targets in these difficult times, as the financial crime has messed up with all the plans of the banks worldwide.
Sabadell will conclude this year its Optima plan, which was initially designed for three years, but as a result of the market situation, it was extended by one financial year.
The customers who will leave the savings banks are the main goal of the bank.
At a breakfast organised by the APIE, Guardiola emphasized that 2011 would be a "difficult" year "with long reach."
The manager assumes that Spain would only "slowly" get out of the crisis.
In addition, it would be "very difficult" to "create work" in two or three years - fundamental for the survival of the banks.
Even if he assumes that the war over liabilities - which punishes interest margins - will abate, the risk surcharges that banks and savings banks will have to pay for their issuances to obtain liquidity are already being passed on to the price of loans as compensation for dwindling profit margins.
"The topics of liquidity and financing are of a structural nature and they will put pressure on the loans."
"We didn't turn off the rooster, but the interest rate differentials are very high," Guardiola admitted.
Banesto already issued an order to its branch network on Thursday to restrict long-term loans and to increase interest rate differentials (cf. Cinco Días of 22 and 27 November).
The manager also admitted that the banks were currently pursuing the strategy of "increasing the fees," but "within a contegration-influenced framework."
Sabadell's guide plan is based on organic growth, even if, as always, the group does not rule out corporative businesses.
It is not for nothing that he assures that "larger companies are necessary in Spain."
"In the coming year, everything will change," he assures.
He stresses that the demand for capital is currently "rising enormously" and does not rule out that some Spanish financial institutions will slip into red numbers.
He is of the opinion that FROB could replace the money market in the case of capital increases.
After assuring that Sabadell could also survive a temporary closure of the markets, he invalidates the argument of some bankers who are already prophesying a new liquidity crisis.
He emphasizes that nothing can be circulated, but in contrast to the incidents in June, short-term financing can be achieved via repo transactions and thus intercept the crisis.
CECA rules out a second wave of mergers of savings banks
CECA's director-general José Antonio Olavarrieta yesterday dismissed the possibility that there could be a wave of fusions by the savings banks and stressed the speed with which the industry will be restructured.
When the concentration process of the Spanish savings banks began, several savings banks did not insist on participating in the first phase. They reserved the second round.
On the other hand, the director-general of the Spanish Savings Banques Association (CECA), José Antonio Olavarrieta, assured that this second round would not take place.
"I believe that most of the mergers are completed. Cajasol's inclusion in Banca Cívica group cannot be regarded as a second round, but it was rather adjustments."
"Most part is completed. It may be that instead of seventeen we have thirteen or fourteen, but all important processes have already been completed. They have been controlled by Banco de España, who strongly supported all operations."
At a breakfast organised by the Association of Managing Directors and Manager of Aragonia (ADEA), Olavarrieta stated that the concentration process "was carried out as soon as possible and that we must be satisfied with the timely expiry, which in earlier times, not now, has been established as part of the business plans with Banco de España," he stressed.
"The transition from 45 savings banks with an average of 28 000 million assets to 17 with 75 500 million each is a historically incomparable process. The number of financial institutions has been reduced by 62 percent and they are now much larger, by which we have fulfilled the Basel agreement," he pointed out.
Olavarrieta explained that "there were a series of indiscriminated opinions about the slowness of the trial that revealed dubiousness and nonsensity," because "Banco de España exhorts us to conclude on time before the end of the year."
CECA Director-General José Antonio Olavarrieta assured yesterday that Spain has one of Europe's most rigorous financing systems and "perhaps" the whole world and added that those who doubted this would do so "without deeper knowledge."
The manager noted that, in the current economic economy, we "don't have to worry" about the site of the financial institutions and the savings banks that have gone through a "very significant" concentration process that will take place "as soon as possible."
He also reminded that the Spanish banks had passed the stress tests carried out by the ECB in July very well.
Zapatero and Germany's red line
Berlin has opted to retain the euro, but at the same time has established conditions for the entire European Union.
Tensions in the markets have sparked a double response from the government.
On the one hand, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero has presented new adjustment measures, ranging from tax reductions for small and micro-enterprises, the regulation of temporary employment agencies or the lifting of the 426 euros of support for the unemployed who do not receive unemployment benefits, to the privatization of 49 percent of AENA, the administration of Barajas and El Prat airports, as well as 30 percent of the state lottery.
On the other hand, the deputy head of government, Elena Salgado, with the help of former Prime Minister Felipe González, supports the criticism of the Chancellor Angela Merkel as the person in charge, who has evoked the current crisis.
How can these processes be understood?
It is anticipated that Merkel's position is not a mistake of her public relations work.
On the contrary, it is a new round in the difficult process of redefining the euro.
In doing so, Germany occupies a fundamentally new perspective for shaping our future, which collides with the maintenance of the traditional traditions of a majority of its partners.
In view of the resistances in Europe, Germany is beginning to speculate about three possible ways out of the crisis.
The first: A strong euro with Germany, Austria, the Netherlands and other countries, while the south would have to leave the euro area.
The second: The reintroduction of the Deutsche Mark - although this currently sounds more like science fiction - , supported by a majority of public opinion, about 50 percent of the population.
The thirds: a reorganization of the euro according to German proposals. This means: more macroeconomic scrutiny; strict budgetary management; punishment of the countries that exceed the requirements; possibly the suspension of payments by some countries as well as the extension of the costs of potential adaptation measures to the private sector.
The German position is unequivocal and has been emerging for some time. That is why there are no excuses now.
First in 2009, when both the IMF and the European Commission created incentives for deficits and the German government dragged a constitutional reform out of its sleeve to prevent this from 2016.
That was the first sign of divergencing between what Germany considered necessary (strict tax plans and stable public finances) and the practices of its European partners, including France.
The second sign was made by the central European country when Merkel painfully postponed the decision to save Greece.
At the time, this was heavily criticised. But the fact is that the political consensus in Germany, the public and the published opinion were absolutely against a bailout of Greece.
Finally, in the last days of Octobery, Merkel comes out again with a clear position. She calls for a reform of the agreements of the European Union in order to enable the rescue of a country and at the same time oblige private lenders to bear part of the costs.
This position was designed to be anti-European as it advanced the Irish crisis and everything that followed.
But their proposal, albeit somewhat sweetened, has been adopted. In the next few months, we will participate in the reform of the European Union, which is intended to enable these measures from 2013 onwards.
Germany has drawn a red line that doesn't commemorate crossing it.
It tells everyone that - even if unpopular measures have to be taken now - there will no longer be the old functioning of the euro from this date.
2013 will be the year of birth of a new currency area, in which the infringing countries are severely punished, can be forced to declare themselves incapacitated and, in this case, the private sector will have to cover some of the costs.
Germany shows us that we have to pursue a hard course of growth, with high savings and investment rates, moderation of consumption, improvement in productivity and competitiveness, and positive external balances.
The other alternative, based on indebtedness and construction with huge holes in the balance of payments, as cited by Spain in boom times, will simply not be tolerated.
The offer is clear.
And we're going to do badly about not understanding it.
It's not just about reducing the government deficit to three percent by 2013.
The demands are much harder.
And the worst thing we could do under these conditions is to deceive ourselves.
I hope the current turbulence will pass into more calm.
And that we will take advantage of the opportunities offered to us by spring 2013.
It seems Zapatero understood it that way, too, and his dual announcement (the promised reforms after the meeting with the big companies last Saturday and the announcement of new measures from yesterday) points out that despite the criticism of Merkel, the hypothesis of creating order in-house has paved its way.
But the red line drawn by Germany is there, and it doesn't look like anything would change.
Among the possibilities considered, it cannot be ruled out that we would have to leave the euro area, which is fully filled with little serious countries.
In this case, Germany could still tell us it had warned us.
Sports and circumvention
Tricks such as those of Alonso and Ramos in the game against the Ajax show inventiveness, but not the will to circumvent a sanction.
The exclusion allegedly instigated by two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) in the match of November 23 against Ajax in the Champions League caused a lot of ink to be shed, whether it was an unsportsmanlike act or not and whether both players should possibly be sanctioned by UEFA.
As is generally known, the two players lost time a few minutes before the end of the game (her team had already clearly won) and played several fouls so that the referee showed them the second yellow card.
In this way, and since Real Madrid would lead his group, the two players will meet the prescribed sanction in the next league game against the Auxerre and thus enter the next phase of the game without cards.
Finall, UEFA's disciplinary committee penalised the club's coach, José Mourinho, with two games and the players involved with varying amounts of money.
Provoking a yellow card under such or similar circumstances is not a new practice, it is more commonly found in the world of football.
It is so widespread that it is part of the ethos - the unwrited but followed rules in this sport.
Part of the debate surrounding the depicted case goes back to the fact that a team from the football club Real Madrid (who, in his anthem, pretends noblemoth and honor and on other occasions had punished his players for similar incidents) played the lead role in the matter, and on the dramatization by Mourinho, who added an episode more to his long history as a theatrical and provocative technician.
In any case, the debate revolves around whether this behavior, which is not expressly prohibited by the statutes, contradicts the principles of sport, that is, whether these actions constitute a circumvention. In fact, this procedure adheres to the statutes of sport, but seem to circumvent its meaning.
On this matter, the damage caused to the sports spirit should be investigated.
The sporting spirit defends the physical integrity of the opponents, an aspect that was not jeopardized by these actions, because it was not an aggressive game.
Another aspect of a clean game is to avoid the inflicting players getting an unjustified benefit from the counter-team. Even that didn’t happen.
The paradox of the action of Real Madrid's players is that it does not correspond to what we normally mean by misplay or bypassion of rules, because bypassion of the rule has not been carried out secretly in order to avoid sanction, but it should be as conspicuous as possible so that it is punished by the referee.
Players waited and accepted the sanction gleefully.
Another question is what kind of advantage the infringers pulled out of the intentional fouls.
It should be emphasized that the rival was neither put in a more unfavorable position nor benefits for the game played, because the obvious result of this action was that his own team had to continue playing with fewer players.
It’s also not a useful strategy for the next game, because the club doesn’t have these players.
The advantage is obviously that the players start the next phase of the Champions League without cards that could threaten their participation in a future game.
But it is a long-term, up to a certain degree uncertain advantage, as the team could also retire at some point. In addition, the players with this approach receive a sanction and move into the second card cycle, in which they excrete less than in the first cycle due to card accumulation.
So it's not so clear whether it's really an advantage.
Ultimately, such practices are not directed against the spirit of sports, which also includes strategic decisions, in order to derive maximum benefit from the regulations.
In this case, the players did not want to circumvent a sanction (that would actually be a clear case of circumvention), they were more likely to choose when to undergo it.
They made a strategic decision without dodging or fleeing from a penalty.
Although the line between inventiveness and regulatory environment is blurred, I believe that this matter is a clear case of inventiveness.
I have the impression that the polemics arose as a result of two factors: because it was about two self-excretions and because of the type of provoked rule transmission.
The harm inflicted on the principles of sport by these actions is less ethical than rather aesthetic.
Offer offensive for customer capture for group Christmas dinners in Barcelona
The calendar's dictatorship this year drops an avalanche of reservations to the days of December 16 and 17.
The celebrations paid for by employees themselves are spreading. They don't want to spend more than 20 to 30 euros.
Like the Turrón and the Christmas lottery, there are group dinners in the pre-Christmas period, which supply oxygen to the restaurant and leisure industry of Barcelona. But as a result of the economic crisis, they trigger a price offensive in order to also capture the customer segment, which represents the large mass: Almost everyone wants to spend between 20 and 30 euros for a lunch or dinner, confirm the entrepreneurs and associations surveyed.
In order for the accounting to become even more complicated, the calendar (New Year's Eve falls on a Friday) has dropped all orders into the week of December 13, with the result of an avalanche of reservations from dinner to two peak tagers (the 16th and 17th) and almost no orders the week before.
Since 2008, when the bad times began, many companies have ceased group dinners for budgetary reasons.
In order not to have to give up on this tradition, many employees pay them out of their own pocket.
No matter what the economy is, lowest prices are demanded, confirms Gaietà Farràs, chairman of Barcelona's restaurant committee.
The representative of the Umbrellan Association dares not to predict until now what the holidays will look like this month.
"People think about it 'twexty times before reserving.'
"They'd like to run out, but they're paying great attention to prices," he insists.
The business will go well with those "offering quality at good prices with a good service," he adds, and reminds us that customers save in times of crisis, which is why prices around 25 euros are most in demand.
In a survey in various centrally located restaurants, it turns out that the evening of December 17 leads the providers to collapse.
Group Soteras says the demands for this Friday with the intention of spending less than 30 euros would be booming. The group has adapted its menus with complete offers of between 25 and 35 euros to the economic situation.
At another leading group, Andilana, which maintains 16 hospitality venues in the city (Hostal de la Rita, Market, La Fonda, and others), the peculiarity of the calendar has lead to early reservations, even from the beginning of November.
"We made the impossible possible to maintain the previous year's prices with menus for every taste of 19.95 to 30 euros," he explains.
At AN Grup, which El Mussol, Attic, QuQu and a total of 23 venues, one does not lose optimism, after revenues have blossomed again since the second quarter and good, albeit a few days of concentrated prospects for the Christmas business.
Sources from the group of companies emphasize that even the 18 will be an overactive day with groups of friends celebrating Christmas.
In addition to the six group menus, a Christmas menu has been added for 30 euros or more as a price hit. One has the impression that customers are willing to spend 10 percent more than in the previous year and, in return, want more details, such as Cava.
Also in some companies you can see green sprouts sprouting, which is why the employees are invited again. This gives the impression that the group dinners are back on the upswing.
On the part of the Fecasarm Umbrella Association (with mergers such as the pub, restaurant and leisure association of Barcelona), the vice-president, Enric Gomà, trusts to see the end of the tunnel and notices that the reservations are quite "livable" overall, even though customers "smash out a maximum."
However, the fun of partying has not declined, no matter who pays.
The Pakistani nuclear program raises great concern in the U.S
Islamabad manufactures nuclear weapons "faster than any other country."
WikiLeaks' revelations confirm relations between the Taliban and Pakistan.
The content of the correspondence between the North American Embassy in Pakistan and the U.S
On the verge of clarifying and juicy details, which the documents about the contents of the talks and the mindset of the accredited North American diplomats in Islamabad, they reveal that cooperation between the both countries in terms of nuclear security will not be strengthened, but on the contrary "decreases," and that the unstable Asian country produces more nuclear weapons "to a faster rhytmus than any other country."
In particular, the peculiarity of Pakistan's nuclear program - that the weapons aren't assembled, but their items are stored to make their destruction more difficult in a possible attack - make Washington sleepless nights.
Anne W. Patterson, ambassador to Pakistan, in a reporting ahead of a visit by the former head of the National Security Council, James Jones: "The proximity of some nuclear facilities to the territory attacked by the Taliban, the presumptive scattering of the nuclear material and the vulnerability to the transport of nuclear material."
The large number of people implicated in the nuclear arsenals and the missile programs is no longer seen only by the U.S., but also by Russia as sprawling and reason to exercise caution.
"Pakistan has to hire strictly devout personnel to guard the nuclear facilities; due to the low level of education and culture, extremist organizations have more opportunities to recruite people involved in nuclear or missile programs," expert Yuri Korolev of the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a meeting with his North American counterparts.
New to the documentary revealed by Julian Assange's internet portal, cooperation between the U.S. and Pakistan in terms of nuclear proliferation is waning and the increased pacemony at which Pakistan manufactures nuclear weapons.
In May, an office of the embassy, which describes communication with Pakistan as "frustrating," declares: "Often there is no transparency; we don't get any responses to offers of help or they are rejected."
In another, Peter Lavoy, state intelligence official for South Asia, confirms that "despite the economic catastrophe, Pakistan produces nuclear weapons as quickly as no other country."
Ambassador Patterson asks Washington "clarified" during a visit by Supreme Army Director General Ashfaq Kayani that "Pakistan's army must cease its tacit support of terrorist groups."
Hunting for WikiLeak's boss
Obama appoints a high person in charge of the anti-terrorism struggle to prevent new revelations.
Interpol issues a red warning on Assange's persecution and discovery.
Everything related to Wikileaks and its founder Jullian Assange forms a complicated labyrinth, in which the facts can be enlightened difficultly.
Yesterday Interpol issued a "red warning" to "detection and persecution" of Assange.
However, this is not a traditional order for arrest.
Besides, she's not new, she's from November 20.
It also has nothing to do with the last disclosure of over four-million diplomatic documents from the U.S., but with the allegations of sexual abuse that the Australian must face in Sweden.
Furthermore, WikiLeak's lawyer Mark Stephens assures "the police would know where and how to make contact with Mr. Assange."
Already on the 20th, the international police authority, based in Lyon (France), issued the order at the request of Sweden, but it was not announced until yesterday after receipt of the authority by the Swedish authorities.
That's how things lie. The 188 Interpol countries are instructed to identify and locate Assange "to arrest and extradite him provisionally."
Although Interpol cannot order the arrest of an individual affected by a red warning, according to the organization, many member countries regard the red warning as a free pass for arrest - which must be carried out by the national police - especially if they have a bilateral extradition treaty, as is the case of Sweden and England, where Assange presumably resides.
The crackdown outraged Assange's lawyers, who gesten published a letter informing that the 39-year-old founder of WikiLeaks is willing to answer Swedish authorities' questions via video conference.
They are also reminiscent of Assange requesting departure from Sweden and received the appropriate approval from the public prosecutor, who is compared to the fabled Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria, security chief of Stalin.
"This is like hunting, not like a legal pursuit," the letter said.
While the world seeks him - and his mother claims her son's innocencence from Australia - Assange is staying in an undisclosed place, but not in silence.
On Monday, he gave an interview to the head of Time magazine via Skype from an indefinite location. During the conversation, he requested the resignation of U.S. secretary of state, Hillary Clinton.
"If it can be proven that she was responsible for ordering North American diplomats to spy on the U.N. and thus violating U.S.-signed international agreements, she would have to resign," he said.
His opinion was condemned yesterday by White House press secretary Robert Gibbs, who described the request for resignation as "ridiculous and absurd."
"I'm not sure why we're supposed to attach importance to a guy's opinion with a website," he told CNN.
Obama himself has tasked a high-in charge of anti-terrorism combat, Rusell Travers, with preventing new document revelations.
Attempt by Speaker Barack Obama's attempt to pay less attention to Assange contrasts with the opinions that conservative politicians and commentators are proclaiming these days.
Congressman Peter King, who will preside over the National Security Committee of the House of Deputies from January, has urged the state department to investigate the possibility of putting WikiLeaks on the list of terrorist organizations "to seize their funds and to pursue anyone who supports them or make a contribution."
Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian President Stephen Harper, had gone even further, and he had to retract his statement that Assange must be "murdered."
"Obama would have to turn him into a target and use an unmanned plane or something."
Whoever didn't apologise was Mike Huckabee, Baptist minister, former governor of Arkansas and possible presidential candidate.
"Whoever, whoever leaked information in the government, he're guilty of treason and all that's not an execution is too lenient," he told Fox.
Puigcercós seeks peace in the ERC with a twinkle in his eye to the Carod
The Republican leader allows the party a secret vote on its continued existence in office.
Huguet proposes a more pluralistic government and the end of the General Assembly principle.
Joan Puigcercós appears to have found a recipe to avoid for the tenty time an internal turmoil in the ranks of the directionless ERC after the loss of 11 deputies and half the electoral votes.
The party chairman is aware that his resignation threat is not enough for the loved ones of former party leader Josep Lluís Carod-Rovira. But he also knows that the influence of this sectorage today is as weakened as their mentor himself and will not give him the same headache as other former currents.
That's where Esquerra's leader is trying to win the new critics with a twinkle in his eye, which would secure his position until the next local elections.
For the beginning, yesterday announced that ERC's over 200 regional councillors could make a secret statement about his whereabouts in office.
With ballot papers, not with an upstained hand.
If the carodists wanted to give the president a ticket, they could do so without having to fear reprisals.
In fact, the boss wants to avoid both the impression of division and that of subjugation under his person.
In several interviews, he reiterated he would feel unvinciated with half of the votes plus one, and he added, without any indication of percentages, a result "in the Bulgarian style" wouldn't be good, either.
Carod-affiliated people are demanding more from the president than just making his office available to the party. An insufficient gesture as Puigcercós controls the supreme organ of the congresses.
But since they don't have a sufficient majority to promote resignations, they're going to agree with the board a procedural plan from now until May.
For this, they expect to be able to take part in the preparation of the document on strategic reorientation so that the head will submit to the Council for a vote.
Their intention is to vouch for Puigcercós. In return, he is to promise to move the congress forward to the autumn of 2011, which the party leadership already considers a fact.
The party chairman of the ERC is of the opinion that the "polyphony" harms the stability and he strives once and for all to enable the organization to wash its dirty laundry behind closed doors.
In this sense, the article signed by incumbent conseller Josep Huguet on his blog is symptomatic.
In view of what Puigcercós is an ideologue, the owner of Innovació considered it a fact that the party chairman would be backed "in exchange for various goals."
He listed five, two of which are in the foreground.
Firstly, the "restructuring" of the board so that it represents at least 90 percent of party members, i.e
The second could be the big news.
Huguet proposes that ERC should finally abandon the general assembly principle as functioning.
In doing so, he advocates a reform of the statutes, which should make it possible to hold congresses with elected delegates.
Puigcercós's team, which was already campaigning at the last two congresses, is obsessed by the termination of the general assembly model - an exception in the Spanish party system.
In fact, at the quaking meeting of 2008, the party leadership was tasked with reviewing the general assembly principle at a congress that was supposed to deal with reforming the statutes.
This date is still pending, as infogle did not make it advisable to tackle this matter in the internal restlessness of recent years.
Now, without the critical sector lying lurking, the time may have come to take up this matter again and conclude it forever.
23 Murdered in November, 290 in 2010
According to MILENIO's recount, four fewer cases were recorded in México than in 2010.
In November, 23 people were murdered in México, according to MILENIO México's recounts, that's four fewer than the previous month.
Among the murdered are three women and the find of two amputated bodies in the valley of Mexico.
In the penultimate month of the year, we reach a total of 390 deaths, which are probably related to organized crime.
Among the cases, one stands out in San Diego de los Padres Cuexcontitlán, a place in Toluca.
The subject had at least three shots caused by firearms, the mercy shot was delivered to his head and feet and hands were handcuffed with a cable.
The next day, the body of a person was found in the trunk of a vehicle parked on Mixteca's streak in Santa Cruz Acatlán of Naucalpan municipality.
On Thursday's 4th, farmworkers found a body in the early morning with traces of torture and at least three defiant imprisonments in the rib cage on the Tenango-Ixtapan de la Sal highway on the section that crosses Villa Guerrero.
In addition to the body, a message presumably signed by the crimulian organization La Familia Michoacana was found.
A day later, a 50-year-old man with eight gunshots was murdered after leaving his house to do his job as a rubbish driver.
On November seven, two 30-year-old people died as a result of bullets to the head in the settlement of San Pedro Xaloxtoc of Ecatepec.
In Chalcro, the corpse of a man with the shot of mercy in his forehead was found on a solitary path in the settlement of El Naranjo and under the body a bullet casing shot down with a caliber firearm.
In Ecatepec, parts of a human body were discovered in the sewerage sewer of Playa Golondrina.
The emergency service searched for the remaining body parts, but did not find them.
Tepotzotlán residents notified of the discovery of a corpse they had discovered in Peña Colorada's area. The hands were tied together to the back and the head was connected.
The man had a wound that stemmed from the shot of a firearm into the neck.
In the middle of the month, it was reported from the murder of two men wrapped in blankets and discovered in the trunk of a vehicle parked on the streets of Villas de la Hacienda of Tizapán de Zaragoza. In addition to the victims, two death threats were also found.
A day later, in a cardboard box, a human head was found in the settlement of Valle de los Reyes in the village of Los Reyes La Paz.
In a nearby settlement, the remaining body was found in a black plastic sack with a message signed by El Incorregible.
On the 17.1, two dead women shrouded in blankets were discovered in Zoquiapan settlement of the municipality of Ixtapaluca and, the next morning in Huixquilucan, the corpse of a man wrapped in a yellow-and-blue blanket was found, who, according to the news left by his killers beside him, was probably called Mario Ríos.
In Ixtapaluca's San Francisco Acuautla settlement, the lifeless body of a woman was fought with shots from firearms in her neck.
On the woman's skin, tattoos of Santa Muerte as well as chains with corresponding illustrations were discovered.
In the last half of the month, in addition to a vehicle, the corpse of a man with at least three bullets and a message signed by a criminal group was found in Zinacantepec.
Two days later, the bodies of two men whose heads were covered in tape and a message were discovered in the Puente de Piedra community in Santiago Tepatlaxco.
Opposite the body was probably a message signed by a criminal organization that read: "This happens to those who don't participate."
In Ecatepec, two bodies handcuffed on their hands and feet were found with traces of torture in the interior of a taxi parked behind the San Agustín settlement's judicial centre.
On the last day of the month, the bodies of two men were discovered in the communities of Los Reyes La Paz and Nezahualcóyotl.
The first had been beheaded in the settlement of Cuchilla Ancón and constricted in black plastic bags, beside a message. The second had been tortured and was found in a plastic bag in the streets of Juárez settlement.
Next to him, the police found a cardboard with a message.
China maintains its greenhouse gas reduction targets
China's representative stated at COP16 that his country had made enormous efforts to reduce carbon footprints and that it would maintain its supply to reduce 40 to 45 percent of its greenhouse gases by 2020.
China maintains its offer to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases by 40 to 45 percent by 2020, assures Su Wei, this country's representative at COP16.
At his first public appearance at the 16th United Nations World Climate Summit (COP16), Su Wei assured that his country had made enormous efforts to reduce carbon traces and change its industry.
He explained that in the last four years, his country has invested 245 million dollars to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases.
On the other hand, he explained that China would not change its goals, but would work together with the international community on this matter.
He explained that China's climate change strategy is divided into four aspects: dialogue, capacity building, institution-building and supporting pilot programmes.
Progress has been made in all aspects, including the adoption of a new energy law last year, which includes the targets for the reduction of emissions of gases, he said.
On the other hand, he emphasized that in China the authorities of the states had cooperated with those of the central state and the municipalities in order to achieve the targets for energy saving, effective use of energy and the reduction of emissions.
In addition, he argued that a number of multi-year plans have already been embarked on or have been completed, both those financed by the Chinese government and those who received funds from other countries.
For example, he was referring to the programme of effective use of energy introduced over the last five years and had received investment by the United Nations (UN), the government, and from private hands.
There is also a provincial-level project on climate change, another to replace the traditional light bulbs through energy-saving bulbs, and another for mitigation and adaptation, he explained.
Martin Krol, scientific government adviser on climate change in this country, confirmed that China’s efforts would be far greater than commonly assumed.
He pointed out that the per capita emissions of greenhouse gases in China were six tons per person, while in the United States it was 100 tons. China is responsible for a nine percent total contribution to environmental pollution.
It is also making unprecedented advances in the field of energy use. Energy effectiveness has been increased by twenty percent in the last five years and another is to happen by 2020, he explained.
Espino: César Nava
The national leader of the PAN declared that the former panistic party chairman had the right to turn to the National Ordinary Commission and to demand what the right was entitled to him.
The national leader of the PAN, César Nava V�ázquez, asserted that the exclusion Manuel Espiníon Barrientos had been legal and that the former party chairman of the PAN had the right to turn to the National Ordinance Commission and demand what the right granted.
In an interview held prior to complaint Gabino Cué Monteagudos as governor of the state, he explained the exclusion of Espino was a decision that had been made at the local level.
"This is an independent decision of the Commission of Ordinance in Sonora."
"Mr Espino has the right, in his case, to contact the Comisión de Orden del Consejo Nacional in order for a decision to be taken by a competent body," he added.
On the other hand, he explained, it was not time to think about 2012 and that an alliance with the presidential candidate had never sat at the negotiating table.
Nava Vázquez was convinced that Acción Nacional would emerge stronger from the internal vote next 4 and 5 December, at which his successor will be elected.
"This (an alliance in 2012) is an issue that has never been on the negotiating table, at least not until now. It has been a proposal for the municipal election, it has been successful and served its purpose," he said.
The panistic leader explained that the PAN looks back on 71 years of dialogue, that's why "we don't scare internal competition; on the contrary, "we're enjoying it and it strengthens us," he commented.
He said that the council would no doubt select the or best presidential Acción Nacional needs for the next three years. That's where he'd hand over the squadron "to the one that the council would determine."
"I'm unconcerned and open. I'd be proud to confer the office of the party chairman of each of the five."
He predicted that the upsurgence of the party, recorded in the last elections of July 4, would be reflected in the next election, although "it's no time to think about 2012."
"Now it's about assisting Governor Gabino Cué in his task."
In this context, he emphasized that the three governments emerging from alliances between Oaxaca, Puebla and Sinaloa would carry the flag and be observed across the country. That's why "many hopes and longings are placed into them and I'm sure Gabino is at the level of demands."
When he is questioned about PRI's criticism of President Felipe Calderón about his discus course as part of his four-year-old government, César Nava called on PRI's supporters to reflect on former presidential presidents Ernesto Zedillo and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
"If the PRI believes the governments of Zedillo and Salinas were better than those of Fox and Calderón, should they start defending them, they should finally come out of the closet," he reiterated.
He is of the opinion that the owner of the federal government expressions such as those he can make in his discourse in Auditorio Nacional, because in this country there would be no ban on mouth and even less censorship.
Obamia is campaigning for the law in favor of undocumented students
"If you're in favor of passing the Dream Act law, then call your senators and push for the passage of this law," the president of the United States tweeted on the popular social network.
President-elect Barack Obama today backed nationwide mobilization in support of the Dream Act law on legalizing undocumented students with a message on his personal blog on the social network Twitter.
"If you're in favor of passing the Dream Act law, then call your senators and push for the passage of this bill," Obama tweeted.
His short message includes a cyber link to a broader document calling on the public to subliminately contact their senators, as voting of the law is imminent.
"With the voice we're waiting for, it's time to listen," he insists.
The news was circulated after the 42 Republican senators threatened to blain all votes for any law, except the expansion of tax reductions from Bush's times and government funding would be expanded.
Such a blockade announced on a letter signed by the 42 Republican senators could have an impact on voting the Dream Act law and other projects such as ratifying the new START agreement with Russia against the proliferation of nuclear weapons.
Asked about the blockade of Republicans from the Dream Act law and other projects, presidential spokesman Robert Gibbs said he trusts that once the tax issue has been resolved, a schedule for the remaining projects could be set up.
"I think we've got a lot of time..."
"We all agree that the tax matter must be settled before the end of the year..."
"I believe that we can make an agreement about this at any time and after that we can plan the expiration of the rest," he said.
Senate Democratic Majority Chairman Harry Reid announced on Tuesday "he'd request the vote at the end of the debate" so that the Dream Act law, with which the initiative to legalize undocumented students could be voted on in the Senate starting next Thursday, although next week would be more likely.
The version of the Dream Act or the Law of the American Dream would legalize approximately 800 000 young people immigrating to the United States when they weren’t sixteen years old while living in that country for the past five years and being enrolled at a higher education institution or the army.
But advocates of the law need sixty votes to pass the initiative, which is why they rely on the votes of a handful of Republicans. At the moment, the advocates of this project organize an intensive influence on the passage of the law.
Democrats, the majority of whom support the Dream Act, have 58 seats.
However, one of them, Senator Ben Nelson of Nebraska, said he would vote against it and only one Republican, Richard Lugar of Indiana, has announced his support for the law.
That's whhere Dream Act defenders will have to get several Republicans on their side if other Democrats choose to turn their backs on the initiative.
Among the most courted Republicans are those from Maine, Olympia Snowe and Susan Collins, as well as Senator George Lemieux of Florida, who assumed the mandate of Senator Mel Martínez.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch from Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchinson of Texas, but they said they no longer supported it on another occasion.
Another Republican who voted in favour of the bill, John McCain, has so far failed to set his stance.
Even though the House of Representatives has a different version, the analysts agree that the Dream Act in the Senate faces the biggest obstacles.
Put your finger in the wound with literature
The second round of 'The Lust for Language' was a walk in which the participants campaigned for the narrative
"That with sex in the language I understand yes, but... The finger in the wound? What is expected of me?" asked writer Luisa Valenzuela, who was supposed to open the second round of 'Los placeres de la lengua', a cycle that celebrated Spanish-language literature and used the subtitle of her question on this occasion (at another eventing, the round table was dedicated to sex in the language).
The finger in the wound... "brings us into trouble," the Argentinian spewed sweetly towards the audience composed mainly of young people coming together at the International Book Fair in Guadalajara (FIL) to attend this round table moderated by Marisol Shultz, where Valenzuela, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Montero and Luis
Luisa Valenzuela read a text dedicated to Gonzalo Celorio, which Shultz characterized as a great narrative afterwards. Luisa recounts in the first person and is the protagonist who is looking to learn what is a “wound,” and goes from doctor to doctor, from whom she may not receive an answer, but receives hints for her narrative’s guide.
The infectologist leaves her convinced: "Patience is not my strength, maybe that's my wound."
Finally, after a frustrating approach to the traumatologist, who robbed her in the middle of the aisle, but who explains to her that wound was not a scientific term, the psychoanalyst revealed the secret: "the wound is the truly open, the symbolic is the scar."
The main character of the narrative sighs and concludes that she "only learned from the wound that it is really open and does not exist". But she is convinced that the lust for the language and its traps mix.
Valenzuela, the writer, played with both.
The Nicaraguan Sergio Ramírez also presented an adorable narrative, which he called 'Praise of Invention', and in which the finger in the wound is not mentioned, but discovered.
With allusions to magical realism, he tells of a famous sorceress who touched the clouds, brought back well-traveled men or restored the virtue lost by a girl. The narrative made a U-turn and reported on America, the great novel that was not lacking in its own chronists from the outset, and he cited Bernal Díaz del Castillo,
"Cervantes, who invited all of us (the writers), mocked the feigned witness who appeared in the stories portrayed as true, but in any other way the history of America would never have been told - with imagination that even lost the feeling of shame."
However, he emphasizes, "it's the lunatics and liars who imagine things best," and that's where reality and lies approach each other, where you no longer know which side the narrative belongs, where fictional literature reaches the peak.
He actually calls this the 'Tradition Cervantes'.
“We inherited from him to be what’s being told.”
When it was Mayra Montero's turn, the writer commented and praised the variety of interpretations to which the FIl's invitation had contributed to 'The Finger in the Wound.'
The Cubanian and Puerto Rican dedicated her contribution to the great wound of social violence afflicting her country - like the whole of Latin America - as a result of the struggles for drug trafficking.
"I believe that good literature, good poetry, good songs contain a tacit, non-deliberately planned engagement," she explained after highlighting that literature is communication and commitmense that build up between the author and the readers as in a "chemical reaction."
The evening was decided by the Spanish poet Luis García Montero, whose contribution also highlighted "this finger of the dedicated writer in the wound of the writer".
He noted that in a fast-paced world, intellectual habits and the control of consciousness also result in dogmatisms, and is forgotten "that behind every confirmation there is a little no and behind every deniancement a little yes."
"To be in possession of our thoughts and of reality, we have to nuance: And nuancing often means putting the finger in the wound and harassing those who feel much more comfortable when thinking in the form of caricatures, in black and white, in pre-written ideas without nuances, and also to harass the mighty, who prefer to divide the world into black and white
Romano fights for his first title
Rubén Omar Romano looks back on three defeats in endgames, he has experienced ups and downs in his career, but three times he played decisive games.
In the duel between Santos Laguna and Monterrey at the end, there are also two different styles dictated by their respective coaches, and their encounter characterized by contrasts is reflected in the results.
Rubén Omar Romano looks back on three defeats in finals, he has experienced ups and downs in his career, but three times he has had decisive games, and so far he has held himself well, which is why this opportunity is of very special importance to him.
The Argentine has played his way up to the medal for the second champion, he has been one step behind the victors and surprisingly he has played successive finals with the same team.
The santistic strategist achieved the first final as a coach at 'Torneo Apertura 2002', when del Morelia had the reins in his hand, with which he experienced a major defeat against Toluca with 4-2; later, in the 'Clausura 2003' he achieved the decisive duel, which he also lost to Rayados with 3-1.
Rubén had to take a setback again with Santos Laguna on "Torneo Bicentenario 2010", in which they played against Los Diablos, because the game with 2-2 against Los Choriceros went out after 210 minutes of match undecided, but in the penalty he came forward with 3-1 and got two opportunities to pocket his title, but ultimately three of his players missed the eleven steps
Rubén had already lost two finals with the Diablos Rojos and one with Monterrey, his great rival. That's why he doesn't want to be haunted again by misfortune and free himself from the nasty spell to be able to celebrate a title for the first time, even though he reaffirms that for him the most important thing is the game itself.
"I want to become champion, I don't want to break a statistic, that's for a club, for my affection, and that's why the title will come to me, but what happens before is the most important thing," the helmsman commented as questioning him about the lost endgames.
The last endgame has remained strongly in his memory, he assures to have learned a lot from it: "The only lesson we can draw from it is that until the referee doesn't whistle the end, we can't celebrate, we're prepared at the line, the players too, because the last time we've been already prepared with two penalties, and I believe that's a lesson
Knowing that this series could be decided by penalty shoot-outs does not worry him, because he trusts his players: "You never want to arrive at the penalty shoot-out, you want to finish in the regular season, but sometimes there is no alternative and you have to be prepared for it, in the previous tour we were prepared, but unfortunately the fate gave us a bad game, sometimes as a result of nervousness
It's power in Romanso to no longer be just second champion and for the first time taste the fame that the titles bring.
U.S
People must envisage visa application services at the consulate prior to their interview; this shortens the costs of waiting times.
From January 10, 2011, the procedures to apply for a visa in the United States embassy and consulates will change.
In the new procedure, most applicants will have to envision visa application (ASC, the initial letters) before their conversation at the consulate.
ASC's staff will record the applicant's biometric data, which will be reviewed by the consulate prior to the interview.
The new method has certain advantages for the aspirants, including:
The total costs for the processing of the visa decrease, because it is no longer necessary to pay the appointment, the processing of the visa application and the transport service with which the document is handed out separately.
The applicant makes only a single payment covering the three services.
The current price remains unchanged: the equivalent of 140 dollars for a tourist visa, 150 dollars for visas with application (including visas for temporary workers) and 390 dollars for hands and investors.
Those who only want to extend their visa do not have to have a conversation at the consulate, they only have to introduce themselves at the nearest ASC, where they present their documents and their fingerprints are taken.
Since the biometric data are obtained in the ASC, the applicants, who need a second appointment for the conversation in the consulate, remain there less than before.
Those who apply for the visa in Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo consulates will no longer have to pay the service surcharge of 26 dollars.
Additional information about the new visa application procedure can be found in the following website: http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201_Visas-FAQs.html
NASA Announces the Find of Extraterrestrial Life
The North American Space Agency convened a press conference to present a find tomorrow that will have an impact on the search for living things in space.
The National Aerospace Agency (NASA) of the United States announces an astrobiological discovery tomorrow, Thursday, which could confirm the theory of existence of extraterrestrial life.
The find will be unveiled tomorrow at 3 p.m
When convening the press conference, NASA assured that it would present "a prudential find for the search for extraterrestrial life," sparking speculation among experts from other countries.
The discovery could prove the theory that in hostile environments previously considered uninhabitable, human-like creatures could exist. It was raised the hypothesis that these could turn into intellest living beings—just like humans—when their living conditions improved.
Apparently, it's a microbe found at the bottom of Mono Lake in Yosemite National Park, California. It lives there in an environment that has been mistaken too poisonous to all-imaginable life forms, since the lake is heavily arsenic.
In any way, the microbie uses arsenic as a resource of survival. Taking into account this ability, the chances increase that there could be similar life on other planets that do not have our protective atmosphere.
If these organisms metabolize the arsenic, according to the astrobiologists, it is proven that "there is actually a different form of life, a second form of life that differs from what is known to us."
The space agency announces the actual range of its find tomorrow, on Thursday, at the press conference.
Geobiologist Pamela Conrad is attending the conference, which examines the possibility of life on Mars, and biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists who studies Titan (Saturn's largest moon) on the question of whether its chemical composition resembles that of Earth.
Gesta PRD, PT, Convergencia and Patrón form an opposition bloc
With the reform of the organ law over Congress, the two-party system will be introduced, they lament.
The 60th Legislature had just begun, and an opposition bloc had already been installed, led by MPs from PRD, PT, Convergencia and Jesús Manuel Patrón Montalvo, who envisioned himself as an independent. The four questioned the following: the entry of the 'two-party system' into the chamber.
Before the introductory session into the 60th legislative session, Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez of Convergencia, Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT, Javier Corvera Quevedo of the PRD and Patrón Montalvo argued against the reform of the organ law, as they would be excluded.
With this reform, they reiterated, a 'two-party system' would be introduced, because now it would be two people, the same number as parties that controlled Congress.
These four deputies, along with Gloria Margarita Santos Aguilar, coordinator of the PRD, were the only ones who voted against the bureau led by panist Salvador López Brito and Luis Antonio Cárdenas Fonseca in his capacity as vice-president.
Corvera Quevedo stated that with these reforms, the PRI and PAN parties had institutionally introduced their primary position.
He explained that in the next few days they would talk to the representatives of PAN and PRI to change the scheme. In doing so, they assumed their political will and if they didn’t, they would be subjugated by both parties, putting an end to the opposition coalition.
"Up to this point I (the Opposition Coalition), this is what I see, because we are excluded from all decisions, they do not even take into account the opinion of the rest."
Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez of Convergencia explained that there were correspondences with PRD, PT and Patrón and that a pluralistic group would therefore be formed.
It cannot be allowed, he continued that after being monopolistic, Congress should now be "duopolistic."
"It is not the spirit of legislative reformancement that a perpetual two-party system should prevail in Congress."
"For the beginning, this group begins with five deputies and they talk to other colleagues so that they become more," the Convergencia member explained.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT, alluding to PAN and PRI, stated that Congress has currently turned into a 'loot' for two.
"Before, it was just a party, now they're splitting it in pairs."
Patrón Montalvo requested to respect the deputies and assign them their place at the negotiating tables.
"It must not be left out because of top-of-the-art decisions that are likely to be nine MPs. The five, who are currently seen as divided into different factions, have chosen to unite to take our place at the negotiating table, because, because because of reform carried out by the others, we have stayed outside and we do not agree with this reform."
200 000 more peso for "the supplier"
Mazatlán city government is paying another cheque to the company's controversial owner for electrical supplies.
Another cheque about just over 200 000 pesos in favour of Lorena Guadalupe Camacho Palazuelos has been issued by the city administration.
Thus, the sum, which was paid in the second half of the year to the owner of the company for electrical materials, amounts to 1 200 000 pesos.
According to the page for more transparency of the city administration, this cheque issued to an amount of 200,144.56 peso with the number 9258447 was issued last 16 November with the intended use "Billing of outstanding invoices".
Treasurer Luis Miguel López Moreno had already sent forward that more money would go from the municipality to this supplier. She is observed by Auditoría Superior del Estado because she sells at inflated prices.
What hasn’t been mentioned is the total amount still owed to it and, as a result, the number of cheques still issued in favour of Camacho Palazuelos.
"Nothing has been bought from her, it's just paid the debt, just as with the rest of suppliers," he said in an interview.
The last cheques "dived up" "again" in the last quarter of the year: 24 September, two from 15 October, 19 October, 29 October and this last from 16 November.
Although the supplier has not yet been published in the Official Journal, as required by law if a company is questioned by the ASE in order to prevent other administrations from requesting their services, the treasurer assured that their services would not be used again.
After the newspaper Noroeste had published on July 25 the examination of cheques issued by the city administration to its suppliers, it was announced that previously, from March 2008 to July 9, 2010 during the three-year tenure of the mayor Jorge Abel López Sánchez, the municipality had already paid over 10 million pesos with 42 cheques to Camacho Palazuelos.
There were also shops with Jumapam, where she collected cheques worth over 3 million pesos.
Tourist places must be improved urgently
Tourist companies regret the accident of the foreign tourist due to the poor condition of the Glorieta Sánchez Taboada in Mazatlán.
After the fall of a foreign tourist into the break-in of Glorieta Sánchez Taboada, Mazatlán's tourism industry applied to the authorities to pay special attention to the areas that are in poor condition and cast a bad picture on the port city.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, chairman of Zona Dorada's merchant association, recalled that on various occasions it was called upon to take care of the destination and to turn the bad conditions the tourists find into new employment opportunities or routes.
He said the poor state of the roads and sidewalks had had a negative effect, as the tourists came to stroll and sew the clutter, whereupon they would turn their backs on the nearby shops.
"Politicians don't know what it means to have to close a company one day and they don't worry about how much money you lose. Because otherwise they wouldn't carry out the construction work slowly and unplanned in this way."
Hotel owner José Ramón Manguart Sánchez said the tourist's overthrown had drawn attention to the city's lack of prevention and lack of upkeering.
"The community has to assume its responsibilities and avoid that something like this happens again. How it's supposed to do that? Well, through pre-emptive maintenance instead of fixing everything in retrospect," he explained.
"We are eagerly waiting to have a clean city. Because not only because it is a tourist area, it has to be maintained, also we residents have a right to a clean habitat. The city administration has to change its strategy to maintain the city in good condition."
"There are other places that also look unkeen and unfinished," he said, "el Escudo y el Venadito of Mazatlán."
"Today we need more than just advertisements to attract tourists. There has to be a good product offered, a city that cleans its beaches and roads, and keeps the tourist attractions constantly going," he added.
"Not enough attention has been paid to these precautions and these kinds of incidents expose what is evident to all: maintenance is urgently needed."
Raúl Llera Martínez, under-secretary of Promoción Turística, expressed how important it was to save Mazatlán's touristic places, because they were not used properly and created a bad image.
He mentioned a number of places that would have to be brought to the fore together with the city. Designing the city clean, tidy and attractive for tourism should be a priority.
"Fortunately, Mazatlán offers many historical and cultural sites that would first have to be cleaned up and cleaned in order to make them attractive for tourism and supply them to the inventory with which we advertise this destination.
IP calls for results from Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, chairman of the CCE, suggested that MPs should assume the great responsibility to set a role model for a better Sinaloa facing many challenges.
Mazatlán's corporate industry is calling for a dignified parliament, free from collusion between the parties and the government, because Sinaloa is agitated and faces major problems.
Yesterday the 60th parliament was inaugurated. It is composed of seven political forces, 19 deputies of the PRI, 13 of PAN, three by Partido Nueva Alianza, two by PRD, one by PT, one by Convergencia and another one by PVEM. That makes a total of 40 people's representatives.
The new members of the State Congress are expected to do a clean job, to be committed to society and the different social groups that rely on responsible parliamentary work, and that the same patterns of partisance and paternalism will not be repeated.
Eduardo Dávalos, chairman of the CCE business coordination council, suggested that the new MEPs should assume the great responsibility to set a role model for a better Sinaloa facing many challenges.
"We hope that MPs will really exercise their right to act free from collusion between the parties, because it is customary for them to reach agreements with which they represent their own interests, transforming themselves into carnival comparises of the leaders and the leaders of the parliamentary factions."
He said that they should demonstrate their commitment to building the necessary bridges to support the current government.
"We take care that they carry out a trustworthy job and, above all, stand up for society and its groups, because it was the inhabitants of Sinaloa who expressed their confidence in them in the last elections."
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, chairman of the Tres Islas hotel association, thinks one of the great challenges faced by the 40 representatives of the people is the drive and joint work for the economic growth of the state, because nowadays the economic situation is extremely complicated.
"The most important thing is that they drive economic growth, create investment incentives and infrastructure measures, which in some way will encourage job creation."
With regard to the tourism industry, he demanded from the new MEPs that, in order to promote, they should assign the necessary importance to the Ministry of Tourism in the state budget.
"I have understood that it is the ministry with the second fewest means. This is incompatible with an important economic activity that brings foreign exchange and investment into the country and is in direct competition with the agro-industry."
Funds must be allocated to tourist projects, state security must be supported and the alcohol law, which has not been recast in previous legislative periods, must be updated.
Holy King is wanted
Dolls, cars, bicycles and other toys the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family are eagerly desired.
It will be difficult for the parents to fulfill these desires on the next Epiphany Day, because the household is composed no more and no less than twelve people and a goat.
Nevertheless, Adelaida, Isabel, Jesús, José Alfredo, Mar�ía Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not give up the hope that Melchior, Caspar and Balthasar will give them the toys and the garments they so long for.
The family inhabits a modest house in the settlement of Salvador Allende. They live from hand to mouth, because the father has no permanent work.
"Sometimes hard times are called because my husband doesn't have a job. But we're selling mandarins and potatoes... Already in the afternoon I'm going on the street with the brats to sell," Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, the mother of the little ones, said.
Four-year-old Adelaida would like a doll or a bicycle, while her three-year-old sister Isabel wants a Barbie doll.
Ten-year-old José Alfredo has been wanting to have a 'monster wagon' for some time and for his eight-month-old brother Jesús, Cayetana proposes one-year-old clothes for one-year-olds.
(José Alfredo) told me, 'I'd love to have this monster,' but I answered him, 'let's wait, boy,' because it was very expensive," Cayetana recalled sadly.
For the older children who were in school, clothes and shoes would be ideal.
Eleven-year-old María Guadalupe is size 12; ten-year-old Isaías size 10, while eight-year-old Miguel is size 8.
14-year-old José Juan is missing tennis shoes. A pair in size 5 1/2 or 6 would be very helpful.
If you would like to help realize the dreams of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family, you can participate in the 'Holy King is wanted' campaign by donating a toy or cash in the reception hall of Noroeste newspaper.
14 suspected drug dealers arrested
On Wednesday, as part of the break-up of a production and sales network of cannabis around ten house searches in the Estria administrative region were carried out.
Fourteen people have been arrested and will face various charges.
The house searches took place in Sherbrooke, Orford and Stoke, in the places that are said to have mainly served to manufacture drugs.
"Approximately 750 cannabis plants, four kilos of hashish, cash and equipment used for production have been confiscated," Louis-Philippe Ruel, spokesman for Quebec security police.
A total of eleven men and three women were arrested, two of the men believed to be the main wire pullers of the drug ring from Sherbrooke.
The persons arrested are said to have appeared in front of the Sherbrooke judicial building hours after their arrest.
Among other things, they could be accused of the production of cannabis, its possessions for sales purposes, the plot and the service of crimes.
These house searches had taken place as a result of a lengthy investigative activity, which took place for over a year.
"The information received from a publicity has enabled us to carry out these arrests," M. Ruel clarified.
Around 70 police officers from the Estria Regional Investigative Unit of the Quebec Security Police, the Sherbrooke Police Department and the Memphrémagog Police Department had taken part in this police operation.
The secret immigration could easily be pursued
According to the government, the Conservatives’ bill, which, according to the government, is intended to address the problem of ships loaded with clandestine immigrants, is expected to be stamped after the three opposition parties announced on Wednesday that they cannot endorse the proposal.
“I’m leaning strongly against this charter.”
"I'm sticking with a party that's put shackles on your feet," Michael Ignatieff, head of the liberals, declared in front of the assembled MPs after the weekly caucus session expired.
The liberals claimed that the bill, which allows every person identified by a seagoing vessel to be identified as an illegal immigrant trade for up to one year, violated the Canadian Constitution of Law and Liberty as proposed.
Gilles Duceppe, head of Bloc Québécois, also hinted that he would not be able to support the bill because he opposes the wrong target group.
"We are against it and it seems that all opposition parties are against it, so we're going to wait and see what the government does," explained M. Duceppe.
Criticism of Olivia Chow's immigration issue, NDP, has dismissed critics demanding that her party was not behaving steadfastly enough towards the secret immigrants.
"The neo-Democrats agree to take action against the blind passengers, and that's exactly what we're trying to enforce by brokering another bill, the C-35," Chow said.
We are therefore not in favour of the Conservative bill because it seeks guilt among the refugees and keeps children captive for at least a year," Chow explained.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney accused the opposition parties of playing with politics and ignoring the will of Canadians.
"The Canadians urgently inform MPs that they do not tolerate this kind of secret imigration, these criminal events that affect Canada and that treat the country like a foothalker."
"They expect Canada to take tough action," according to Minister Kenney's reaction.
"The government hasn't announced any vote of confidence regarding its bill that could have led the country to elections that the government would have rejected.
However, one Conservative has expressed to the QMI agency that the government would convene a vote so that every opposing MP immediately declares himself vis-à-vis his constituents.
The new unity finds no unanimity
Despite the announcement of the possible establishment of a new standing unity to fight corruption and collusion on the part of Jean Charest, numerous observers continue to call for the formation of a public committee of inquiry on this issue.
Robert Dutil, minister of public security, claims that this idea did not come from yesterday: Quebec had already consulted the New York Department of Investigation on this issue last January.
Since 1870, the metropolitance has had a standing unit against corruption.
Still, Wednesday's announcement has surprised more than just one.
It must be said that this measure was not discussed in the slightest on the occasion of the meeting of the standing committee of the Quebec police service on 29 October last year.
There was also no lack of Stéphane Bergeron, critic and member of the Parti Québécois, qualifying this announcement in terms of public safety as a "rabbit conjured out of the hat" as well as "rightly crafted solution at the end of the table."
Minister Dutil, in turn, had reached out to those who fear that political power had too much influence on future unity in question.
"They're going to see the result when we're going to announce it," he said
"You're aware of this problem."
According to Réjean Parent, the president of the CSQ, nothing can replace the conduct of a public survey.
"The more M. Charest insists on trying to prevent the formation of this investigative committee, the more people like you and I think he has something to hide," he explained.
"The only measure to form an investigative committee is the resignation of Jean Charest," said Yves Boisvert, lecturer in ethics and political systems at the ENA (state university for the training of senior administrative officials).
In order to get out of this impasse, he proposes to give Quebec a special mandate for financial audit control, so that it can carry out corresponding investigations on corruption and secret collusion.
"One should be able to extend the mandate of audit control," he decided.
He also argues that Quebec could give examination control the same power as commissioners, which would also allow it to require people to testify.
The formation of a standing unit is not the only project in the drawers of the Charest government.
Quebec will also require Ottawa to amend the penal law in order to be better attuned to these kinds of crimes.
This demand stems from the Chief Prosecutor of Operation Marteau, who has informed Justice Minister Jean-Marc Fournier that the jurisprudence is inconclusive in this type of prosecution.
Vic Toews, Minister of State for Public Security, for his part, confirmed that his colleague from the judiciary, Rob Nicholson, is currently dealing with certain amendments concerning this dossier.
During Operation Printemps in 2001, which affected the groups of motorcycle police officers, 131 people had been arrested, even though the penal law had not been amended.
As a result, Ottawa enforced gangsterism law.
Flours under strict surveillance
Several rivers remain under strict guard due to the high rainflates that have spilled out in some regions of the province since Tuesday.
A landslide occurred in Shawinigan, in the Mauricie administrative region on Wednesday afternoon.
Two houses were evacuated preventively.
In the evening, another landslide occurred in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, in the Lanaudiere administrative region.
A road has collapsed into itself.
From Tuesday to Wednesday evening, heavy rains of 40 to 70 milliliters of rain have poured into several regions.
Between 20 and 40 more millimeters of precipitation were expected here according to the regions by Thursday evening.
Areas north of the river as well as the Outaouais region's valley were most affected.
A total of up to 100 millimeters of rain showers had descended here.
Since the snowmelt, which has already accumulated in certain sub-areas, is to be added to these precipitations, the transfer of several watercourses is feared.
On Wednesday evening, warnings about the flood alarm concerning the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in the Lanaudiere region were proclaimed by the Quebec security service.
The river courses in the north, in Saint-Jérôme, in the Laurentine mountains and the Chêne in Lotbinière, become the object of flood surveillance here and remain under surveillance.
In the Lanaudière region, the heavy rainfall has led to the crossing of some lakes in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare as well as in Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, an evasive bridge was torn away due to the heavy rains.
Ten residencies had to be evacuated as a preventive measure.
In Outaouais, crossing certain lakes has caused the closure of several roads, particularly in the municipality of Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
The security service remains on alert and monitors the situation closely, according to security councillor Annik Bouchard.
For its second, the Quebec State Flooding Centre on Wednesday issued warnings of the sharp rise of several rivers, in particular of Maskinongé, Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and Loup.
Dupont partly rebuked
Yequean radio host Stéphane Dupont has suffered a partial setbance in front of the Canadian Supervisory Board for Broadcasting Standards (CCNR) because of his controversial comments about social welfare and Haitians, as well as because of his eye-catching speech via radio.
The CCNR argues that M. Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team have the right to express their own opinions, but certain statements contained "inaccurate information about social welfare and uneligibly negative representations of the Haitians, as well as such an unrugging manner of speech, which is not appropriate to the broadcasts broadcast during the day."
The Supervisory Board had also concluded that a discussion of suicide, which includes the dissemination of the names of two persons who committed suicide, violates the code of broadcasting.
The CCNR had received several complaints relating to the comments made during the shipments broadcast during the 2008 and 2010 period.
The first injunction was lodged as a result of a complaint by the "Front commun des personnes assistées sociales du Québec".
The activist organization had complained that the amounts mentioned in the broadcast were inaccurate.
The moderators should rather give solid than misleading information.
With regard to the topic of suicide, moderator Stéphane Dupont had argued that suicide was an expression of cowardice.
The majority of the members of the Supervisory Board had drawn the conclusion that the mediated message is that suicide should not be regarded as a solution to life crises.
"The majority of the Supervisory Board takes into account that the possible consequences of the broadcast "Dupont le midi" would have addressed a sensitive topic in a family-friendly and appropriate manner."
Therefore, he was given no rebuke to his person.
The last point of contention was the statements that had referred to the earthquake in Haiti.
Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort had engaged in a media-effective dispute that provoked a lot of reactions.
The CHOI moderator had declared that he had not donated in favor of Haiti aid, since one did not know with certainty whether those in need would really receive the monetary donations.
The Supervisory Board is of the opinion that these comments were non-abusive in character, but that their cumulative effect presented an unjustifiably negative representation of Haitians.
"I've already lost my three-day suspension sentence on this matter."
"My file has been javenilar for a year and a half," the presenter explained.
In all resolutions, the Supervisory Board denounces the dissemination of an unravaged mode of expression.
In all cases, the CHOI-FM acted radio announcer "beyond its obligations."
The consequences for the radio station, which, among other things, was supposed to communicate its decisions once during the prime period within a period of three days, are minor.
A King of Kung-Fu in Montreal
Nacereddine Zemmal, of Algerian Montreal origin, has embarked on a very special course.
The athlete, who excels in "Sanda" (full contact Kung-Fu), in kickboxing as well as Thai boxing, is now ascending in search of new challenges to Tatami and into the boxing rings of the world.
"I started martial arts such as kung fu and kickboxing in Algeria 12 years ago," explained Zemmal, who has been based in the metropolitan since 2008 to develop among the professionals.
Personally, I'm looking for the fight everywhere, because for those who don't like the ground combat like the mixed martial arts, competitions in North America are rare."
Recently, the one working of his sign as a security guard as well as employed by UPS had given bad news to his rival, in which he won two gold medals from the World Karate and the Kickboxing Association (WKA) at the World Championship of Amateurs held between 23 and 30 October in Edinburgh, Scotland.
"I've won victory in the categories of full-contact kickboxing and Thai boxing," he explained.
"Currently, I'm getting ready for the World Cup at San Shou in China."
"It had been invited those fighters from each category who had placed themselves among the top four at the last World Cup."
"I won bronze in Toronto in 2009."
"I finished eighth in Vietnam in 2005."
"In 2007, I finished the tournament in fifth place in China, while I finished third in Toronto in 2009," he added.
"It's worth noting that I've progressed in every competition."
"With this rhythm I'm going to be first this time or next year!"
"San Shou" or "Sanda" are disciplines originating from China in which fist bumps and footsteps as well as ground throws are permissible.
The duels usually take place on a platform, less often in a boxing ring.
The most renowned box fighter who emerged from this type of martial art is Cung Le, who is well known in the martial artiary.
If Nacer Zemmal only retained a single event in his career, he would certainly name his presence at the "King of Sanda" super tournament here.
"This competition is the highest-ranking and respected in China," the sympathetic poundhead explained.
"It's a bit like the equivalent to the Lumpinee stadium (the largest stadium in Thailand)."
“It was a super experience.”
Zemmal had won his first fight in the semi-finals of his category (80kg) before losing in the final.
"The one who defeated me was a real professional," he said.
"There are four competitors per category (80 kg, 85 kg, 90 kg and 95 kg)."
"The winners of the semi-finals each meet in the finals of their category."
"Then the victors of each category face each other to crown a single champion, the king of kung fu."
450 million in treaties excluding tenders
Hydro-Québec, by mutual agreement, has granted project planning firms 450 million dollars in contracts for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydropower project.
In the middle of this avalanche of more than 100 contracts stands a consortium consisting of the companies BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor, which has received the most important share, namely that of the project management, to a sum of 295 million dollars without tender.
This consortium, called "Énergie gérance," was formed in 2004 as a companion of Hydro-Québec's decision to the private sector's responsibility to hand over this important hydro-electric project in the amount of 5 billion dollars.
At the beginning of 2005, the "Énergie gérance" consortium obtained the lucrative treaty without a public tender.
For Parti Québécois, resorting to corporate consortia poses a serious ethical problem that evokes the risk of competitive displacing and increasing prices.
"This is a new way in a mapped-out game and invalidates the competition."
"This means that instead of mapped-out games instead of a secret way, you are now carrying out in open form," Sylvain Simard, a member of parliament from PQ, said at the press conference yesterday.
In the early morning, Simard had raised the question before the National Assembly, because it seems difficult for him to understand how the interest of the public can be served in this way.
Resource Minister Nathalie Normandeau had reacted in the afternoon by vehemently denying that this contract would have been a mapped-out game.
"I remain true to my line," she squealed, confirming that the PQ had acted "dishonestly" and "lacked hardship."
She said it "was the least" the PQ apologised.
The Consortium's model is common practice worldwide.
It is usually such a temporary alliance that allows two or more companies to pool their expertise with regard to a bid for a tender for a complex project.
The "Eastmain" project has a high level of complexity with regard to its level of work.
The construction process is underway and is expected to be set up by 2012.
Nevertheless, the contract was awarded without any competition having taken place; there had already been negotiations.
In addition, the consortium’s model in Quebec is by no means an exception, even for relatively simple projects.
As Le Devoir newspaper noted last week, the consortium's model used in the civil engineering and consulting sector is the subject of government investigations.
A brief overfling of the Quebec business register allows more than 200 consortia to be named, some of which appear to be of constant duration.
In this way, for example, Dessau cooperates in partnership with the majority of its core competitors, including CIMA+, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin or Roche and Tecsult.
Any connection, of which there are several dozen, leads back to one and the same legal person, namely Dessau.
As far as "Énergie gérance" is concerned, Minister Normandeau has stressed that "the territory of Baie James is subject to a particular labour dynamic and that Hydro-Québec has been paying special attention to this fact since the 1970s".
Réal Laporte, the great employer of the Société d'énergie de la Baie James (SEBJ), has issued a statement that has ruffled over the fact that the contract for project management has been approved after agreement.
"If you make a tender and there are few applicants, your prizes will skyrocket."
"So you have two options: either you take the people who are on this mart and tell you: they will negotiate a satisfactory agreement at a good price."
"The thought, however, is that it is not the price that is decisive, but the expertise of the individuals," Laporte affirmed.
This contract granted to the "Énergie gérance" consortium is part of a lengthy list of treaties for more than 50,000 dollars received by Parti Québécois.
The contracts were approved either by Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
After review, Le Devoir newspaper counted more than 100 engineering and consultant contracts approved by companies or their branches, including resource laboratories in particular, between 2002 and March 2010.
SNC-Lavalin had garnered 22 contracts, Génivar had received 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, without including their participation in "Énergie gérance".
All of these contracts have been negotiated.
In total, they amount to $449 783 344, including the contract with "Énergie gérance" for $295 131 564.
Sylvain Simard had stated that the four companies that form the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) of the "Parti libéral du Québec" were well known.
Minister Normandeau had hinted that the PQ was equally connected with the companies that "financed" them, before clarifying that they were rather their executives.
Barometer of Responsible Consumption - The "green" label is non-confidential
Green, but unequivocally skeptical.
The Yequean consumer has little confidence in the main players in sustainable growth, but nor to the labeled brands or the eco-markings that are supposed to steer his choice.
It’s a pity, even though it’s bathing in a media-effective universe, where it’s teeming with information in favor of a more responsible consumption: the barometer of responsible consumption has a lack of credible information to lament, an inadequacy that identifies itself as the brake of its commitment, as evidenced by the first reading held yesterday.
Although they advanced the consumption of so-called socially acceptable products in 2010, Quebecers still seem to pay little credit to those who are trying to sell them.
Two-thirds of the Quebec population admits, in fact, not to believe the environmental commitment of the companies as well as green corporate advertising, reveals the measuring instrument elaborated by the responsible consumer center of the University of Sherbrooke.
Another finding: half of the consumers doubt the trustworthiness of the official certificates attached to the supposedly fair products, but also to the credibility of the products and brands, which declare themselves to be environmentally conscious, according to the barometer.
According to the same pattern, the discourse of the politicians on this topic is taken seriously by only 14.6% of the Quebec population.
Nevertheless, the information regarding responsible consumption seems important for the stimulation of this consumer segment, which is currently revived by almost one-third of Quebec consumers.
In fact, emptiness and dubious nature of information dampened the zeal of 46% of consumers.
According to the higher price of the products, this is the second brake identified by the barometer.
In addition, a third of Yevectors are suspicious of the stated performance of the green products and services they are trying to put on their hands.
According to another communication, more than half of consumers had already changed their habits in 2010 and predominantly changed the brand of one or more products to express their conviction.
According to the barometer, they had also favored 61% purchases from merchants in their neighborhood and favoured the purchase of food grown in their region.
Greener and more responsible - the index of eco-confident consumption this year had reached a 64 of the maximum value of 100 - Quebec's Homo consumus loves to present itself as the king of recyclation, trying to reduce its traces left in the environment by taking care of animal welfare.
But the social and ecological consciousness still seems to stop in front of its car, according to the barometer: just 28% of Quebecers stated that they had used public transport in 2010 or preferred the carpooling center to demonstrate their ecological interestence.
The walking as well as cycling were also endorsed by 39.1% of the participants of this comprehensive behavioural survey feeding the clichèèèes.
Government bond to acquire eXcentris
The Quebec government, which has been urged by Société Angus as well as Parallèle to participate financially in the takeover of the eXcentris cinema complex, agrees to cooperate, but only in the form of a competitive bond.
The development company Angus as well as Parallèle, who had hoped for a donation of seven million dollars from the Ministry of Culture to take over Daniel Langloi’s eXcentris cinema complex on Boulevard Saint-Laurent in order to hand it over again to its actual cineastic function, apparently does not receive what they have demanded.
Although the government has accepted to involve itself in this matter, on its own terms.
Instead of the expected donation, the Quebec government offers a bond in less than the initially hoped-for amount, which could launch a viable solution on advantageous terms, at least according to the sources removable from the state rumour mill.
An initial action plan, as well as a request for 12.8 million, had already been rejected by Quebec last spring, and the request has been corrected downwards in total since then.
A proposal together with an action plan has already been sent by the President of the "Société de développement des entreprises culturelles" (SODEC), François Macerola, to Minister St-Pierre, who heads the eXcentris file.
Several days ago he had sent her one last factual report.
However, the cabinet's counterproposal was submitted to the SODEC on Wednesday.
Parallèle and Angus now have until December 31 to respond to it.
In any case, the premise of state donation seems to have really been pushed aside.
Let’s recall that the Quebec ownership association of cinemas and cinema complexes had opposed a direct state investment to support the acquisition of eXcentris by putting forward the argument that the Minister of Culture had to provide no financial aid for real estate projects there, which he did not also provide to other cinema halls.
However, several other market participants in the segment sued for substantive economic assistance.
Cinema boss Daniel Langlois had presented the author cinema on the premises of his cinema complex under great difficulty for more than ten years after the self-financed construction of the cinema, which could justify the exceptional position on the part of the state treasury.
The lack of cinema halls showing independent films is more than obvious in Montreal, and eXcentris knew how to keep his clientele at bay.
Société Angus as well as Parallèle with Christian Yaccarini at the head, in turn, are heading for a privately-funded Campagne, from which they are hoping for a total of $700 000 to boost the acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, only a single screen continues to show films in eXcentris, Le Parallèle.
The aim of the future acquirer is to reopen the three cinema halls on 27 February next year with the screening of the very successful film of Xavier Beauvois "Des hommes et des dieux".
Christian Yaccarini, however, did not recall the newspaper "Le Devoir" and one ignores what he and his group think of the downwardly corrected proposals they have been made.
Mystery of complex-shaken Canada revealed
The new U.S. diplomatic leaders, circulated by WikiLeaks, unravel the secret of what the United Nations deects beneath Canadians' inferiority complex over the Americans; however, with the same blow, they expose the latter's sense of superiority.
Consequently, American diplomats are astonished that the Canadian voter does not have Canadian-American relations in mind on the ballot, and they believe President Obama’s visit would have helped the Conservative administration to enforce its 2009 budget!
The four new messages between the U.S. embassy in Ottawa and Washington, publicly circumnavigated yesterday, provide even more piquant details than the mere substantive content you've got to nibble.
A connection dating from September 22, 2008, i.e. in the period in which the federal election campaign was in full swing, reports on which point the United Nations no longer participates in the deployments discussed by its political bosses.
The great astonishment of David Wilkins, the American ambassador appointed in Ottawa.
Mr Wilkins offers various explanations of this situation.
"Circumstanness-related complex of inferiorities to Canadians may cause Canadian, political leaders much more likely to talk about internal contentious issues of everyday life rather than to blame the United States in this election (as it did in the 1988 campaign on free trade)," he said.
The United Nations, he concludes, is thus "the proverbial Goliath of this election: omnipresent, but potentially too threatening, to speak of him."
The ambassador understands this "deafening silence" all the less, since the two countries have intense political and economic relations.
"Logically," he assesses, "it should be the ability of a candidate, a party, and even more of a party leader to lead such an essential relationship that the voter has as a key factor in mind when it comes to ballout."
The other expression of this feeling of importance reads in the message sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, business official in Ottawa, which President Barack Obama is to prepare for his first visit to Ottawa.
"Their journey will help ensure the continued existence of the government in the confidence vote about the federal budget in early February, during which Canada will announce its first deficit after more than a decade incurred due to a revitalization program amounting to 30 to 40 billion dollars," Breese describes.
Nowhere had he mentioned that the government had to adjourn the parliamentary session before Christmas in order to prevent parliament from being overthrown by the coalition and the so conditional persistence of the opposition parties would affect the quality of the proposed revival programme.
Another communication, dated January 2008 and drafted by Wilkin's ambassador, informs Washington of the increasingly negative image that the dramatic series broadcast via CBC are spreading from the United States.
The ambassador is troubled by broadcasts such as Border, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which one can observe unsubstance allusions to Maher Arar, the "no-fly-list" or even derivations for the purpose of interrogations carried out by muscle prouncers of the CIA.
Another communication reports in detail on how Hillary Clinten asked her French counterpart Bernard Kouchner at her first bilateral meeting whether Europe was prepared to accept certain prisoners from Guantanamo.
Mr Kouchner is said to have answered yes to the question, but on the basis of an individual "case-on-fall" decision.
À at the end of the meeting, "he handed the secretary of state a paper in terms of Omar Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin."
"The secretary of state has pledged to look through the file again."
Let’s finally note that the stimulus is heating up with regard to WikiLeaks website.
Tom Flanagan, an old and Stephen Harper's very close adviser, had stated via CBC that those responsible for this massive leak should be "killed."
He had referred to Mike Huckabee from the United States here.
Worldwide plan to renew lighting
The UN proposed yesterday in Cancun a plan to renew the lighting of households, commerce and industry in 100 countries with a view to achieving a reduction in greenhouse gross (CFCs) equivalent to nearly one-third of the additional reduction that needs to be realized to stabilise the Earth’s climate by 2020.
This ambitious plan occupies its position in the step of the concrete large-scale projects that the UN hopes to implement in Cancun in order to compensate for the absence of mandatory reduction commitments from industrialized countries.
According to the plan put forward yesterday, the main problem would be avoided in the form of the 1.8 billion tons (Gt) of the CFC discharged due to lighting to the earth if the white-glowing bulbs, which so far still represent a monopoly of 50 to 70% on the market, would be replaced by fluorescent or even better, by light-emitting diodes (LEDs).
Illumination is responsible for 8% of global emissions.
This corresponds to an equivalent of 70% of the exhaust gases of the worldwide fleet.
In Copenhagen last year, the major emitters, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, were committed to voluntary reduction plans, which were intended to reduce global CFC emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
However, one would still have to subtract 5 Gt from this record in order to stabilize the climate increase by 2 degrees Celsius from today until 2020, in order to prevent it from spiraling out of control.
In the white annealing bulbs, 95% of the energy escapes in the form of heat, and the remaining 5% produces the light.
With the compact phosphors, the energy consumption is reduced by 75%, with the new LED light bulbs, this energy requirement is reduced again by twice.
The compact phosphor pears last on average between 6000 and 10,000 hours.
The new LED light bulbs, such as those launched this week – this company has participated in the UN study, by the way – have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consume 80% less energy than a white-glowing pear, which conveys a hint of the savings possibilities of the exchange plan, which is to be applied in 100 countries.
In addition to the faction that these completely novel light bulbs release very little heat, they can also work with a control resistor.
And they don't contain any mercury.
Several specialists have shown that compact phosphor pears have reserves because they contain mercury and that they must therefore be disposed of as hazardous waste, which is not always possible in the developing countries, unless the producers provide an acceptance point.
According to the report, the main source of mercury emissions on the planet is not the compact phosphor bulb, but rather the coal-fired power plants that emit significantly more mercury in order to generate electricity wasted by the white annealing pears.
In the United States alone, these coal-fired power plants emit 25,000 tons of mercury annually.
The report sets out in detail the profits each country would generate thanks to this international programme.
Indonesia, for example, would save one billion dollars annually on the purchase of electricity and eight million tons of CO2, which means an equivalent of 3.5 coal-fired power plants, the edification of which Indonesia would cost 2.5 billions.
Ivory Coast excitedly awaits announcement of
UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has demanded the "immediately" publication of the results of Ivory Coast's presidential election immediately after its deadline.
"The Secretary-General underscores the need of the independent Electoral Commission to maintain its work and to announce the preliminary results without further deferral," according to a press spokesman for the U.N.
"It asks all Ivory Coast parties and institutions to demonstrate their accountability and abstain from any initiative until promulgating the preliminary results."
Despite the pressure exerted on the part of certain foreign governments, the Electoral Commission had passed the deadline, which had been set at midnight on Wednesday, three days after the election on Sunday, without publishing the results.
Approximately forty minutes prior to the deadline, the President of CEI had confirmed on television that the Commission would "continue with work" to announce the results.
On Wednesday, many calls from all over the world have become loud that the CEI should finally proclaim the name of the winner of the second round of the electoral under Laurent Gbabbo and his opponent Alassane Ouattara, who left office.
The Gbagbo camp, however, anticipates cancellation of "fraudulent" ballots in the north by the Constitutional Council, scored by the ex-rebel camp of the Forces nouvelles (FN) during the coup failed in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, head of Ivory's Ivory Coast presidential campaign, had confirmed that the Manoeuvers practiced by the FN to intimidate voters in the north of the country had deterred the president's supporters from going to vote.
The whole election could be annulled if the lawsuits filed by Gbagbo are taken into account by the Constitutional Council.
The Gbagbo counterparties, however, are now more of the view that the country's highest court is not neutral, as it is headed by Paul Yao N'Dré, a politically closely associated with Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, man of the North denying any connection to the ex-rebels, has judged the delay in promulgating the election results as "unacceptable," asking Gbagbo to respect them, even if he hasn't gone so far to claim victory.
His allies reaffirm Gbagbo blocked the publication of the results because he knew that he had lost; and filed a complaint before the Constitutional Court, after the Electoral Commission was prevented from publishing the results was illegal.
Sunday's second round of presidential elections were held for crowning the unification and reconciliation process of a country that had been divided into two for years.
The United Nations on Wednesday had warned Ivory Coast politicians that it could be held responsible for them in the event of possible riots.
Laurent Gbagbo had the first round of voting on October 31 with 38% of the votes against the 32% for Alassane Ouattara, who enjoyed the public support of former president Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round), as a top candidate behind him.
Unemployment rate in France remained stable in third quarter
Within the meaning of the International Labour Office (IAA), the unemployment rate in France is at a stable 9.3% (with regard to 9.7% of the overseas departments), according to the provisional, sea-adjusted data published on Thursday by the Federal Statistical Office.
Unemployment figure in the metropolis establishes itself at 2.631 million.
Data for the second quarter amounted to 9.3% of the working population in the metropolis and 9.7% with the involvement of overseas departments.
Over the year, the unemployment rate recorded an increase of 0.1 percentage points.
In detail, 3.4 million employees of the French metropolis were unemployed in the third quarter, but they wished to find a job, including whether they were operational every two weeks or not, or whether they were job-seeking or not.
The situation of young people in the labour market continued to deteriorate with an increase in the unemployment rate of 24.2% in the metropolitan (+ 0.9 percentage points within three months, +0.5 percentage points over the year) and 25% involving the overseas regions.
In the third quarter, 637,000 of youngsters between the ages of 15 and 24 were unemployed, according to the IAA.
Senior citizens, in turn, have seen a slight increase in the unemployment rate of 6.4% in the metropolis (+0.2 percentage points over three months as well as over the year), i.e. 482 000 of people over the age of 50, 6.6% involving overseas departments.
Only the 25-49-year-olds had been able to derive benefit from the stability of unemployment in the third quarter; 8.3% in the metropolis (-0.1 percentage point over three months, +0.1 percentage points over a year) and 8.8% in France overall.
After a dispute over his calculation methods, the Federal Statistical Office of Insee no longer publishes the monthly unemployment figures since 2006.
Eurostat, on the other hand, the statistical office of the European Union, continues in this method and on Tuesday announced a rate of 9.8% in France for October (including overseas departments) compared to 9.9% in September.
Alexande Guérini charged and arrested
An investigation proceedings have been launched against Alexandre Guérini, 53, head of a disposal company and brother of the President of the General Council of the Bouches-du-Rhône (PS) General Council, and he was taken to Luynes near Aix-en-Provence in an investigation proceedings concerning public and suspected defrauded markets.
Alexandre Guérini was indicted for embezzlement of company assets, embezzlement of general goods, concealment, complicity in corruption, illicit bribery and possession of weapons, according to his lawyer.
"Alexandre Guérini ready to fight and intends to comment on all the questions that the prosecutor's office will ask him instead of spreading fantasies."
"It's easy to insinuate him with a number of criminal charges, another thing is proving them," his lawyer Florence Rault told AFP (Agence France Press) ahead of his client's hearing.
Mr Guérini, brother of Jean-Noël Guérini, the first husband of the PS (Parti socialiste) in the department, had attached great importance to giving the judge a memoradum to his justification before his hearing by means of his lawyers.
A relative of Guérinis, Philippe Rapezzi, was also indicted and jailed on Wednesday evening.
According to lawyer Lucien Simon, her client had never done anything other than to keep his landfill, which he had to run.
With regard to the area of business competence or the objective of the associated funds, he would never have been aware, according to Simon.
In this affair, Daniel Pinna, former director-general of the Aubagne metropolitan area, was also indicted on Wednesday for embezzlement and complicity, but then released under the condition of staying away from the protagonists of these legal acts under judicial supervision.
"My client was able to provide explanations of the facts he was accused of."
"The lead prosecutor took into account not depriving him of his freedom, said his lawyer Yann Arnoux.
Four other persons had been charged and released from custody in the night from Tuesday to Wednesday, including two of them under legal supervision, according to Chief Prosecutor Michel Raffin.
Michel Karabadjakian, deputy managing director of the Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), is being prosecuted due to "unauthorised influence and passive bribery."
Two civil servants Aubagne, Michèle Duval and Christophe Bringuier, were also arrested, the one on the basis of the title "Evadication and embezzlement and embezzlement by a public service representative", the other for "complicity in embezzlement of public funds".
Alexandre Guérini's partner, Jeannie Peretti, was also indicted to "sanctuation and embezzlement of social assets."
Among the people facing Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, recently imprisoned due to a similar affaire of fraudulent machinations from the public purse that had affected Corsica.
Investigators suspect his society of having helped launder money originating from "high" criminal circles.
The investigation against X for alleged fraud in the award of public contracts, here in particular in the disposal branch, will be conducted in April 2009 as a result of anonymous letters in the files of several open investigative proceedings.
It is clear from sources of investigation that the investigation focuses in particular on the conditions of the surcharge as well as the operation of a landfill in La Ciotat, which also draws waste from the Aubagne area as well as from certain towns of the Marseille urban basin (MPM).
Affaire Clotilde Reiss: the Elysee Palace exaggerates its mediating function for Syria
Within more than six months, from July 2009 to May 2010, the fate of Clotilde Reiss, a young French explorer who was helded in Iran because of "espionage," kept the French public in breath, putting diplomacy on alert and accentuating the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
All of this under the observant eyes of United States diplomats.
The American documents obtained by WikiLeaks and reviewed by the newspaper "Le Monde" reveal some background of this crisis.
They describe how the Elysée had explained to the Americans his strategy, which went so far as to give this advice on how to deal with their own crisis of "hostages" in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show how the French officials tried to convince Washington that the liberation of the young Frenchwoman did not represent the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian who was imprisoned in France and wanted by the American judiciary due to trade in favor of Iran's nuclear program.
Finally, they put the way in which the French Bureau tried to manipulate the media for the purpose of making it believable that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad had played a central role in liberating Clotilde Reiss.
In truth, he had done nothing in that regard.
Redicated in Paris on August 12, 2009, a diplomatic telegram is titled "Iran: French Commentaries on the Way of Dealinging Hostage."
François Richier, Elysée adviser on strategy issues, chatted accompanied by Patrice Paoli, director of Quai d'Orsay for North Africa and the Middle Orith with American diplomat Kathleen Allegrone.
They explained the French method to her.
They advised the Americans to do everything they could to return their own nationals, who would be forcibly detained in Iran.
"Makes noise," they said, "articulates loudly and clearly and without ceasation" the words "right-of-land rights," "innocence," "immediately release" in the media.
This is necessary to embarrass the Iranian regime, which pays very sensitive attention to its image.
"Do this all the more, especially as the Iranians ask you not to do so."
Because "silencing does not solve anything", past the discussion attempts in an inconspicuous way, the Iranians are "masters in evasive manoeuvres".
Mr Richier adds that the arrest of foreigners is a "common Iranian tactic": "hostagism for the purpose of political extortion."
He announces the release of Clotilde Reiss from the prison (which was supposed to come about on August 16, the day she flees to the French embassy) "demand the payment of a large sum of money to the Iranians" (230,000 euros bail).
And he announces that the Elysée anticipates further hostage-taking, a phenomenon that "will intensify."
Iranians "could, in the near future, arrest another of the approximately 1,800 French citizens living in Iran," he said.
August 28, 2009.
Clotilde Reiss was released from prison twelve days ago, but continues to be held in Iran.
Information has been circulated: Iranian Majid Kakavand, whose expatriation the United States is demanding due to illegal trade in sensitive electronic material to Iran, has been released from custody after the decision of a judge in Paris.
The American embassy, like numerous other observers, faces questions about this disturbing coincidence.
For Washington, which prosecutes the illicit Iranian networks of acquiring the material earmarked for the nuclear program, the release of Majid Khakavand represents bad news.
Did France accept a cow trade?
The liberation of the Iranian against that of the Frenchwoman?
On the occasion of a renewed discussion with Kathleen Allegrone on 4 September, François Richier assures that this is not the case.
The Elysée, he says, was not only "completely surprised by the judge's order," but had also tried to rebel against it.
"There has been a lively exchange between the French Bureau and the French Minister of Justice" (currently Michèle Alliot-Marie), but "the French government cannot change the judicial decision," he explains.
Mr Richier would also like to point out that Majid Kakavand continues to be under "strict" oversight from French intelligence.
The Elysée consultant dials a telephone number, hangs up again, then informs the American diplomat that the Iranian "is currently located in the buildings run by the Iranian embassy in Paris".
During Clotilde Reis' custody in Iran, the Elysée does not spare his efforts to believe, by means of pronouncements and "escape attempts" organized by the media, that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad plays an "important" and "influential mediating role."
The Americans do not observe this activity without ironyism and state that the manipulation bears fruit, as several press articles reflect the official French version.
A telegram dating from August 25, 2009 conjures up "the media flash of French authorities."
What is based so much "thank-yoke testimony" towards Mr. Assad, the Paris message wonders.
"Despite their declarations full of praise, the French authorities secretly admit that they only have a vague idea of what the Syrians actually did."
The document then deals with the reasons that led the Elysée to forfeit this fiction.
Intensive communications efforts "aim to confirm Sarkozy's policy of opening up towards Syria," he analyses.
A policy launched in 2008 that at that time was "perceived by numerous critical voices in France as well as abroad as premature and undeserved."
"Exaggerating the Syrian role" Paris tries "to show Syria what commemoration you can achieve by playing a constructive role in the region."
The aim is "to sow discord between Syria and Iran."
The U.S. embassy knows this from good sources.
On August 11, François Richier confided to her: "Of course, we don't know if the Syrians have done anything."
"But we thanked them in spite of everything."
"This should at least cause confusion among the Iranians."
Clotilde Reiss will return to France on May 16, 2010.
Nicolas Sarkozy welcomes her at the Elysée.
In a communiqué, he thanked the presidents of Brazil, Senegal and Syria for their "active role in favour of the release of our compatriot."
No online games for young Koreans after midnight
The South Korean government will pass a law called "the Cinderella," which will forbid the youth to play online games after midnight by blocking their access, government officials indicated.
A bill designed to call on online game operators to deny youngsters under the age of 16 access to these games after midnight will be submitted to the parliament in December, representatives of ministries of culture and families said.
"Many children play these games all night long and then have difficulties with learning at school and also with living a normal life during the day."
"We are of the opinion that this law is necessary to ensure their health and their right to sleep," Jo Rin, a ministerial commissioner entrusted with this bill by the French press agency Agence France Presse, explained.
The online games are then open again from 6 a.m.
The manufacturing companies would have one year to prepare for the application of the law, he clarified.
The government is also envisaging to ask manufacturers to reduce the time limit granted daily for access to these games to those young people whose parents are asking to do so, the person responsible added.
He will also provide free programs to those people who wish, which make it possible to limit the time spent on the Internet.
South Korea is one of the most "networked" countries, and the newspapers regularly report on various events associated with intensive internet use.
The country is also undisputed champion in worldwide online game competitions.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals in the World Cyber Games compared to 58 other nations.
World Cup 2018-2022: FIFA appoints host country
In bright excitement due to suspicions of corruption, FIFA's executive committee on Thursday appoints the host countries of the 2018 and 2022, a highly political choice in which wrangling for influence as well as lobbying threaten to push the technical criteria into the background.
However, one thing is for sure: FIFA’s 2018 edition will take place in Europe, its traditional stronghold (the 4 candidates: England, Russia, Spain-Portugal, Netherlands-Belgium).
The 2022 edition will then be a little more exotic; the International Football Federation hopes to win new territories (Qatar, Australia) for itself after its premiere on the African continent in 2010, or to pursue its establishment in the United States or the Far East (Japan, South Korea).
The list of personalities belonging to the delegation (David Cameron, Prince William, José Luis Rodriguez Zapatero, José Socrates, Bill Clinton, the Emir of Qatar, the Belgian and Dutch head of government, Zidane, Beckham, Cruyff) sets out the measure of the economic and political commitment of a World Cup.
So behind-the-scenes machinations threaten to stop until the last minute.
Difficult otherwise to establish a hierarchy of candidates.
According to FIFA's evaluation report, the English, Spanish-Portuguese (2018) and American (2022) acts offer better arguments than those of their competitors.
But these considerations won't be as important to the 22 members of the Executifi Committee at hour of choice.
The Sunday Times, which was at the beginning of the revelations with the suspension of two members of the Executif Committee (Reynald Temarii and Amos Adamu), could just as well have ruined the chances for their own country, cradle of football, which was only allowed to hold the World Cup once, namely 1966.
The BBC had once again given this breeding ground on Monday by airing a documentary report that fires three influential members of the committee, namely Ricardo Teixeira, Nicolas Leoz and Issa Hayatou.
Reason enough to upset FIFA and embarrass English leaders, who had been lobbying intensively with David Cameron in those last few weeks.
The head of government hopes for a re-run of the chess move successful by his predecessor Tony Blair, who had scored the organization of the 2012 Olympic Games for London in 2005.
Would this intensive commitment suffice to upgrade England's image before the committee?
Nothing is less certain, because the duo Spain-Portugal do not lack trumps with their already finished stadiums, his expertise (World Cup 82 in Spain, Olympic Games 92 in Barcelona, European Championship 2004 in Portugal) and his football culture.
Yet the economic and financial downsides of the two countries could dampen their ambitions.
The possible reprisals against the English, as well as the doubts about solvency of Spain and Portugal, increase Russia’s chances, which have become a weighty outsider despite its gaps in the dossier (stadiums, hotels, transport).
Prime Minister Vladimir Putin, however, has decided denouncing the unequivocaled hint to the English press "an unfair competition in the preparation of the vote" on the part of the committee members.
"You're throwing them with dirt, you're trying to compromise them," Putin complained.
For 2022, the United States, boosted by the experience of 1994, seems to be the logical favorites.
First economic world power, seat of FIFA’s main sponsors, the country seems to have a long list of advantages over Japan and South Korea, which have set up a very beautiful project separately.
But these new candidacies seem premature for two countries that co-organized the 2002 competitions.
Another threat: FIFA's much-awaited approval of the 2026 World Cup for China, which makes Asian chances of 2022 lapse.
With Australia and Qatar, which had pulled out all stops by waiting for futuristic stadiums, FIFA is pursuing its expansion towards new areas, but these two countries pose a greater risk, because of certainty.
Australia, as well as Japan and South Korea, could suffer from the weakness of television rights accrued by European countries (due to the time difference), FIFA's sensitive nerve.
Second Mandate: Obviousness
De Gaulle had really stumped.
Mitterand had made him believe that he was stumping.
Valéry Gismard d'Estaing and Jacques Chirac showed not the slightest hesitation.
Nicolas Sarkozy even less than this one.
One mandate in the Elysée summons the next.
The current head of state ergo will be a candidate in 2012.
But obviousness doesn't look good on the side of the mystery attached to the presidential function.
