On November 15, Nicolas Sarkozy asserted in earnest tone to make his decision "sometimely in the fall of 2011."
The fact that he would hint at the perspective of "two mandates" on Tuesday evening at the Elysée in front of the UMP parliamentarians had therefore spread a little feverishness.
Was that the official announcement of his candidacy?
Far from it.
The president merely reminded that the 2008 constitutional amendment limited the number of five-year terms of office to just two.
He made a statement, no announcement.
It's not the first time he's mentioned the presidential election on the occasion of such meetings in a humorous tone.
"At worst, you have seven and a half years left with me, or at best," he explained to them in July 2009.
To a new in May 2010: "Don't think I'm thinking about the stage afterwards."
His words from the dawn don't make him a candidate more than the day before; but no less.
If they evoke comments, then for a double reason.
Accelerating the race for candidacy with the PS on one side.
However, it is not the inclusion of the competition with Ségolène Royal, which drives Nicolas Sarkozy to reveal herself.
On the contrary, it is his obvious candidacy in 2012, which many members of the PS admonish earlier than expected to fight.
The poor polls on the other side.
For the first time, the voters of the right would prefer a candidacy of François Fillon of Nicolas Sarkozy (46% against 44%, according to the BVA-L'Express).
"We have a candidate, and there is no discussion about it whatsoever with the UMP," responded immediately and cautiously the prime minister's environment.
"If he were to find defeat as possible, he wouldn't be a candidate," Jean-Pierre Raffarin shares.
"He will always believe in being able to win," corrects Alain Juppé, who has come to terms with obviousness.
Euro: the ECB is forced to maintain its purchases of government securities
Last year's meeting of the ECB's central bank council was supposed to provide an opportunity to point out ways to get out of the crisis, or at least announcing the order of a large number of unconventional monetary policy measures that are due to conclude at the end of the year.
The crisis of European public debt obliges, the central bank in Frankfurt undoubtedly cannot afford to forfeit even today's, more than tense situation in the European bond markets, so as not to evoke their aggravation.
The political costs would be far too high.
French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde welcomed the active role taken by the ECB in favour of resolving Europe's national budget debt at a press conference yesterday.
She did not dare to say whether she felt that the central bank could do even more, for example by boosting its programme of purchases of government bonds.
"I reserve my considerations for my European trading partners (...) ".
Jean-Claude Trichet, President of the ECB, had not shown himself to be very eloquent with regard to his plans the previous day in Brussels on the occasion of his hearing by the Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee of the European Parliament, but was rather pleased to point out that the programme of purchases of government securities would "continue to operate" on the secondary market and that we will "see how the decisions turn out" at the meeting on Thursday.
Only certainty: The ECB will keep its key interest rate unchanged at 1%, the historically lowest level since May 2009.
In practice, treasury bond purchases were doubled by the ECB last week to about 1.35 billion euros.
And according to indiscretions of some traders, she is said to have made new purchases of Irish and Portuguese government securities yesterday.
In total, the central banks of the Eurosystem have made purchases amounting to 67 billion euros since May 2010.
Among dealers, the anticipation of pursuing the purchase price is by no means questioned (see page 29).
Economists no longer expect a remarkable change of course.
If bond transactions of over three months have already disappeared from the ECB's arsenal, the latter will still face unrestricted cash reserves in a generous manner over a period of one week to three months.
Economists are already thinking that this measure will be extended by a few more months.
"I can't imagine that the ECB will change its programme in an essential way."
"Otherwise, it would take the risk of provoking new tensions in the short term," predicts Michael Schubert, Commerzbank's economist.
"A progressive phase-out planned for the course of the first quarter of 2011 seems more likely to me," says Norbert Bräms, an economist at Sal.
Oppenheim.
In the area of its interventions in the market of public debt, "the ECB "should maintain such a purchase volume of bonds that enlivence the sluggish market," adds Norbert Bräms.
Considering the relevance of the exposed position of European banks on the markets of public debt in the eurozone, the central bank has no choice but to maintain its presence in the market.
"It has to step at any price to the place of the lack of investors in the market and act in such a way in order to prevent rash enthusiasm about the returns paid to the weakest countries," a key bondholder testified yesterday.
Because if interest rates start at a treble, the states would be even more prevented from reducing their debt or even stabilizing them.
This is the price to protect the euro.
Berlusconi government takes cover in Italy
After a worrying start to the week for Italy, the situation eased a bit yesterday with the decline in the canine interest rates to 10 years.
A return to deceptive silence.
At the end of the meeting with the economy minister, the regional chairs Giulio Tremonti had qualified the situation as "indecipherable" and hearded an "unpredictable" weekend, which, however, immediately denied the person concerned.
On Tuesday, the scissors with the German interest rates would have reached its record of 210 basis points, and the prices of the contracts to cover the risk of bankruptcy had risen to 263 points, which had provoked a certain headlessness in Rome.
Silvio Berlusconi had pointed out at his Council of Ministers that Spain's discrepancy at German interest rates is 400 basis points.
His right-hand purse, Gianni Letta, however, had commented as "heavily concerned" by the "market turbulences" and a possible contagion danger from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the eurozone's third economy, isn't like Greece or Ireland."
"The bad news is that Italy is like Portugal," explains Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
In other words, his problem is the absence of economic growth and the concomitant, swindling tax inputs that put the state at risk of no longer being able to meet its financing needs.
The last Brussels forecasts were a cold shower for the government.
In the future, Italian growth of 1.1% is expected in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012... although the budget available at the Senate for examination expects 1.3% and 2% respectively.
Under these conditions, the EU Commission calculates, the budget deficit will still account for 4.3% of GDP next year (contrary to 3.9% predicted by governments) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
Hence the turmoil over a new budgetary collective, which could be used after the holidays to a sum of 7 billion euros.
"If necessary, new austerity measures must be taken," says auditor Olli Rehn.
At the hour, Italy receives the support of international institutions.
"The foundations of Italy's economy are fine and its accounts are regulated, we have been repeating this for a long time," assures Arrigo Sadun, Italian trustee of the International Monetary Fund, which is entrusted with Italy at the IMF.
"At the moment there are no problems, neither in the short-term nor in the long term, because Italy, despite its high debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, A. d. R.) requires lower stabilisation progress than from other countries," observes Pier Carlo Padoan, chief economist of the OECD.
Remains the question of political instestiation.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a motion of no confidence in the Chamber of Deputies, as a result of which the early elections could already be organized at the end of March.
Thus, there is an unfavorable period for a resolution regarding a serious crisis.
Europeans are considering expanding anti-crisis measures
Christine Lagarde emphasized it yesterday at a press conference in Paris: "I think it was proven on Sunday afternoon as well as in the evening: The Europeans clearly stick together, are willing and committed to defend their currency as well as their currency area".
Although perfectly relative - the deduction recorded yesterday in the interest rates of the European government bonds was water on the mills of the governments as well as the ECB.
However, there may not yet be talk of a start of normalization.
The rating agency Standard & Poor's had announced that it could reduce Portugal's debut note, arguably the most effective target of speculators today.
"Like in May, there is a real lack of clarity and political perceptibility, which weighs particularly on the banking systems," according to the opinion of the Economists of Exane BNP Paribas.
Above all, especially since "political time elapses much more slowly than that of the markets."
The European countries, which are well aware of both this gap and the danger in it, are intensifying the discussions to reinforce the existing anti-crisis measures.
The informal discussions between the most directly concerned finance ministers and in the broader sense with their counterparts of the G20 are proceeding well.
And also with regard to the meetings between Eurogroupe and Ecofin early next week.
The ECB is meeting today.
On the plan is the lot of unconventional measures to reinforce the countries of the eurozone in the crosshairs (see below).
As far as the EU Stability Funds are concerned, its Executive Director Klaus Regling already started his Asian tour in Singapour on Tuesday to inform investors in the Far East about developments regarding the crisis in Europe.
If the aim of his visit is not explicit fundraising, he certainly prepares the terrain for it.
The first securities expenditure on the part of the EU Stability Fund is envisaged for next month.
Yesterday, an official U.S. representative entrusted to the Reuters agency that the United States supported a possible increase in funds mobilized by the IMF's additional direct engagement.
Portugal and Spain, countries at the forefront, are in turning engaged in adopting structural reforms, particularly in the labour market organisation.
Reforms added to the austerity measures, but are intended to show effects in the near future.
Lisbon and Madrid take a different path than Greece and Ireland did.
Both one and the other is at any price to avoid having to solicit international assistance before setting up their own budget correctives and about inevitable structural reforms, which in the long term brings them back confidence.
European investors, in turn, are discussing the options available to speed up the normalisation process of government bond markets.
Axel Weber, who was in transit in Paris on 24 November, was the first to create the idea that if the 750 billion euros mobilised by Europe and the International Monetary Fund weren't enough, so the Union wouldn't hesitate to reach into its own pocket again.
Barclays Capital's economists, who are at the forefront of the market for European public debt, rule that an extension of around 100 billion euros would suffice, "preferably" in the form of a direct injection of liquidity.
This remedy exists: the European mechanism of endowed financial stabilisation, which currently amounts to 60 billion euros.
An advisory board that has all the chances to be closely examined in European capitals.
Especially if the market conditions worsen.
Snow and freezing ice: 24 departments in alarm level Orange
The snow episodes are still leading to traffic difficulties, especially in Britany's secondary transport networks, and threatens to impair various economic activities.
Twenty-four departments are now alerted due to the impending risk of snow and freezing ice.
The regions Eure-et-Loir, Deux-Sèvres and Vienne have been added to the 20 departments that are already on alert orange; 18 since Wednesday afternoon and two, the Dordogne and the Gironde, then later at night.
According to Météo France, the intense snow showers affect the north of the Gironde as well as the Charentes, it snows easily on the et Ile-de-France.
In the affected departments, snow covers of 2 to 5 cm are expected, sometimes from 7 to 10 cm, as well as 1 to 3 cm, even up to 5 cm in the Paris region.
Affected are Charente, Charente-Maritime, Côtes d'Armor, Finistère, Gironde, Dordogne, Poitou and the Basse-Normandie, Ile-de-France and Limousin regions.
The snowfalls are considerable enough to evoke traffic obstructions and affect certain economic activities, Météo France predicts.
In the Côtes d'Armor region, several regional roads are closed to heavy traffic of more than 7.5 tonnes, including the Guingamp-Lannion and Saint-Brieuc-Loudéac routes.
The traffic situation of the secondary transport networks in Brittany is very difficult, according to the traffic polices, who advise against using their own vehicle only in emergencies, and the use of some TER coaches has been cancelled throughout the day.
In Picardy, school traffic has been lifted for Thursday.
No specific restrictive measures were envisaged for heavy traffic on Wednesday evening.
The prefecture "urges motorists to exercise the utmost caution on the roads as well as to appropriately adjust their driving behaviour."
The Directorate-General for Civil Aviation (DGAC) urged airlines to cancel 25% of their flights departing from Paris's Roissy airport and 10% of flights departing from Orly on Thursday due to expected heavy snowfall.
DGAB recommends air passengers to contact their airline.
Gatwick airports in southern London, as well as Edinburgh's airport, Scotland, are closed until Thursday morning.
Métro Grand Paris: first financial sketches
Since the debate about the modernization of the transport industry on the Ile-de-France is in full swing, it has been raining billions.
At least in discussions.
In particular, we are recalling the 23 billions for the Metro Grand Paris, which is to be built by the Société du Grand Paris.
In order to initiate the start of construction of the SGP, the state has proclaimed a capital endowment of 4 billion euros.
In the theoretical scheme, this is the sum that the SGP would then allow to lend a portion of the financial sum to its two-fold in the markets.
Today there have finally been initial projections.
The supplementary budget of 2010, reviewed this morning by the financial commission of the National Assembly, contains the planned measures to raise the first part of these funds.
These are two measures: the creation of a special tax to access and increase the taxation of the offices at Ile-de France.
The former, according to Article 15 of the draft law, goes "in favour of the Société d du Grand Paris public institution".
"The product of this tax amounts to 117 million euros per year".
The latter is a reorganization of the already existing corporate taxation: "to modernize it in order to increase the product resulting from it and to transfer this increase to the Société du Grand Paris".
How much would that bring in?
It's a mystery.
This sum is not numbered in the text.
You're a long way away from that 4 billion.
"It is stated that the tax resources will be added to the 4 billion euros of the capital dotation raised by the state and the Société du Grand Paris will gradually be provided for the project," can be seen from the explanatory statement.
At SGP, this system isn’t bothered.
"Since the namount when you record revenue (the two taxes, i.e.), you can cover the planning over three years and then only draw from the 4 billions," it is explained.
"This is noted in the budget and deducted gradually."
The SGP estimates that 2013 will be started to record the 4 billion for the 2014 budget.
This measure, whose 4 promised billions can still be sought for a long time, runs the risk of evoking a rather moving, parliamentary discussion.
The first public meeting will take place on 7 December.
Vengeful hackers and busy spies
Affaire WikiLeaks has a double meaning.
On one side, it has turned out to be an obvious scandal, a scandal that is merely a scandal of hypocrisy that governs relations between the states, the citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it announces in-depth changes at the international level, and gives a foretaste of the future dominated by regression.
But let’s proceed in turn.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is the confirmation of the fact that every file created by an intelligence service (an arbitrary nation) is composed solely of newspaper clippings.
Berlusconi's "extraordinary," American revelations about the sexual habits of Berlusconi cast nothing further than what you've been read for months anyway in any x-arbitrary journal (except for those whose owner is Berlusconi), and Gaddafi's frighteningly caricatured profile has been providing cabaret artists with material for their sketches for a long time.
The rule, according to which the secret dossiers may only consist of already known news, is essential for the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
If you go to a library dedicated to esotheric publications, you will realize that every work (about the grail, mystery of Rennes-le-Chateau, the Templars or the Rosicrucians) repeats exactly what has already been written in previous works.
This is not only because the author of occult texts does not want to publish any research (never then that he did not know where news about the non-existing could be found), but because those who devote themselves to occultism believe in nothing other than what they already know and confirm what they have already learned.
This is also Dan Brown's mechanism of success.
Same with the secret files.
The informant is lazy, and lazy (or limited spirit) is also the head of the secret service (otherwise he could be whatever, for example, editor at Libération), who only considers true what he acknowledges.
The top-secret information about Berlusconi, which the American Embassy in Rome had sent to the State Department, were the same as the ones Newsweek had already published the week before.
So why was such a fuss made about the revelations of these dossiers?
On one side, they say what every educated person already knows, namely that the embassies - at least since the end of the Second World War and since the heads of government can also call each other or take a plane to meet for dinner - have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small representational tasks, have turned into espionage centers.
Any viewer of investigative films knows this very well, and it does not happen out of hypocrisy that one pretends to ignore this.
Still: the fact of repeating it publicly violates the task of hypocrisy and serves to put American diplomacy in a bad light.
In the second place: The idea that an arbitrary hacker can tack the most secret secrets of the world's most powerful land carries the significant blow against the prestige of the State Department.
In addition, the scandal puts less the casualties than the "snipers" in distress.
But let’s dedicate ourselves to the profound nature of what happened.
Formerly, in Orwell's time, you could bundle all power like a Big Brother that controlled every gesture of his subjects.
Orwell's prophecy has since turned out to be perfectly correct, where you can control every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction made, the visited hotel, the chosen highway and so on; the citizen would become the total victim of the eye of power.
But if one remembers what is happening today, that even the secret crypts can no longer escape the control of a hacker, then the assemblement ceases to be one-sided and becomes circular.
Power controls every citizen, but every citizen, or at least the hacker - chosen as an avenger of the citizen - can find out the secrets of power.
But how can a power that is no longer able to keep its own secrets?
It is true, Georg Simmel has already said that a real secret is an empty secret (and an empty secret can never be revealed); it is also true that any knowledge of the character of Berlusconi or Merkel is basically a secret free of secrets, since it reveals the public sphere; but in the style of WikiLeaks reveal that the secrets of Hillary Clinton were empty secrets means depriving them of any power
WikiLeaks did neither Sarkozy nor Merkel wrong, but Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this offense inflicted on such tremendous power?
It is obvious that states will no longer provide any reserved information online in the future - this would amount to a notice attached to the street corner.
But it is equally obvious that with the current technologies it is in vain to hope to be able to keep confidential reports over the phone a secret.
There's nothing easier than figuring out if and when a head of state moved on a plane and contacted one of his colleagues.
In the future, how could private relationships be maintained, only for certain ears?
I know very well that my prediction at the moment sounds like science-fiction and novelistic, but I feel compelled to introduce myself to the governing agents, moving in a secret manner on uncontrollable routes, hiding their messages nowhere other than in mere memory, and, moreover, hiding the little written information they have received.
The information would be stored in locked drawers in a single copy: basically, Watergate's espionage attempt had less success than WikiLeaks.
I've had the opportunity to write that the technology is progressing like a cancer these days, namely backwards.
A century after the wireless telegraph revolutionized communication, the Internet reconstructs a telegrave with (phone) wire.
The (analogical) video cassettes had made it possible for the film explorers to scout out a film band after band by being able to rewind from back to front and reveal all the secrets of the montage, while the (numerical) CDs nowadays allow nothing more than to jump from chapter to chapter, i.e. in macro portions.
High-speed trains run from Rome to Milan within three hours, during which this requires three and a half hours by plane, including changing trains.
It is therefore not exceptional that politics and communication technology come back on the car and the horse.
One last observation.
In earlier times, the press tried to understand what secrets were forged between messages.
At present, it is the embassies demanding confidential information from the press.
Climate: how France talks its financial promises nicely
One year after the Copenhagen Treaty, it is time to investigate compliance with the quick-resolved funding promises (also known as "fast start" or premature financing) realized by developing countries.
And already the rough French fine speeches are uncovered.
With the Copenhagen Treaty, developing countries have pledged to allocate 30 billion dollars between 2010 and 2012.
The EU has pledged 7.2 billion euros over three years, France 1.26 billion euros (i.e. 420 million per year).
On the occasion of COP16, the EU pulled out all the stops to convince its partners of the fact of their commitment regarding fast-start financing: detailed action report of their member states, press conferences, large side events, distribution of brochures.
Operation Transparency is remarkable and especially appreciated for its actual value: the developing countries and civil society would like to see all developed countries doing the same at the same level of precision.
The EU announces that it has released 2.2 billion euros in 2010, which puts it on the right path to the right way to pay respect for its commitment of 7.2 billions in 2012.
But if you look into detail, things are a little more complicated.
This money, according to the Copenhagen Treaty, should be "new and additional" to ensure that an additional deployment has been brought in without being detrimental to the detriment of public development aid.
Unfortunately, there's no consensus on what "new and extra" means.
While the European Commission had proposed harmonisation, certain member states, including France, have blocked the procedure.
Today, everyone chooses the indicative value they consider best.
For example, the EU Commission has chosen itself as a guideline value for that of 2009: international financing is maintained, and adds 150 million over three years as part of the "fast-start" financing.
Other countries have chosen similar indicative values, either in terms of year or in terms of conditions at official development aid
In return, France was of the opinion that the funding of "all new projects" of 2010 was "new and additional."
In other words, there are no indicatives on the expenditure of the previous year or at the global level on official development assistance.
In concrete terms, the total of French support for developing countries can remain stable (or also decrease) - France pretends to comply with its obligation regarding the "new and additional" financing.
Another crucial element is the breakdown into donations and loans.
There's no rule about that yet, and everyone chooses their computation mode.
Some, such as France, are €1 borrowed € borrowed as equivalent with €1 donated.
Obviously, this is difficult to be justifiable: the effort is not comparable.
If the use of loans for certain profitable investments (often projects linked to issuance containment based on most effective technology) can be justified, it is deceptive to present the euro, which is collected as the equivalent for a donated euro... In addition, only the cost of the loan for the investor country should be credited as a commitment (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France has nothing else in sentence than the following consideration: it "respects" its commitment of 420 million euros by accepting 91.5% loans (389.8 million euros) and donating 8.5%) 36.1 million euros) - see also the EU document to convince itself.
France is the EU's only country that uses loans to a large extent.
Germany calls for 46%, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia (among others) carry out their commitments 100% with donations.
In other words, if the vast majority of the European Union spends €1 to fulfill its obligations, France gives just 10 cents and understands a comparable stake.
Beautiful hocuspokus!
All in all, France is operating with a double hoax in its fast-start financing: in part with a further development of the action over time and partly with the reality of the financial commitment.
France manages to believed that it spent 420 million "new and additional" millions in accordance with its promises, saving 36.1 million euros.
The manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (since it seems that it was Bercy who carried out the arbitrage) could have a doubly negative effect; once EU-internally (why are the other member states proceeding with the donations, if it is enough to borrow?) and then in the negotiations, since the developing countries are not completely deceiving...
The chapter of premature financing only a small question for a relatively short time compared to the totality of discussions about the climate.
But it could be the drop too much in UNFCCC's immense machinery.
Let's hope this won't be the case.
Anyway, it's essential to learn his lesson from this first year of early funding.
First and foremost, France could announce a change in its type of accounting for the years 2011 and 2012, in the spirit of respect for its commitments and balancing justice with the other countries of the European Union.
Above all, France could bring forward the discussions on the subject of long-term financing (another, crucial and currently discussed topic): one does not escape the question of "computability" or the type of accounting of its loans.
France could change its position in order to be just as progressive in terms of contentious issues as it is with other things.
The hope is therefore based on the new Environment Minister Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
A surprising announcement about France's fast launch and its vision of "computability," as well as the use of loans, would inherently raise the international base of trust that is necessary for further discussions.
Alarm › Pesticides taken by children
A child of 10 years occupies 81 different chemical substances over the day, according to a study published this Wednesday by the Générations Futures Association (former movement for the protection of rights and respect of future generations).
The association, which explains that its results "in view of the small number of samples analysed do not represent a statistically significant value, but represent an illustrative character of the treated problem," had bought the food usually consumed within four, from an approximately ten-year-old child with meals plus an intermediate meal in the supermarkets in Oise and Paris.
These typical meals - including the official recommendations of five fresh fruit and fruit parts, three dairy products and 1.5 liters of water per day - as well as snacks from laboratories in search of chemical substances, pesticides, heavy metals and other pollutants were investigated.
The plate balance is devastating: 81 chemical substances, including 42 classified as possibly or probably carcinogenic and five classified as safely carciogenic, as well as 37 substances with endocrinine disturbing factor, according to studies by several institutions such as the International Cancer Research Center.
"Even if, in the approximate totality of cases, the legal limits for each chemical, individually taken substance are not exceeded, this survey is removed very well from the fact that the reality in which the consumer is exposed to potentially carcinogenic substances or substances with endocrinine-disturbance factor is worrying," the association observes.
Already at breakfast, butter and tea with milk alone already contain more than ten of potentially carcinogenic residues and threen safely proven to be carcinogenic, as well as more than twenty residues that can affect the hormonal system.
The apple imported from Brazil, envisaged as an intermediary meal for the study, has traces of six chemical substances, including a fungicide banned in France.
The same applies to green beans from Kenya, included in lunch, where the study found traces of an insecticide strictly banned in the EU.
In addition, the chopping steak, the tuna from the box, even the baguette and the chewing gum of pesticides and other chemical substances were also peppered.
In the tap water, the analyses have brought the presence of nitrates and chloroform to light.
At dinner, the salmon steak was "richest" with 34 determined chemical residues, and even the plastic plate used to warm up the meal in the microwave was not unencumbered.
The ultimate risk of this "pollutant cocktail" for the consumer "is likely undervalued," according to the study.
The Alliance Europe Ecologie-Les Verts (EE-LV), for its part, has proposed "given the urgency of the situation" to introduce a moratorium on pesticide use in order to stop the "increase in civilization diseases."
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) referred to the presence of residuences of 365 different pesticides identified in fruit and vegetables that are consumed in Europe, including 76 in cereals.
